/*
 * OCR A A-Level Mathematics (H240) — Exam Practice Question Bank
 * Shape: { topicId: { name, questions: [{ q, marks, command, markScheme, diagram? }] } }
 * Topic ids unchanged so topics-config.js still matches.
 *
 * WHY THIS SHAPE. Every item is written the way the board writes it: whole
 * questions with lettered parts and part marks in square brackets where the
 * papers use them ('Hence', 'Show that', 'You must show detailed reasoning'),
 * modelling contexts with 'state a limitation' parts in pure topics, and
 * statistics and mechanics always set in context with hypothesis tests written
 * in full. Multiple-choice items are not in this bank.
 *
 * TARIFFS. Items run from 2 to 10 marks, most 3–6. Mix per topic: 10% 2, 18% 3,
 * 20% 4, 18% 5, 14% 6, 10% 7, 6% 8, 2% 9, 2% 10.
 *
 * MARK SCHEMES. OCR layout: one line per mark with M1 / A1 / B1 / E1 codes, FT,
 * BC, soi, cao, AG and DR where OCR uses them; codes sum to the tariff.
 *
 * Data marked "written for this practice question" is invented but realistic and
 * every calculation has been checked.
 * Drawing canvas flag (diagram: true) enables the sketch area in practice.html.
 */

const MATHS_OCR_ALEVEL_PRACTICE = {
  '1.1': {
    name: 'Proof',
    questions: [
      { q: `Ali claims that \\(n^2 + n + 41\\) is prime for every positive integer \\(n\\).\n\nShow that Ali's claim is false.`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `B1: Chooses a suitable counter-example, e.g. \\(n = 40\\) (or \\(n = 41\\))\nB1: \\(40^2 + 40 + 41 = 1681 = 41^2\\), which is not prime, so the claim is false\nGuidance: any correct counter-example with the value shown to be composite. A statement that "it fails for large n" with no value scores B0 B0.` },
      { q: `Priya states that, for all real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), if \\(a \\gt b\\) then \\(a^2 \\gt b^2\\).\n\nProve that Priya's statement is false.`, marks: 2, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: A counter-example with \\(a \\gt b\\), e.g. \\(a = 1,\\ b = -2\\)\nB1: Shows \\(a^2 = 1\\) and \\(b^2 = 4\\), so \\(a^2 \\lt b^2\\); hence the statement is false\nGuidance: the counter-example must have \\(b\\) negative with \\(|b| \\geq |a|\\). Both values must be evaluated.` },
      { q: `A student claims that if \\(p\\) is a prime number, then \\(2^p - 1\\) is also prime.\n\nUse a counter-example to show that the student is wrong.`, marks: 2, command: 'Use', markScheme: `B1: Uses \\(p = 11\\), giving \\(2^{11} - 1 = 2047\\)\nB1: \\(2047 = 23 \\times 89\\), so it is not prime and the claim is false\nGuidance: the factorisation (or a correct factor) must be shown; "2047 is not prime" alone is B1 B0.` },
      { q: `Prove that \\(x^2 - 6x + 10 \\gt 0\\) for all real values of \\(x\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Completes the square: \\(x^2 - 6x + 10 = (x - 3)^2 + 1\\)\nA1: \\((x-3)^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real \\(x\\), so \\((x - 3)^2 + 1 \\geq 1 \\gt 0\\) AG\nGuidance: alternative M1 for discriminant \\(36 - 40 = -4 \\lt 0\\) with A1 for "no real roots and positive \\(x^2\\) coefficient, so the curve lies above the x-axis".` },
      { q: `Prove that the difference between the squares of any two consecutive odd numbers is a multiple of 8.`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Lets the odd numbers be \\(2n - 1\\) and \\(2n + 1\\) (or \\(2n+1\\), \\(2n+3\\)) for integer \\(n\\)\nA1: \\((2n + 1)^2 - (2n - 1)^2 = 4n^2 + 4n + 1 - (4n^2 - 4n + 1) = 8n\\)\nE1: \\(n\\) is an integer, so \\(8n\\) is a multiple of 8; conclusion stated\nGuidance: checking numerical examples only scores 0.` },
      { q: `Prove by exhaustion that \\(n^2 + n + 11\\) is prime for every positive integer \\(n\\) with \\(n \\leq 6\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Substitutes each of \\(n = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\)\nA1: Obtains 13, 17, 23, 31, 41, 53 (all correct)\nE1: States that each value is prime (e.g. none has a prime factor up to its square root), so the result holds for all six cases\nGuidance: all six cases must be shown for the E1.` },
      { q: `The positive real numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are such that \\(a \\neq b\\).\n\nProve that \\(\\frac{a + b}{2} \\gt \\sqrt{ab}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Considers \\((\\sqrt{a} - \\sqrt{b})^2\\), noting it is positive since \\(a \\neq b\\)\nA1: Expands to \\(a - 2\\sqrt{ab} + b \\gt 0\\)\nA1: Rearranges to \\(\\frac{a + b}{2} \\gt \\sqrt{ab}\\) AG\nGuidance: working backwards from the result is acceptable only if each step is reversible and the argument is clearly stated in the correct direction.` },
      { q: `Prove by contradiction that there is no largest even number.`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes there is a largest even number \\(N\\)\nM1: Considers \\(N + 2\\), which is even since \\(N + 2 = 2(k + 1)\\) where \\(N = 2k\\)\nE1: \\(N + 2 \\gt N\\), contradicting the assumption; so there is no largest even number\nGuidance: the assumption must be clearly stated for B1; the conclusion must refer to the contradiction.` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nProve that \\((\\sin \\theta + \\cos \\theta)^2 \\equiv 1 + \\sin 2\\theta\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Expands LHS: \\(\\sin^2 \\theta + 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta\\)\nB1: Uses \\(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta \\equiv 1\\)\nB1: Uses \\(2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta \\equiv \\sin 2\\theta\\) to reach \\(1 + \\sin 2\\theta\\) AG\nGuidance: DR — every step must be shown; substituting values of \\(\\theta\\) scores 0.` },
      { q: `Sam solves the equation \\(\\sqrt{x + 6} = x\\) as follows.\n\n\\(x + 6 = x^2\\)\n\\(x^2 - x - 6 = 0\\)\n\\((x - 3)(x + 2) = 0\\)\nSo the solutions are \\(x = 3\\) and \\(x = -2\\).\n\nExplain the error in Sam's reasoning and state the correct solution set.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `B1: Checks \\(x = -2\\): \\(\\sqrt{4} = 2 \\neq -2\\), so \\(x = -2\\) does not satisfy the original equation\nE1: Squaring is not reversible: \\(\\sqrt{x+6} = x \\Rightarrow x + 6 = x^2\\) but not conversely (\\(\\Leftarrow\\) fails), so an extra root was introduced\nB1: The only solution is \\(x = 3\\) cao` },
      { q: `Kim says that there is a positive integer \\(n\\) for which \\(n^3 + n\\) is odd.\n\nBy considering the cases \\(n\\) even and \\(n\\) odd, prove that Kim is wrong.`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Factorises \\(n^3 + n = n(n^2 + 1)\\), or writes \\(n = 2k\\) and \\(n = 2k + 1\\)\nA1: Even case: \\(n\\) even ⇒ product even (e.g. \\(8k^3 + 2k = 2(4k^3 + k)\\)); odd case: \\(n^2 + 1\\) even ⇒ product even (e.g. \\((2k+1)^3 + (2k+1) = 2(4k^3 + 6k^2 + 4k + 1)\\))\nE1: Both cases give an even result, so \\(n^3 + n\\) is always even and Kim is wrong\nGuidance: both cases required for A1.` },
      { q: `\\(n\\) is an integer.\n\nProve by contradiction that if \\(n^2\\) is even, then \\(n\\) is even.`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes \\(n^2\\) is even and \\(n\\) is odd\nM1: Writes \\(n = 2k + 1\\) for some integer \\(k\\) and squares\nA1: \\(n^2 = 4k^2 + 4k + 1 = 2(2k^2 + 2k) + 1\\), which is odd\nE1: This contradicts \\(n^2\\) being even, so \\(n\\) must be even\nGuidance: E1 requires a clear statement of the contradiction and conclusion.` },
      { q: `Prove that \\(n^3 - n\\) is divisible by 6 for every integer \\(n\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Factorises: \\(n^3 - n = (n - 1)n(n + 1)\\)\nA1: Identifies this as the product of three consecutive integers\nE1: Among any two consecutive integers one is even, so the product is divisible by 2; among any three consecutive integers one is a multiple of 3, so it is divisible by 3\nE1: Since 2 and 3 are coprime (both factors present), the product is divisible by 6\nGuidance: examples alone score 0.` },
      { q: `Prove by contradiction that the sum of a rational number and an irrational number is irrational.`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes \\(a\\) rational, \\(b\\) irrational and \\(a + b\\) rational\nM1: Writes \\(a = \\frac{p}{q}\\), \\(a + b = \\frac{r}{s}\\) with \\(p, q, r, s\\) integers, \\(q, s \\neq 0\\)\nA1: \\(b = \\frac{r}{s} - \\frac{p}{q} = \\frac{rq - ps}{sq}\\), a ratio of integers with non-zero denominator, so \\(b\\) is rational\nE1: This contradicts \\(b\\) being irrational, so \\(a + b\\) is irrational` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nProve that \\(\\frac{1 - \\cos 2\\theta}{\\sin 2\\theta} \\equiv \\tan \\theta\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Uses \\(\\cos 2\\theta \\equiv 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta\\) in the numerator\nA1: Numerator \\(= 2\\sin^2\\theta\\)\nM1: Uses \\(\\sin 2\\theta \\equiv 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{2\\sin^2\\theta}{2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = \\tan\\theta\\) AG\nGuidance: DR — must start from one side and reach the other with every step shown.` },
      { q: `A small company models its annual profit \\(P\\) thousand pounds, when it sells \\(x\\) hundred items, by\n\\(P = 12x - 2x^2 - 10\\).\n\n(a) Prove that, according to the model, the annual profit can never exceed £8000. [3]\n(b) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Attempts to complete the square: \\(P = -2(x^2 - 6x) - 10\\)\nA1: \\(P = 8 - 2(x - 3)^2\\)\nE1: \\(2(x-3)^2 \\geq 0\\) so \\(P \\leq 8\\), i.e. profit never exceeds £8000 AG\n(b)\nB1: e.g. \\(x\\) must be a whole number of items / the model gives very large losses for large \\(x\\) / prices and costs may change over time\nGuidance: in (a) differentiation to find the maximum with a justification that it is a maximum is also acceptable.` },
      { q: `Prove that the square of any integer can be written in the form \\(3k\\) or \\(3k + 1\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer.`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: States that every integer is of the form \\(3m\\), \\(3m + 1\\) or \\(3m + 2\\) (or \\(3m - 1\\))\nM1: Squares at least two cases correctly\nA1: \\((3m)^2 = 3(3m^2)\\); \\((3m + 1)^2 = 3(3m^2 + 2m) + 1\\); \\((3m + 2)^2 = 3(3m^2 + 4m + 1) + 1\\)\nE1: All cases give \\(3k\\) or \\(3k + 1\\), so the result is proved` },
      { q: `Jo writes: "For all real numbers \\(x\\), if \\(x^2 \\gt 9\\) then \\(x \\gt 3\\)."\n\n(a) Show that Jo's statement is false. [2]\n(b) Write down the converse of Jo's statement and state, with a reason, whether it is true. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: Counter-example, e.g. \\(x = -4\\)\nB1: \\((-4)^2 = 16 \\gt 9\\) but \\(-4 \\lt 3\\), so the statement is false\n(b)\nB1: Converse: if \\(x \\gt 3\\) then \\(x^2 \\gt 9\\)\nE1: True, since if \\(x \\gt 3\\) then \\(x\\) is positive and \\(x^2 \\gt 3x \\gt 9\\) (or equivalent reasoning)` },
      { q: `An arithmetic series has first term \\(a\\) and common difference \\(d\\).\n\nProve that the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of the series is \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\left[2a + (n - 1)d\\right]\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: \\(S_n = a + (a + d) + \\dots + (a + (n - 1)d)\\)\nM1: Writes the series in reverse: \\(S_n = (a + (n-1)d) + (a + (n-2)d) + \\dots + a\\)\nA1: Adds term by term: each of the \\(n\\) pairs sums to \\(2a + (n - 1)d\\), so \\(2S_n = n[2a + (n-1)d]\\)\nA1: \\(S_n = \\frac{n}{2}[2a + (n - 1)d]\\) AG` },
      { q: `Prove by contradiction that \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational.`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes \\(\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{p}{q}\\) where \\(p, q\\) are integers with no common factor, \\(q \\neq 0\\)\nM1: \\(p^2 = 2q^2\\), so \\(p^2\\) is even, hence \\(p\\) is even\nM1: Writes \\(p = 2k\\): \\(4k^2 = 2q^2\\), so \\(q^2 = 2k^2\\)\nA1: \\(q^2\\) even, so \\(q\\) is even\nE1: \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) share the factor 2, contradicting the assumption, so \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) is irrational\nGuidance: the "no common factor" condition must be stated for B1 and used for E1.` },
      { q: `Prove by contradiction that there are infinitely many prime numbers.`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes there are finitely many primes \\(p_1, p_2, \\dots, p_n\\)\nM1: Considers \\(N = p_1 p_2 \\cdots p_n + 1\\)\nA1: Dividing \\(N\\) by any \\(p_i\\) leaves remainder 1, so no \\(p_i\\) is a factor of \\(N\\)\nE1: So either \\(N\\) is prime or it has a prime factor not in the list\nE1: Either way there is a prime not in the list — contradiction; so there are infinitely many primes\nGuidance: claiming "N is prime" without the alternative loses the first E1.` },
      { q: `Prove by contradiction that \\(\\log_2 3\\) is irrational.`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes \\(\\log_2 3 = \\frac{p}{q}\\) with \\(p, q\\) integers, \\(q \\neq 0\\)\nB1: Notes \\(\\log_2 3 \\gt 0\\) so \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) may be taken as positive integers\nM1: Converts to \\(2^{p/q} = 3\\), so \\(2^p = 3^q\\)\nA1: \\(2^p\\) is even (as \\(p \\geq 1\\)) and \\(3^q\\) is odd\nE1: An even number cannot equal an odd number — contradiction; so \\(\\log_2 3\\) is irrational` },
      { q: `In a card game, a player's score is calculated as \\(a^2 - 4b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers.\n\n(a) Prove that the square of any integer leaves a remainder of 0 or 1 when divided by 4. [3]\n(b) Hence prove that a score of 2 is impossible. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Considers \\(a\\) even, \\(a = 2k\\), and \\(a\\) odd, \\(a = 2k + 1\\)\nA1: \\((2k)^2 = 4k^2\\), remainder 0\nA1: \\((2k+1)^2 = 4(k^2 + k) + 1\\), remainder 1\n(b)\nM1: \\(a^2 - 4b = 2 \\Rightarrow a^2 = 4b + 2\\), which leaves remainder 2 on division by 4\nE1: This contradicts (a), so a score of 2 is impossible` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nProve that \\(\\sin 3\\theta \\equiv 3\\sin\\theta - 4\\sin^3\\theta\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `M1: Writes \\(\\sin 3\\theta = \\sin(2\\theta + \\theta) = \\sin 2\\theta\\cos\\theta + \\cos 2\\theta\\sin\\theta\\)\nM1: Uses \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta\\)\nA1: \\(2\\sin\\theta\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin\\theta - 2\\sin^3\\theta\\)\nM1: Uses \\(\\cos^2\\theta = 1 - \\sin^2\\theta\\)\nA1: \\(2\\sin\\theta - 2\\sin^3\\theta + \\sin\\theta - 2\\sin^3\\theta = 3\\sin\\theta - 4\\sin^3\\theta\\) AG\nGuidance: DR — all steps required.` },
      { q: `The concentration \\(C\\) mg per litre of a drug in a patient's blood, \\(t\\) hours after it is given, is modelled by\n\\(C = \\frac{6t}{t^2 + 9}\\), \\(t \\geq 0\\).\n\n(a) Prove that, according to the model, \\(C \\leq 1\\) for all \\(t \\geq 0\\). [3]\n(b) Find the value of \\(t\\) for which \\(C = 1\\). [1]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Considers \\(1 - C = \\frac{t^2 + 9 - 6t}{t^2 + 9}\\)\nA1: \\(= \\frac{(t - 3)^2}{t^2 + 9}\\)\nE1: Numerator \\(\\geq 0\\) and denominator \\(\\gt 0\\), so \\(1 - C \\geq 0\\), i.e. \\(C \\leq 1\\) AG\n(b)\nB1: \\(t = 3\\) cao\n(c)\nB1: e.g. the model predicts some drug is always present (\\(C \\gt 0\\) for all \\(t \\gt 0\\)) / it does not allow for repeat doses / patients differ` },
      { q: `(a) Prove that \\(x + \\frac{1}{x} \\geq 2\\) for all positive real numbers \\(x\\). [3]\n(b) Hence prove that \\(\\tan\\theta + \\frac{1}{\\tan\\theta} \\geq 2\\) for \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt 90°\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Considers \\((x - 1)^2 \\geq 0\\)\nA1: \\(x^2 + 1 \\geq 2x\\)\nE1: Divides by \\(x\\), valid since \\(x \\gt 0\\), giving \\(x + \\frac{1}{x} \\geq 2\\) AG\n(b)\nB1: \\(\\tan\\theta \\gt 0\\) for \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt 90°\\)\nE1: So substituting \\(x = \\tan\\theta\\) in (a) gives the result\nGuidance: in (a) the sign of \\(x\\) must be referred to when dividing.` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\((n + 1)^3 - n^3 = 3n^2 + 3n + 1\\). [2]\n(b) Hence prove that the difference between the cubes of any two consecutive integers is odd. [2]\n(c) Prove that the difference between the cubes of any two consecutive integers is never a multiple of 3. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Expands \\((n + 1)^3 = n^3 + 3n^2 + 3n + 1\\)\nA1: Subtracts \\(n^3\\) to obtain \\(3n^2 + 3n + 1\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: Writes \\(3n^2 + 3n = 3n(n + 1)\\)\nE1: \\(n(n+1)\\) is even (consecutive integers), so \\(3n(n+1)\\) is even and \\(3n^2 + 3n + 1\\) is odd\n(c)\nM1: Writes \\(3n^2 + 3n + 1 = 3(n^2 + n) + 1\\)\nE1: This leaves remainder 1 on division by 3, so it is never a multiple of 3` },
      { q: `A puzzle asks for two integers \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) with \\(x^2 - y^2 = 10\\).\n\nProve that the puzzle has no solution.`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: Assumes integers \\(x, y\\) exist with \\(x^2 - y^2 = 10\\)\nM1: Factorises: \\((x - y)(x + y) = 10\\)\nM1: Notes \\((x + y) - (x - y) = 2y\\), which is even\nA1: So \\(x - y\\) and \\(x + y\\) are both even or both odd\nE1: If both odd the product is odd; if both even the product is a multiple of 4\nE1: 10 is even but not a multiple of 4 — contradiction; so there is no solution\nGuidance: listing positive factor pairs (1, 10), (2, 5) only, without negatives or the parity argument, scores at most B1 M1 A1.` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\n(a) Prove that \\(\\cos^4\\theta - \\sin^4\\theta \\equiv \\cos 2\\theta\\). [3]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(\\cos^4\\theta - \\sin^4\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\) for \\(0° \\leq \\theta \\leq 180°\\). [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Factorises as a difference of two squares: \\((\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta)(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta)\\)\nB1: Uses \\(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\equiv 1\\)\nA1: Uses \\(\\cos^2\\theta - \\sin^2\\theta \\equiv \\cos 2\\theta\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\cos 2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\) with \\(0° \\leq 2\\theta \\leq 360°\\)\nA1: \\(2\\theta = 60°, 300°\\)\nA1: \\(\\theta = 30°, 150°\\) and no others in range\nGuidance: DR — answers from a graph or calculator with no working score 0 in (b).` },
      { q: `The cross-section of a bridge arch over a river is modelled by\n\\(h = 0.08x(20 - x)\\), \\(0 \\leq x \\leq 20\\),\nwhere \\(h\\) metres is the height of the arch above the water at a horizontal distance \\(x\\) metres from one bank.\n\n(a) Prove that, according to the model, the arch is never more than 8 m above the water. [3]\n(b) Find the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the arch is at least 6 m above the water. [2]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Writes \\(h = 0.08(20x - x^2)\\) and attempts to complete the square\nA1: \\(h = 0.08\\left[100 - (x - 10)^2\\right] = 8 - 0.08(x - 10)^2\\)\nE1: \\((x - 10)^2 \\geq 0\\) so \\(h \\leq 8\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(0.08x(20 - x) \\geq 6 \\Rightarrow x^2 - 20x + 75 \\leq 0\\), i.e. \\((x - 5)(x - 15) \\leq 0\\)\nA1: \\(5 \\leq x \\leq 15\\)\n(c)\nB1: e.g. the water level varies / a real arch has thickness / the shape may not be exactly a parabola` },
      { q: `By considering the cases \\(n = 5k,\\ 5k + 1,\\ 5k + 2,\\ 5k + 3\\) and \\(5k + 4\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer, prove that \\(n^2 + n + 1\\) is never divisible by 5 for any integer \\(n\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: States that every integer takes one of the five forms\nM1: Substitutes and expands at least one case correctly\nA1: \\(n = 5k\\): \\(25k^2 + 5k + 1\\), remainder 1; \\(n = 5k + 1\\): \\(25k^2 + 15k + 3\\), remainder 3\nA1: \\(n = 5k + 2\\): \\(25k^2 + 25k + 7 = 5(5k^2 + 5k + 1) + 2\\), remainder 2\nA1: \\(n = 5k + 3\\): \\(25k^2 + 35k + 13\\), remainder 3; \\(n = 5k + 4\\): \\(25k^2 + 45k + 21\\), remainder 1\nE1: No case gives remainder 0, so \\(n^2 + n + 1\\) is never divisible by 5` },
      { q: `(a) Factorise \\(n^5 - n\\) completely. [1]\n(b) Hence explain why \\(n^5 - n\\) is divisible by 6 for every integer \\(n\\). [2]\n(c) By considering the possible remainders when \\(n\\) is divided by 5, prove that \\(n^5 - n\\) is divisible by 30 for every integer \\(n\\). [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(n(n - 1)(n + 1)(n^2 + 1)\\)\n(b)\nE1: \\((n - 1)n(n + 1)\\) are three consecutive integers, so one is even and one is a multiple of 3\nE1: Hence the product is divisible by \\(2 \\times 3 = 6\\)\n(c)\nM1: If \\(n = 5k\\), \\(5k \\pm 1\\), a factor \\(n\\), \\(n - 1\\) or \\(n + 1\\) is a multiple of 5\nA1: If \\(n = 5k \\pm 2\\), \\(n^2 + 1 = 25k^2 \\pm 20k + 5 = 5(5k^2 \\pm 4k + 1)\\)\nE1: So 5 always divides \\(n^5 - n\\); with (b), and 2, 3, 5 distinct primes, it is divisible by 30` },
      { q: `\\(n\\) is an integer.\n\n(a) Prove that if \\(n^2\\) is a multiple of 3, then \\(n\\) is a multiple of 3. [3]\n(b) Hence prove by contradiction that \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) is irrational. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: Considers (contrapositive) \\(n = 3k + 1\\) and \\(n = 3k + 2\\)\nM1: \\((3k + 1)^2 = 3(3k^2 + 2k) + 1\\); \\((3k + 2)^2 = 3(3k^2 + 4k + 1) + 1\\)\nE1: Neither square is a multiple of 3, so if \\(n^2\\) is a multiple of 3 then \\(n\\) is a multiple of 3\n(b)\nB1: Assumes \\(\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{p}{q}\\), \\(p, q\\) integers with no common factor\nM1: \\(p^2 = 3q^2\\) so \\(p^2\\), and hence \\(p\\), is a multiple of 3 (by (a))\nA1: \\(p = 3m\\): \\(9m^2 = 3q^2 \\Rightarrow q^2 = 3m^2\\), so \\(q\\) is a multiple of 3\nE1: \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) share factor 3 — contradiction; \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) is irrational` },
      { q: `The population \\(P\\) of a species of bird on an island, \\(t\\) years after it was first recorded, is modelled by\n\\(P = \\frac{500 \\times 3^t}{3^t + 4}\\), \\(t \\geq 0\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(P = 500 - \\frac{2000}{3^t + 4}\\). [2]\n(b) Hence prove that, according to the model, the population is always less than 500. [2]\n(c) Without using differentiation, prove that the model predicts the population is always increasing. [2]\n(d) Comment on the suitability of the model for predicting the population in the long term. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Writes \\(500 \\times 3^t = 500(3^t + 4) - 2000\\)\nA1: Divides by \\(3^t + 4\\) to obtain \\(500 - \\frac{2000}{3^t + 4}\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(3^t + 4 \\gt 0\\), so \\(\\frac{2000}{3^t + 4} \\gt 0\\)\nA1: Hence \\(P \\lt 500\\) for all \\(t\\)\n(c)\nM1: As \\(t\\) increases, \\(3^t\\) increases, so \\(3^t + 4\\) increases\nE1: So \\(\\frac{2000}{3^t + 4}\\) decreases and \\(P\\) increases\n(d)\nE1: e.g. a limiting population of 500 is reasonable as food/space is limited, but the model ignores events such as disease or predators and gives non-integer values` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\n(a) Prove that \\(\\frac{\\sin 2\\theta}{1 + \\cos 2\\theta} \\equiv \\tan\\theta\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the exact value of \\(\\tan 15°\\), giving your answer in the form \\(a + b\\sqrt{3}\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers. [3]\n(c) Explain why the identity in (a) is not valid when \\(\\theta = 90°\\). [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Uses \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1\\)\nA1: Denominator \\(= 2\\cos^2\\theta\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}{2\\cos^2\\theta} = \\tan\\theta\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\tan 15° = \\frac{\\sin 30°}{1 + \\cos 30°} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}\\)\nM1: Rationalises: multiplies by \\(\\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{2 - \\sqrt{3}}\\)\nA1: \\(2 - \\sqrt{3}\\) cao\n(c)\nE1: \\(1 + \\cos 180° = 0\\), so the LHS is undefined (and \\(\\tan 90°\\) is undefined)` },
      { q: `(a) Prove that if \\(n\\) is an odd integer, then \\(n^2 - 1\\) is divisible by 8. [3]\n(b) Show that the statement "\\(n^2 - 1\\) is divisible by 8 for every integer \\(n\\)" is false. [1]\n(c) Prove that if \\(p\\) is a prime number with \\(p \\geq 5\\), then \\(p^2 - 1\\) is divisible by 24. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(n = 2k + 1\\): \\(n^2 - 1 = 4k^2 + 4k = 4k(k + 1)\\)\nA1: \\(k(k + 1)\\) is even, being a product of consecutive integers\nE1: So \\(n^2 - 1 = 4 \\times\\) even \\(= 8m\\), divisible by 8\n(b)\nB1: e.g. \\(n = 2\\): \\(n^2 - 1 = 3\\), not divisible by 8\n(c)\nB1: \\(p\\) is odd so, by (a), 8 divides \\(p^2 - 1\\)\nM1: \\(p\\) is not a multiple of 3, so \\(p = 3k \\pm 1\\) and \\(p^2 - 1 = 9k^2 \\pm 6k = 3(3k^2 \\pm 2k)\\)\nE1: 8 and 3 are coprime, so \\(p^2 - 1\\) is divisible by 24` },
      { q: `(a) Prove by contradiction that \\(\\sqrt{6}\\) is irrational. [5]\n(b) Hence prove by contradiction that \\(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3}\\) is irrational. [3]`, marks: 8, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: Assumes \\(\\sqrt{6} = \\frac{p}{q}\\), \\(p, q\\) integers with no common factor, \\(q \\neq 0\\)\nM1: \\(p^2 = 6q^2\\), so \\(p^2\\) is even and hence \\(p\\) is even\nM1: \\(p = 2k\\): \\(4k^2 = 6q^2 \\Rightarrow 2k^2 = 3q^2\\)\nA1: \\(3q^2\\) is even so \\(q^2\\), and hence \\(q\\), is even\nE1: \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are both even — contradiction; \\(\\sqrt{6}\\) is irrational\n(b)\nB1: Assumes \\(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3} = r\\), \\(r\\) rational\nM1: Squares: \\(r^2 = 5 + 2\\sqrt{6}\\), so \\(\\sqrt{6} = \\frac{r^2 - 5}{2}\\)\nE1: The RHS is rational, contradicting (a); so \\(\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3}\\) is irrational` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(x^3 + y^3 \\equiv (x + y)(x^2 - xy + y^2)\\). [2]\n(b) Prove that \\(x^2 - xy + y^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), with equality only when \\(x = y = 0\\). [4]\n(c) Hence prove that, for all real \\(x\\) and \\(y\\), \\(x^3 + y^3\\) and \\(x + y\\) are either both positive, both negative or both zero. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Expands RHS: \\(x^3 - x^2y + xy^2 + x^2y - xy^2 + y^3\\)\nA1: Simplifies to \\(x^3 + y^3\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: Completes the square in \\(x\\): \\(\\left(x - \\frac{y}{2}\\right)^2 + \\frac{3}{4}y^2\\)\nA1: Correct expression\nE1: Both terms \\(\\geq 0\\), so the expression is \\(\\geq 0\\)\nE1: Equality requires \\(y = 0\\) and \\(x - \\frac{y}{2} = 0\\), i.e. \\(x = y = 0\\)\n(c)\nM1: If \\(x + y \\neq 0\\) then \\(x, y\\) not both 0, so \\(x^2 - xy + y^2 \\gt 0\\) by (b)\nE1: So \\(x^3 + y^3\\) has the same sign as \\(x + y\\); if \\(x + y = 0\\) then \\(x^3 + y^3 = 0\\)` },
      { q: `\\(f(x) = x^3 + x + 1\\)\n\n(a) Show that the equation \\(f(x) = 0\\) has a root between \\(-1\\) and 0. [2]\n(b) Prove by contradiction that this root is irrational. [6]\n(c) Explain why \\(f(x) = 0\\) has exactly one real root. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(f(-1) = -1\\), \\(f(0) = 1\\)\nA1: Sign change and \\(f\\) is continuous, so there is a root between \\(-1\\) and 0\n(b)\nB1: Assumes the root is \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), \\(p, q\\) integers, \\(q \\neq 0\\), with no common factor\nM1: Substitutes and multiplies by \\(q^3\\): \\(p^3 + pq^2 + q^3 = 0\\)\nB1: \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) cannot both be even, so considers: both odd; \\(p\\) even, \\(q\\) odd; \\(p\\) odd, \\(q\\) even\nA1: Both odd: odd + odd + odd = odd\nA1: \\(p\\) even, \\(q\\) odd: even + even + odd = odd; \\(p\\) odd, \\(q\\) even: odd + even + even = odd\nE1: In every case the LHS is odd so cannot equal 0 — contradiction; the root is irrational\n(c)\nE1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 + 1 \\gt 0\\) for all \\(x\\), so \\(f\\) is increasing and crosses the x-axis only once` },
      { q: `Ann is investigating which integers can be written as the sum of two square numbers, \\(a^2 + b^2\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers.\n\n(a) Prove that, for any integer \\(n\\), \\(n^2\\) leaves remainder 0 or 1 when divided by 4. [3]\n(b) Hence prove that no integer of the form \\(4k + 3\\), where \\(k\\) is an integer, can be written as the sum of two squares. [3]\n(c) Ann claims that every positive integer of the form \\(4k + 1\\) can be written as the sum of two squares. Show that Ann's claim is false. [2]\n(d) Prove that the sum of the squares of two odd integers is never a perfect square. [2]`, marks: 10, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: Considers \\(n = 2m\\) and \\(n = 2m + 1\\)\nA1: \\((2m)^2 = 4m^2\\), remainder 0\nA1: \\((2m + 1)^2 = 4(m^2 + m) + 1\\), remainder 1\n(b)\nM1: \\(a^2 + b^2\\) has remainder \\(0 + 0\\), \\(0 + 1\\) or \\(1 + 1\\) on division by 4\nA1: So the remainder is 0, 1 or 2 only\nE1: \\(4k + 3\\) has remainder 3, so it cannot be a sum of two squares\n(c)\nB1: Counter-example: \\(21 = 4 \\times 5 + 1\\)\nB1: \\(21 - 0, 21 - 1, 21 - 4, 21 - 9, 21 - 16\\) give 21, 20, 17, 12, 5, none of which is a square, so 21 is not a sum of two squares\n(d)\nM1: Each odd square leaves remainder 1, so the sum leaves remainder 2 on division by 4\nE1: By (a) a perfect square leaves remainder 0 or 1, so the sum is never a perfect square\nGuidance: in (c) another valid counter-example (e.g. 33, 57) with full checking earns both marks.` }
    ]
  },
  '1.2': {
    name: 'Algebra & Functions',
    questions: [
      { q: `Express \\(\\dfrac{4^{x}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\) in the form \\(2^{ax+b}\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants to be determined.`, marks: 2, command: 'Express', markScheme: `M1 Writes \\(4^{x} = 2^{2x}\\) and \\(\\sqrt{2} = 2^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\) soi\nA1 \\(2^{2x - \\frac{1}{2}}\\), so \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) cao` },
      { q: `The intensity of light, \\(I\\) units, at a distance \\(d\\) metres from a lamp is modelled as being inversely proportional to \\(d^{2}\\). When \\(d = 2\\), \\(I = 45\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(I\\) when \\(d = 3\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(I = \\dfrac{k}{d^{2}}\\) with \\(k = 45 \\times 4 = 180\\)\nA1 \\(I = \\dfrac{180}{9} = 20\\) cao` },
      { q: `A ball is thrown upwards. Its height, \\(h\\) metres, above the ground \\(t\\) seconds after it is thrown is modelled by \\(h = 1 + 4t - 2t^{2}\\).\n\nUse the discriminant of a suitable quadratic equation to determine whether, according to the model, the ball reaches a height of 4 metres.`, marks: 2, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `M1 Forms \\(2t^{2} - 4t + 3 = 0\\) and finds discriminant \\(16 - 24\\)\nA1 \\(-8 \\lt 0\\), so no real solutions: the ball does not reach 4 m (conclusion in context required)` },
      { q: `A bureau de change converts pounds to euros. The number of euros received for \\(x\\) pounds before a fee is given by \\(f(x) = 1.15x\\). A fixed fee of 3 euros is then deducted, given by \\(g(x) = x - 3\\).\n\n(a) Find an expression for \\(gf(x)\\). [1]\n(b) Find \\(gf(200)\\) and explain what this value represents in this context. [1]`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(gf(x) = 1.15x - 3\\)\n(b) B1 \\(gf(200) = 227\\): the number of euros received for 200 pounds after the fee is deducted` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nExpress \\(\\dfrac{5 + \\sqrt{3}}{2 - \\sqrt{3}}\\) in the form \\(a + b\\sqrt{3}\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Express', markScheme: `M1 Multiplies numerator and denominator by \\(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\)\nA1 Denominator \\(4 - 3 = 1\\) and numerator \\(10 + 5\\sqrt{3} + 2\\sqrt{3} + 3\\)\nA1 \\(13 + 7\\sqrt{3}\\) cao\nDR: answer from calculator only scores 0` },
      { q: `A model rocket is launched vertically. Its height, \\(h\\) metres, after \\(t\\) seconds is modelled by \\(h = 30t - 5t^{2}\\).\n\nFind the set of values of \\(t\\) for which the rocket is at least 25 metres above the ground.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Forms \\(30t - 5t^{2} \\geq 25\\) and rearranges to \\(t^{2} - 6t + 5 \\leq 0\\) soi\nA1 Critical values \\(t = 1\\) and \\(t = 5\\)\nA1 \\(1 \\leq t \\leq 5\\) (or \\(\\{t : 1 \\leq t \\leq 5\\}\\)); strict inequalities A0` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(|3x - 1| = |x + 5|\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Squares both sides or considers \\(3x - 1 = \\pm(x + 5)\\)\nA1 \\(x = 3\\)\nA1 \\(x = -1\\)\nBoth answers with no working: award 1 mark only (special case)` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(x^{\\frac{2}{3}} - 5x^{\\frac{1}{3}} + 4 = 0\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Uses substitution \\(u = x^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\) to obtain \\(u^{2} - 5u + 4 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(u = 1\\) or \\(u = 4\\)\nA1 \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = 64\\) cao` },
      { q: `Find the remainder when \\(x^{4} - 3x^{3} + 2x - 7\\) is divided by \\((2x - 1)\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses remainder theorem with \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) (long division also acceptable)\nA1 \\(\\frac{1}{16} - \\frac{3}{8} + 1 - 7\\)\nA1 Remainder \\(= -\\frac{101}{16}\\) (or \\(-6.3125\\)) cao\nSubstituting \\(x = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) or \\(x = 2\\) is M0` },
      { q: `A temperature of \\(x\\) degrees Celsius is equivalent to \\(f(x)\\) degrees Fahrenheit, where \\(f(x) = 1.8x + 32\\).\n\n(a) Find an expression for \\(f^{-1}(x)\\). [2]\n(b) Explain what the value of \\(f^{-1}(77)\\) represents in this context. [1]`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Sets \\(y = 1.8x + 32\\) and rearranges to make \\(x\\) the subject\nA1 \\(f^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{x - 32}{1.8}\\) or \\(\\dfrac{5(x - 32)}{9}\\)\n(b) E1 \\(f^{-1}(77) = 25\\): a temperature of 77 °F is equivalent to 25 °C` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = x^{2} - 6x + 11\\) for \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\).\n\nBy expressing \\(f(x)\\) in the form \\((x + p)^{2} + q\\), find the range of \\(f\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\((x - 3)^{2} + q\\) with attempt at \\(q\\)\nA1 \\((x - 3)^{2} + 2\\)\nA1 ft Range \\(f(x) \\geq 2\\) (must be in terms of \\(f(x)\\) or \\(y\\), not \\(x\\))` },
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nA rectangle has area \\((8 + 3\\sqrt{2})\\) cm² and width \\((2 + \\sqrt{2})\\) cm.\n\nShow that the length of the rectangle is \\((5 - \\sqrt{2})\\) cm.`, marks: 4, command: 'Show', markScheme: `M1 Length \\(= \\dfrac{8 + 3\\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}}\\) multiplied by \\(\\dfrac{2 - \\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}}\\)\nA1 Denominator \\(4 - 2 = 2\\)\nA1 Numerator \\(16 - 8\\sqrt{2} + 6\\sqrt{2} - 6 = 10 - 2\\sqrt{2}\\)\nA1 \\(5 - \\sqrt{2}\\) AG, with all working shown` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nOn a plan of a park, the edge of a flower bed is modelled by the curve \\(x^{2} + 2y^{2} = 22\\) and a straight path is modelled by the line \\(x + y = 5\\), where units are metres.\n\nFind the coordinates of the points where the path crosses the edge of the flower bed.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes \\(y = 5 - x\\) (or \\(x = 5 - y\\)) into the curve\nA1 \\(3x^{2} - 20x + 28 = 0\\) (or \\(3y^{2} - 10y + 3 = 0\\))\nM1 Solves their 3-term quadratic: \\((3x - 14)(x - 2) = 0\\)\nA1 \\((2, 3)\\) and \\(\\left(\\frac{14}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\)` },
      { q: `Express \\(\\dfrac{3x^{2} - x + 4}{(x + 1)(x - 1)^{2}}\\) in partial fractions.`, marks: 4, command: 'Express', markScheme: `B1 Correct form \\(\\dfrac{A}{x + 1} + \\dfrac{B}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{C}{(x - 1)^{2}}\\)\nM1 \\(3x^{2} - x + 4 = A(x - 1)^{2} + B(x + 1)(x - 1) + C(x + 1)\\) and substitutes values or equates coefficients\nA1 Two of \\(A = 2\\), \\(B = 1\\), \\(C = 3\\)\nA1 \\(\\dfrac{2}{x + 1} + \\dfrac{1}{x - 1} + \\dfrac{3}{(x - 1)^{2}}\\)` },
      { q: `On a single diagram, sketch the curve \\(y = x^{2} - 4\\) and the line \\(y = x + 2\\), and indicate by shading the region for which \\(y \\geq x^{2} - 4\\) and \\(y \\leq x + 2\\). State the coordinates of the points where the line and curve meet.`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `B1 U-shaped parabola with vertex at \\((0, -4)\\) and crossing the \\(x\\)-axis at \\(\\pm 2\\)\nB1 Straight line with positive gradient through \\((-2, 0)\\) and \\((0, 2)\\)\nB1 Intersections \\((-2, 0)\\) and \\((3, 5)\\) from \\(x^{2} - x - 6 = 0\\)\nB1 Correct region (between line and curve, above curve and below line) clearly indicated; condone shading the unwanted region if key is clear` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = 2x^{3} - 3x^{2} - 8x - 3\\).\n\nShow that \\((2x + 1)\\) is a factor of \\(f(x)\\), and hence factorise \\(f(x)\\) completely.`, marks: 4, command: 'Show', markScheme: `B1 \\(f\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{3}{4} + 4 - 3 = 0\\), so \\((2x + 1)\\) is a factor\nM1 Divides by \\((2x + 1)\\) or compares coefficients to find quadratic factor\nA1 \\(x^{2} - 2x - 3\\)\nA1 \\((2x + 1)(x - 3)(x + 1)\\) cao` },
      { q: `The function f is defined by \\(f(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x - 2}\\) for \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x \\neq 2\\).\n\n(a) Find an expression for \\(f^{-1}(x)\\). [3]\n(b) State the domain of \\(f^{-1}\\). [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(y = \\dfrac{3}{x - 2}\\) and attempts to make \\(x\\) the subject\nA1 \\(x = 2 + \\dfrac{3}{y}\\) or \\(x = \\dfrac{2y + 3}{y}\\)\nA1 \\(f^{-1}(x) = 2 + \\dfrac{3}{x}\\) (in terms of \\(x\\))\n(b) B1 \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x \\neq 0\\)` },
      { q: `The curve \\(y = f(x)\\) has a maximum point at \\((2, 5)\\).\n\nState the coordinates of the maximum point on each of the following curves.\n(a) \\(y = f(x + 3)\\) [1]\n(b) \\(y = 3f(x)\\) [1]\n(c) \\(y = f(-x)\\) [1]\n(d) \\(y = 2f(x) - 1\\) [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'State', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\((-1, 5)\\)\n(b) B1 \\((2, 15)\\)\n(c) B1 \\((-2, 5)\\)\n(d) B1 \\((2, 9)\\)` },
      { q: `Find the values of the constant \\(k\\) for which the line \\(y = kx - 1\\) is a tangent to the curve \\(y = x^{2} + 3\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Equates and rearranges: \\(x^{2} + 3 = kx - 1\\)\nA1 \\(x^{2} - kx + 4 = 0\\)\nM1 Uses discriminant \\(= 0\\): \\(k^{2} - 16 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(k = 4\\) or \\(k = -4\\)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\((x + 2)\\) is a factor of \\(f(x) = 2x^{3} + ax^{2} - 7x + 6\\), where \\(a\\) is a constant.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(a\\). [2]\n(b) Hence factorise \\(f(x)\\) completely. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Sets \\(f(-2) = 0\\): \\(-16 + 4a + 14 + 6 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(a = -1\\)\n(b) M1 Divides \\(2x^{3} - x^{2} - 7x + 6\\) by \\((x + 2)\\) or compares coefficients\nA1 \\(2x^{2} - 5x + 3\\)\nA1 \\((x + 2)(2x - 3)(x - 1)\\) cao` },
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nSolve the equation \\(\\sqrt{3}(x + 2) = 4x - \\sqrt{3}\\), giving your answer in the form \\(a + b\\sqrt{3}\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are rational.`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Collects \\(x\\) terms: \\(4x - \\sqrt{3}x = 2\\sqrt{3} + \\sqrt{3}\\)\nA1 \\(x(4 - \\sqrt{3}) = 3\\sqrt{3}\\)\nM1 Multiplies \\(\\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4 - \\sqrt{3}}\\) by \\(\\dfrac{4 + \\sqrt{3}}{4 + \\sqrt{3}}\\)\nA1 \\(\\dfrac{12\\sqrt{3} + 9}{13}\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\frac{9}{13} + \\frac{12}{13}\\sqrt{3}\\) cao` },
      { q: `A small business sells candles. The weekly profit, \\(P\\) hundred pounds, when each candle is sold for \\(x\\) pounds is modelled by \\(P = -2x^{2} + 48x - 160\\).\n\n(a) Express \\(P\\) in the form \\(a - b(x - c)^{2}\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are constants. [3]\n(b) Hence state the selling price that gives the maximum weekly profit, and the value of this maximum profit. [1]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(-2(x^{2} - 24x) - 160\\) or \\(-2[(x - 12)^{2} - 144] - 160\\)\nA1 \\(c = 12\\), \\(b = 2\\)\nA1 \\(a = 128\\): \\(P = 128 - 2(x - 12)^{2}\\)\n(b) B1 ft Price £12, maximum profit £12 800 (accept 128 hundred pounds)\n(c) E1 e.g. the model gives a negative profit for prices below £4 or above £20; profit is likely to depend on other factors such as costs or season; the price can only take values in whole pence` },
      { q: `Express \\(\\dfrac{4x^{2} + 7x - 12}{x(x + 2)(x - 3)}\\) in partial fractions.`, marks: 5, command: 'Express', markScheme: `B1 Correct form \\(\\dfrac{A}{x} + \\dfrac{B}{x + 2} + \\dfrac{C}{x - 3}\\)\nM1 \\(4x^{2} + 7x - 12 = A(x + 2)(x - 3) + Bx(x - 3) + Cx(x + 2)\\) and substitutes values or equates coefficients\nA1 \\(A = 2\\)\nA1 \\(B = -1\\)\nA1 \\(C = 3\\), giving \\(\\dfrac{2}{x} - \\dfrac{1}{x + 2} + \\dfrac{3}{x - 3}\\)` },
      { q: `The line \\(y = 2x + k\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant, is a tangent to the circle \\(x^{2} + y^{2} = 5\\).\n\nFind the possible values of \\(k\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes \\(y = 2x + k\\) into the circle equation\nA1 \\(5x^{2} + 4kx + k^{2} - 5 = 0\\)\nM1 Uses discriminant \\(= 0\\) for their quadratic\nA1 \\(16k^{2} - 20(k^{2} - 5) = 0\\), so \\(100 - 4k^{2} = 0\\)\nA1 \\(k = 5\\) or \\(k = -5\\)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(kx^{2} + 4x + (k - 3) = 0\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant, has two distinct real roots.\n\nFind the set of possible values of \\(k\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(b^{2} - 4ac \\gt 0\\): \\(16 - 4k(k - 3) \\gt 0\\)\nA1 \\(k^{2} - 3k - 4 \\lt 0\\)\nM1 Finds critical values \\(k = 4\\), \\(k = -1\\)\nA1 \\(-1 \\lt k \\lt 4\\)\nB1 \\(k \\neq 0\\) (when \\(k = 0\\) the equation is linear with only one root)\nFinal answer: \\(\\{k : -1 \\lt k \\lt 0\\} \\cup \\{k : 0 \\lt k \\lt 4\\}\\)` },
      { q: `The arch of a footbridge is modelled by the curve \\(y = 6 - kx^{2}\\), where \\(y\\) metres is the height of the arch above the road and \\(x\\) metres is the horizontal distance from the centre of the road. The arch meets the road at the points where \\(x = -4\\) and \\(x = 4\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(k = \\frac{3}{8}\\). [1]\n(b) A lorry of width 3 m and height 5.2 m drives along the centre of the road. Determine whether, according to the model, the lorry can pass under the arch. [3]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(0 = 6 - 16k\\) so \\(k = \\frac{6}{16} = \\frac{3}{8}\\) AG\n(b) M1 Uses \\(x = 1.5\\) (half the width of the lorry)\nA1 \\(y = 6 - \\frac{3}{8}(2.25) = 5.15625\\) (5.16 m)\nE1 \\(5.16 \\lt 5.2\\), so the lorry cannot pass under the arch\n(c) E1 e.g. a real arch has thickness; the road may not be level; real arches are not exactly parabolic` },
      { q: `The functions f and g are defined by\n\\(f(x) = \\sqrt{x - 3}\\) for \\(x \\geq 3\\),\n\\(g(x) = x^{2} + 1\\) for \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(gf(x)\\) in its simplest form, stating its domain. [2]\n(b) Solve the equation \\(fg(x) = 5\\), giving your answers in exact form. [3]\n(c) Explain why g does not have an inverse function. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(gf(x) = (\\sqrt{x - 3})^{2} + 1 = x - 2\\)\nB1 Domain \\(x \\geq 3\\)\n(b) M1 \\(fg(x) = \\sqrt{x^{2} - 2}\\)\nM1 \\(x^{2} - 2 = 25\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\pm 3\\sqrt{3}\\) (both required)\n(c) E1 g is many-to-one, e.g. \\(g(1) = g(-1) = 2\\)` },
      { q: `The polynomial \\(p(x) = x^{3} + ax^{2} + bx - 6\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants, has \\((x - 1)\\) as a factor. When \\(p(x)\\) is divided by \\((x + 2)\\) the remainder is \\(-12\\).\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). [4]\n(b) Show that the equation \\(p(x) = 0\\) has only one real root. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(p(1) = 0\\): \\(a + b = 5\\)\nM1 \\(p(-2) = -12\\): \\(-8 + 4a - 2b - 6 = -12\\), so \\(2a - b = 1\\)\nM1 Solves simultaneously\nA1 \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 3\\)\n(b) M1 \\(p(x) = (x - 1)(x^{2} + 3x + 6)\\)\nA1 Discriminant of quadratic \\(= 9 - 24 = -15 \\lt 0\\), so quadratic has no real roots; only real root is \\(x = 1\\)` },
      { q: `A farmer uses 60 m of fencing to make three sides of a rectangular pen. A long straight wall forms the fourth side. The two sides perpendicular to the wall each have length \\(x\\) m. The area of the pen must be at least 400 m².\n\n(a) Show that \\(x^{2} - 30x + 200 \\leq 0\\). [2]\n(b) Hence find the set of possible values of \\(x\\). [3]\n(c) State one assumption made in this model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a) M1 Side parallel to wall \\(= 60 - 2x\\) and area \\(= x(60 - 2x)\\)\nA1 \\(60x - 2x^{2} \\geq 400\\), leading to \\(x^{2} - 30x + 200 \\leq 0\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\((x - 10)(x - 20) = 0\\)\nA1 Critical values 10 and 20\nA1 \\(10 \\leq x \\leq 20\\)\n(c) E1 e.g. all 60 m of fencing is used; the wall is long enough; no fencing is needed for a gate or overlap` },
      { q: `The curve C has equation \\(y = \\dfrac{2}{x - 3} + 1\\).\n\n(a) Describe a sequence of two geometrical transformations that maps the curve \\(y = \\dfrac{1}{x}\\) onto C. [3]\n(b) Sketch C, stating the equations of its asymptotes and the coordinates of the points where it crosses the axes. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Describe', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1 Stretch parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, scale factor 2\nB1 Translation by vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\nB1 Correct order stated (stretch then translation); translation followed by stretch is B0 unless the translation is \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 0.5 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\n(b) B1 Two-branch reciprocal shape in correct quadrants relative to asymptotes\nB1 Asymptotes \\(x = 3\\) and \\(y = 1\\)\nB1 Intercepts \\(\\left(0, \\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\) and \\((1, 0)\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nA rectangular field has a perimeter of 34 m and a diagonal of length 13 m.\n\n(a) Find the length and width of the field. [5]\n(b) Hence find the area of the field. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(x + y = 17\\) and \\(x^{2} + y^{2} = 169\\)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = 17 - x\\)\nA1 \\(2x^{2} - 34x + 120 = 0\\), i.e. \\(x^{2} - 17x + 60 = 0\\)\nM1 Solves: \\((x - 5)(x - 12) = 0\\)\nA1 Length 12 m, width 5 m\n(b) B1 ft Area 60 m²` },
      { q: `(a) On the same axes, sketch the graphs of \\(y = |x - 4|\\) and \\(y = 2|x + 1|\\). [2]\n(b) Hence, or otherwise, solve the inequality \\(|x - 4| \\leq 2|x + 1|\\), giving your answer in set notation. [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Solve', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1 V-shape with vertex \\((4, 0)\\) and \\(y\\)-intercept 4\nB1 Steeper V-shape with vertex \\((-1, 0)\\) and \\(y\\)-intercept 2\n(b) M1 Squares both sides or solves \\(x - 4 = \\pm 2(x + 1)\\)\nA1 \\(3x^{2} + 16x - 12 \\geq 0\\) or critical values found\nA1 Critical values \\(x = -6\\) and \\(x = \\frac{2}{3}\\)\nA1 \\(\\{x : x \\leq -6\\} \\cup \\{x : x \\geq \\frac{2}{3}\\}\\)` },
      { q: `The function f is defined by \\(f(x) = x^{2} - 4x + 7\\) for \\(x \\geq 2\\).\n\n(a) Find the range of f. [2]\n(b) Find an expression for \\(f^{-1}(x)\\) and state the domain of \\(f^{-1}\\). [3]\n(c) Show that the equation \\(f(x) = f^{-1}(x)\\) has no real solutions. Fully justify your answer. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(f(x) = (x - 2)^{2} + 3\\)\nA1 \\(f(x) \\geq 3\\)\n(b) M1 \\(y = (x - 2)^{2} + 3\\), so \\(x = 2 \\pm \\sqrt{y - 3}\\)\nA1 \\(f^{-1}(x) = 2 + \\sqrt{x - 3}\\) (positive root only, since \\(f^{-1}(x) \\geq 2\\))\nB1 Domain \\(x \\geq 3\\)\n(c) M1 Since f is increasing, any intersection of \\(y = f(x)\\) and \\(y = f^{-1}(x)\\) lies on \\(y = x\\); forms \\(x^{2} - 5x + 7 = 0\\)\nA1 Discriminant \\(25 - 28 = -3 \\lt 0\\), so no real solutions` },
      { q: `An open box is made from a rectangular sheet of card measuring 20 cm by 12 cm. A square of side \\(x\\) cm is cut from each corner and the sides are folded up.\n\n(a) Show that the volume, \\(V\\) cm³, of the box is given by \\(V = 4x^{3} - 64x^{2} + 240x\\). [2]\n(b) State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the model is valid. [1]\n(c) The volume of the box is 256 cm³. Use the factor theorem to show that \\(x = 2\\) is a possible value, and find, in exact form, the other possible value of \\(x\\). [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(V = x(20 - 2x)(12 - 2x)\\)\nA1 Expands correctly to \\(4x^{3} - 64x^{2} + 240x\\) AG\n(b) B1 \\(0 \\lt x \\lt 6\\)\n(c) B1 \\(x^{3} - 16x^{2} + 60x - 64 = 0\\) and substitution of \\(x = 2\\) gives \\(8 - 64 + 120 - 64 = 0\\)\nM1 Quadratic factor \\(x^{2} - 14x + 32\\)\nA1 \\(x = 7 \\pm \\sqrt{17}\\)\nA1 \\(x = 7 - \\sqrt{17}\\) only, rejecting \\(7 + \\sqrt{17}\\) since it is greater than 6` },
      { q: `The equation \\((p + 1)x^{2} - 4x + (p - 2) = 0\\), where \\(p\\) is a constant and \\(p \\neq -1\\), has real roots.\n\n(a) Show that \\(p^{2} - p - 6 \\leq 0\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the set of possible values of \\(p\\). [3]\n(c) Find the root of the equation when \\(p\\) takes its greatest possible value. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a) M1 Uses \\(b^{2} - 4ac \\geq 0\\): \\(16 - 4(p + 1)(p - 2) \\geq 0\\)\nA1 \\(16 - 4p^{2} + 4p + 8 \\geq 0\\)\nA1 Divides by \\(-4\\), reversing the inequality, to obtain \\(p^{2} - p - 6 \\leq 0\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\((p - 3)(p + 2) = 0\\)\nA1 \\(-2 \\leq p \\leq 3\\)\nA1 \\(p \\neq -1\\) stated: \\(\\{p : -2 \\leq p \\lt -1\\} \\cup \\{p : -1 \\lt p \\leq 3\\}\\)\n(c) B1 \\(p = 3\\): \\(4x^{2} - 4x + 1 = 0\\), \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\)` },
      { q: `The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of a drug in a patient's blood \\(t\\) hours after the drug is taken is modelled by\n\\(C = \\dfrac{20t}{t^{2} + 4}\\), \\(t \\geq 0\\).\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(t\\) for which \\(C = 4\\). [3]\n(b) Hence state the length of time for which the concentration is at least 4 mg per litre. [1]\n(c) Show that \\(5 - C = \\dfrac{5(t - 2)^{2}}{t^{2} + 4}\\), and hence state the maximum concentration according to the model. [2]\n(d) State one limitation of the model for large values of \\(t\\). [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(20t = 4(t^{2} + 4)\\)\nA1 \\(t^{2} - 5t + 4 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = 1\\) or \\(t = 4\\)\n(b) B1 ft 3 hours (from \\(t = 1\\) to \\(t = 4\\))\n(c) M1 \\(5 - C = \\dfrac{5t^{2} + 20 - 20t}{t^{2} + 4} = \\dfrac{5(t - 2)^{2}}{t^{2} + 4}\\) AG\nA1 Since this is \\(\\geq 0\\), max concentration 5 mg per litre (at \\(t = 2\\))\n(d) E1 e.g. the model predicts C never reaches zero, so the drug is never fully removed from the blood` },
      { q: `The functions f and g are defined by\n\\(f(x) = 3 - 2x\\) for \\(x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\),\n\\(g(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x + 2}\\) for \\(x \\geq 0\\).\n\n(a) Find the range of g. [2]\n(b) Find an expression for \\(g^{-1}(x)\\) and state the domain of \\(g^{-1}\\). [3]\n(c) Solve the equation \\(fg(x) = 1\\). [3]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(g(0) = \\frac{5}{2}\\) and g decreasing towards 0\nB1 \\(0 \\lt g(x) \\leq \\frac{5}{2}\\)\n(b) M1 \\(y = \\dfrac{5}{x + 2}\\), so \\(x = \\dfrac{5}{y} - 2\\)\nA1 \\(g^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{5}{x} - 2\\)\nB1 ft Domain \\(0 \\lt x \\leq \\frac{5}{2}\\)\n(c) M1 \\(fg(x) = 3 - \\dfrac{10}{x + 2}\\)\nM1 \\(3 - \\dfrac{10}{x + 2} = 1\\), so \\(x + 2 = 5\\)\nA1 \\(x = 3\\) (which is in the domain of g)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = x^{3} - 3x^{2} - 6x + 8\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\((x - 1)\\) is a factor of \\(f(x)\\). [1]\n(b) Factorise \\(f(x)\\) completely. [2]\n(c) Sketch the curve \\(y = f(x)\\), stating the coordinates of the points where it meets the axes. [3]\n(d) Hence solve the inequality \\(f(x) \\leq 0\\). [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(f(1) = 1 - 3 - 6 + 8 = 0\\), so \\((x - 1)\\) is a factor\n(b) M1 Quadratic factor \\(x^{2} - 2x - 8\\)\nA1 \\((x - 1)(x - 4)(x + 2)\\)\n(c) B1 Positive cubic shape\nB1 ft Roots at \\((-2, 0)\\), \\((1, 0)\\), \\((4, 0)\\)\nB1 \\(y\\)-intercept \\((0, 8)\\)\n(d) M1 Identifies where the curve is on or below the \\(x\\)-axis\nA1 \\(x \\leq -2\\) or \\(1 \\leq x \\leq 4\\)` },
      { q: `The entrance to a tunnel is modelled by the curve \\(y = 4x - 0.4x^{2}\\), \\(0 \\leq x \\leq 10\\), where \\(x\\) metres is the horizontal distance from one side of the base and \\(y\\) metres is the height above the road.\n\n(a) Express \\(4x - 0.4x^{2}\\) in the form \\(a - b(x - c)^{2}\\). [3]\n(b) Write down the maximum height of the tunnel entrance. [1]\n(c) A road of width 7 m runs centrally through the tunnel. Vehicles must have a clearance of at least 0.5 m between their highest point and the tunnel at every point above the road. Find the maximum permitted height of a vehicle, assuming it may occupy the full width of the road. [2]\n(d) A second tunnel entrance is designed by applying a stretch, scale factor 1.5, parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis to the first model. Find the equation of the curve modelling the second entrance and state its width at the base. [2]\n(e) Comment on the suitability of a quadratic model for the tunnel entrance. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(-0.4(x^{2} - 10x)\\)\nM1 \\(-0.4[(x - 5)^{2} - 25]\\)\nA1 \\(10 - 0.4(x - 5)^{2}\\)\n(b) B1 ft 10 m\n(c) M1 Height at edge of road, \\(x = 1.5\\) (or 8.5): \\(10 - 0.4(3.5)^{2} = 5.1\\)\nA1 \\(5.1 - 0.5 = 4.6\\) m\n(d) B1 \\(y = 4\\left(\\frac{x}{1.5}\\right) - 0.4\\left(\\frac{x}{1.5}\\right)^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}x - \\frac{8}{45}x^{2}\\)\nB1 Width 15 m\n(e) E1 e.g. reasonable as tunnel entrances are often arch-shaped and symmetrical, but the walls of a real tunnel are often vertical near the base, so the parabola underestimates the usable width` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = 2x^{3} + x^{2} - 13x + 6\\).\n\n(a) Use the factor theorem to show that \\((x + 3)\\) is a factor of \\(f(x)\\). [2]\n(b) Factorise \\(f(x)\\) completely. [2]\n(c) Express \\(\\dfrac{2x^{2} - 8x - 7}{f(x)}\\) in partial fractions. [4]\n(d) Hence, or otherwise, solve the equation \\(2x^{\\frac{3}{2}} + x - 13x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 6 = 0\\). [2]`, marks: 10, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) M1 Evaluates \\(f(-3) = -54 + 9 + 39 + 6\\)\nA1 \\(= 0\\), so \\((x + 3)\\) is a factor\n(b) M1 Quadratic factor \\(2x^{2} - 5x + 2\\)\nA1 \\((x + 3)(2x - 1)(x - 2)\\)\n(c) B1 ft Form \\(\\dfrac{A}{x + 3} + \\dfrac{B}{2x - 1} + \\dfrac{C}{x - 2}\\)\nM1 \\(2x^{2} - 8x - 7 = A(2x - 1)(x - 2) + B(x + 3)(x - 2) + C(x + 3)(2x - 1)\\) and substitutes values\nA1 Two of \\(A = 1\\), \\(B = 2\\), \\(C = -1\\)\nA1 \\(\\dfrac{1}{x + 3} + \\dfrac{2}{2x - 1} - \\dfrac{1}{x - 2}\\)\n(d) M1 Recognises \\(f(\\sqrt{x}) = 0\\), so \\(\\sqrt{x} = -3, \\frac{1}{2}, 2\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\frac{1}{4}\\) or \\(x = 4\\) only (rejects \\(\\sqrt{x} = -3\\))` }
    ]
  },
  '1.3': {
    name: 'Coordinate Geometry',
    questions: [
      { q: `A circle has equation \\((x + 4)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 20\\).\n\nWrite down the coordinates of the centre of the circle and the exact radius of the circle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Write down', markScheme: `B1 Centre \\((-4, 1)\\)\nB1 Radius \\(\\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt{5}\\) (accept \\(\\sqrt{20}\\); 4.47 alone is B0 as exact required)` },
      { q: `The line \\(l\\) has equation \\(3x - 2y + 6 = 0\\).\n\nFind the gradient of a line which is perpendicular to \\(l\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Gradient of \\(l\\) is \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\) soi (from \\(y = \\frac{3}{2}x + 3\\))\nB1 ft Perpendicular gradient \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\) (ft negative reciprocal of their gradient)` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = 2t - 1,\\ y = 4 - t\\).\n\nFind a cartesian equation of the curve, giving your answer in the form \\(ax + by = c\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are integers.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Eliminates \\(t\\), e.g. \\(t = \\frac{x + 1}{2}\\) substituted into \\(y = 4 - t\\), or \\(t = 4 - y\\) substituted into \\(x = 2t - 1\\)\nA1 \\(x + 2y = 7\\) oe with integers` },
      { q: `A ball is kicked from a point O on horizontal ground. Its position t seconds later is modelled by the parametric equations\n\\(x = 12t,\\ y = 15t - 4.9t^2\\),\nwhere x m and y m are the horizontal and vertical distances of the ball from O.\n\nFind, according to the model, the horizontal distance from O at which the ball lands.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Sets \\(y = 0\\) and solves for non-zero \\(t\\): \\(t = \\frac{15}{4.9} = 3.06...\\)\nA1 \\(x = 12 \\times 3.06... = 36.7\\) m (awrt 36.7)` },
      { q: `The circle C has equation \\(x^2 + y^2 - 6x + 2y - 15 = 0\\).\n\nFind the centre and radius of C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Attempt to complete the square in both x and y: \\((x - 3)^2 - 9 + (y + 1)^2 - 1 - 15 = 0\\)\nA1 Centre \\((3, -1)\\)\nA1 \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 25\\), so radius 5 cao` },
      { q: `The points A and B have coordinates \\((-2, 5)\\) and \\((4, 1)\\) respectively.\n\nFind the equation of the line AB, giving your answer in the form \\(ax + by + c = 0\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are integers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Gradient \\(= \\frac{1 - 5}{4 - (-2)} = -\\frac{2}{3}\\)\nM1 Uses \\(y - y_1 = m(x - x_1)\\) with their gradient and either point, e.g. \\(y - 5 = -\\frac{2}{3}(x + 2)\\)\nA1 \\(2x + 3y - 11 = 0\\) oe with integers` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations\n\\(x = 3 + 2\\cos\\theta,\\ y = -1 + 2\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\\).\n\nShow that the curve is a circle, stating its centre and radius.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 Writes \\(\\cos\\theta = \\frac{x - 3}{2}\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{y + 1}{2}\\) and uses \\(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\\)\nA1 \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 4\\), so a circle\nA1 Centre \\((3, -1)\\), radius 2` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = \\frac{2}{t},\\ y = 3t - 1\\), for \\(t \\neq 0\\).\n\nFind a cartesian equation of the curve in the form \\(y = \\mathrm{f}(x)\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Makes \\(t\\) the subject: \\(t = \\frac{2}{x}\\)\nM1 Substitutes into \\(y = 3t - 1\\)\nA1 \\(y = \\frac{6}{x} - 1\\) oe, e.g. \\(y = \\frac{6 - x}{x}\\)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe value £V of a machine t years after it was bought is modelled as a linear function of t. When \\(t = 2\\), \\(V = 18\\,000\\) and when \\(t = 5\\), \\(V = 10\\,500\\).\n\n(a) Find an equation for V in terms of t. [2]\n(b) Interpret the gradient of the line in the context of the model. [1]`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Gradient \\(= \\frac{10\\,500 - 18\\,000}{5 - 2} = -2500\\)\nA1 \\(V = 23\\,000 - 2500t\\) cao\n(b)\nE1 The value of the machine decreases by £2500 each year (must refer to value and per year)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe region in which a phone mast gives reliable signal is modelled by the interior of the circle\n\\((x - 2)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 40\\),\nwhere x and y are in km. A farmhouse is at the point P(6, 4).\n\nDetermine whether, according to the model, the farmhouse receives reliable signal. Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `M1 Finds the (squared) distance from the centre \\((2, -1)\\) to P: \\((6 - 2)^2 + (4 + 1)^2\\)\nA1 \\(= 41\\) (or distance \\(\\sqrt{41} = 6.40\\) km compared with radius \\(\\sqrt{40} = 6.32\\) km)\nE1 \\(41 \\gt 40\\) so P lies outside the circle: the farmhouse does not receive reliable signal (conclusion in context)` },
      { q: `Two straight roads on a map are modelled by the lines\n\\(l_1: y = 3x - 4\\) and \\(l_2: x + 2y = 13\\).\nThe roads cross at the junction P.\n\nFind the coordinates of P.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes one equation into the other: \\(x + 2(3x - 4) = 13\\)\nA1 \\(x = 3\\)\nA1 \\(y = 5\\), so P is \\((3, 5)\\)` },
      { q: `The points A and B have coordinates \\((-3, 2)\\) and \\((5, 6)\\) respectively.\n\nFind the equation of the perpendicular bisector of AB, giving your answer in the form \\(ax + by + c = 0\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Midpoint of AB is \\((1, 4)\\)\nM1 Gradient of AB \\(= \\frac{4}{8} = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and uses negative reciprocal: \\(-2\\)\nM1 Line through their midpoint with their perpendicular gradient: \\(y - 4 = -2(x - 1)\\)\nA1 \\(2x + y - 6 = 0\\) oe` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA garden designer models the edge of a circular flower bed as a circle with centre C(2, −3), where units are metres. A straight path touches the edge of the flower bed at the point A(6, 0).\n\nFind the equation of the line which models the path, giving your answer in the form \\(ax + by = c\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Gradient of CA \\(= \\frac{0 - (-3)}{6 - 2} = \\frac{3}{4}\\)\nM1 Tangent is perpendicular to radius: gradient \\(-\\frac{4}{3}\\)\nM1 Line through A with their gradient: \\(y - 0 = -\\frac{4}{3}(x - 6)\\)\nA1 \\(4x + 3y = 24\\) oe` },
      { q: `The line \\(y = x + k\\) is a tangent to the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = 8\\).\n\nFind the possible values of the constant k.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes \\(y = x + k\\) into the circle equation\nA1 \\(2x^2 + 2kx + k^2 - 8 = 0\\)\nM1 Uses discriminant \\(= 0\\): \\((2k)^2 - 4(2)(k^2 - 8) = 0\\)\nA1 \\(k = 4\\) or \\(k = -4\\)` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations\n\\(x = \\cos 2\\theta,\\ y = \\sin\\theta,\\quad 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n(a) Show that a cartesian equation of the curve is \\(x = 1 - 2y^2\\). [2]\n(b) State the set of possible values of x and the set of possible values of y for this curve. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Uses \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 1 - 2\\sin^2\\theta\\)\nA1 \\(x = 1 - 2y^2\\) AG, correctly obtained\n(b)\nB1 \\(-1 \\leq x \\leq 1\\)\nB1 \\(0 \\leq y \\leq 1\\)` },
      { q: `The points A(−1, 4) and B(7, −2) are the ends of a diameter of the circle C.\n\nFind the equation of C.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Centre is the midpoint of AB: \\((3, 1)\\)\nM1 Finds the length of AB (or distance from their centre to A or B): \\(\\sqrt{8^2 + 6^2}\\)\nA1 Radius 5 (or \\(r^2 = 25\\))\nA1 \\((x - 3)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 25\\) oe` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^2 + y^2 + 2x - 10y + k = 0\\), where k is a constant, represents a circle.\n\n(a) Given that the radius of the circle is 4, find the value of k. [3]\n(b) State the set of values of k for which the equation represents a circle. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Completes the square: \\((x + 1)^2 + (y - 5)^2 = 26 - k\\)\nA1 \\(r^2 = 26 - k\\) soi\nA1 \\(26 - k = 16\\), so \\(k = 10\\)\n(b)\nB1 \\(k \\lt 26\\) (since \\(r^2 = 26 - k\\) must be positive)` },
      { q: `A stone is thrown from the top of a small wall. Its position t seconds later is modelled by\n\\(x = 20t,\\ y = 1.5 + 10t - 4.9t^2\\),\nwhere x m is the horizontal distance from the wall and y m is the height above the ground.\n\n(a) Find a cartesian equation for the path of the stone. [2]\n(b) Find, according to the model, the greatest height of the stone above the ground. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Substitutes \\(t = \\frac{x}{20}\\) into the expression for y\nA1 \\(y = 1.5 + \\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{49x^2}{4000}\\) oe, e.g. \\(y = 1.5 + 0.5x - 0.01225x^2\\)\n(b)\nM1 Any complete method for the maximum, e.g. \\(t = \\frac{10}{9.8}\\) or completing the square or vertex at \\(x = 20.4\\)\nA1 6.60 m (awrt 6.6)` },
      { q: `Show that the line \\(3x + 4y = 25\\) is a tangent to the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = 25\\), and find the coordinates of the point of contact.`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes \\(y = \\frac{25 - 3x}{4}\\) (or x in terms of y) into the circle\nA1 \\(25x^2 - 150x + 225 = 0\\), i.e. \\(x^2 - 6x + 9 = 0\\)\nA1 \\((x - 3)^2 = 0\\): a repeated root (or discriminant \\(= 36 - 36 = 0\\)) so the line is a tangent\nA1 Point of contact \\((3, 4)\\)` },
      { q: `The line \\(y = x + 1\\) meets the circle \\(x^2 + y^2 = 25\\) at the points A and B.\n\nFind the exact length of the chord AB.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes: \\(x^2 + (x + 1)^2 = 25\\)\nA1 \\(x^2 + x - 12 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(x = 3\\) or \\(x = -4\\), giving A(3, 4) and B(−4, −3)\nM1 Uses Pythagoras on their two points: \\(\\sqrt{7^2 + 7^2}\\)\nA1 \\(7\\sqrt{2}\\) cao` },
      { q: `The points A, B and C have coordinates \\((-1, 2)\\), \\((3, 4)\\) and \\((5, 0)\\) respectively.\n\n(a) Show that AB is perpendicular to BC. [2]\n(b) Hence find an equation of the circle which passes through A, B and C. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Gradient AB \\(= \\frac{1}{2}\\) and gradient BC \\(= -2\\)\nA1 Product of gradients is \\(-1\\), so AB is perpendicular to BC AG\n(b)\nB1 Angle ABC \\(= 90°\\) so AC is a diameter (angle in a semicircle)\nM1 Centre at midpoint of AC, \\((2, 1)\\), and radius from centre to A, B or C\nA1 \\((x - 2)^2 + (y - 1)^2 = 10\\)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe position of a passenger on an observation wheel t minutes after boarding is modelled by\n\\(x = 20\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{15}\\right),\\ y = 22 - 20\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{15}\\right)\\),\nwhere x m is the horizontal displacement from the vertical line through the centre of the wheel and y m is the height above the ground.\n\n(a) Show that the path of the passenger satisfies \\(x^2 + (y - 22)^2 = 400\\). [2]\n(b) State the height of the passenger when they board and the time taken for one complete revolution. [2]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{15}\\right) = \\frac{x}{20}\\), \\(\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{15}\\right) = \\frac{22 - y}{20}\\) and uses \\(\\sin^2 + \\cos^2 = 1\\)\nA1 \\(x^2 + (y - 22)^2 = 400\\) AG\n(b)\nB1 Height 2 m (at \\(t = 0\\))\nB1 30 minutes (period \\(\\frac{2\\pi}{\\pi/15}\\))\n(c)\nE1 e.g. assumes the wheel turns at constant speed / does not stop to let passengers on and off / treats the passenger as a point` },
      { q: `The circle C has equation \\((x - 4)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 10\\). The line \\(y = 3x + c\\) is a tangent to C.\n\nFind the possible values of c.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Substitutes \\(y = 3x + c\\) into the equation of C\nA1 \\(10x^2 + (6c - 20)x + c^2 - 4c + 10 = 0\\) oe\nM1 Sets discriminant to 0: \\((6c - 20)^2 - 40(c^2 - 4c + 10) = 0\\)\nA1 \\(c^2 + 20c = 0\\) oe\nA1 \\(c = 0\\) or \\(c = -20\\)\n(Alternative: perpendicular distance from (4, 2) to the line equals \\(\\sqrt{10}\\): M1 A1 \\(\\frac{|10 + c|}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\sqrt{10}\\), M1 A1 \\(|10 + c| = 10\\), A1 both values)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA straight cycle path is modelled by the line through A(1, 2) and B(9, 8), where units are km. A café is at C(8, 1). A new straight path is to be built from C to meet AB at the point D, so that CD is perpendicular to AB.\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of D. [4]\n(b) Find the length of the new path CD. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Gradient AB \\(= \\frac{3}{4}\\) and equation of AB: \\(y - 2 = \\frac{3}{4}(x - 1)\\)\nM1 Line through C with gradient \\(-\\frac{4}{3}\\): \\(y - 1 = -\\frac{4}{3}(x - 8)\\)\nM1 Solves their two equations simultaneously\nA1 D(5, 5)\n(b)\nB1 ft \\(CD = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5\\) km` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations\n\\(x = 3\\tan\\theta,\\ y = 2\\sec\\theta,\\quad -\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\n(a) Show that a cartesian equation of the curve is \\(9y^2 - 4x^2 = 36\\). [3]\n(b) State, with a reason, the set of possible values of y. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Writes \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{x}{3}\\), \\(\\sec\\theta = \\frac{y}{2}\\)\nM1 Uses \\(1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta\\): \\(1 + \\frac{x^2}{9} = \\frac{y^2}{4}\\)\nA1 \\(9y^2 - 4x^2 = 36\\) AG\n(b)\nE1 For \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2} \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), \\(0 \\lt \\cos\\theta \\leq 1\\) so \\(\\sec\\theta \\geq 1\\)\nB1 \\(y \\geq 2\\)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA surveyor marks the corners of a triangular plot of land at A(2, 1), B(6, 3) and C(4, 7), where units are metres.\n\nShow that the triangle ABC is right-angled, and find its area.`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 Finds gradients of two sides: AB \\(= \\frac{1}{2}\\), BC \\(= -2\\)\nA1 Product \\(= -1\\), so angle ABC is a right angle\nM1 Finds lengths of AB and BC using Pythagoras\nA1 \\(AB = BC = \\sqrt{20}\\)\nA1 Area \\(= \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\sqrt{20} \\times \\sqrt{20} = 10\\) m²` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA lighthouse is at L(3, 4), where units are km. A ship can see the light when it is within 5 km of L. The ship sails along a straight course modelled by the line \\(y = 2x - 7\\).\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of the points where the ship first sees the light and where it loses sight of the light. [4]\n(b) Find the distance the ship travels while it can see the light. [1]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Uses circle \\((x - 3)^2 + (y - 4)^2 = 25\\) and substitutes \\(y = 2x - 7\\)\nA1 \\(5x^2 - 50x + 105 = 0\\), i.e. \\(x^2 - 10x + 21 = 0\\)\nM1 Solves their quadratic: \\(x = 3\\) or \\(x = 7\\)\nA1 \\((3, -1)\\) and \\((7, 7)\\)\n(b)\nB1 ft \\(\\sqrt{4^2 + 8^2} = \\sqrt{80} = 8.94\\) km\n(c)\nE1 e.g. visibility would depend on weather / the ship is treated as a point / the course may not be exactly straight / the Earth is treated as flat` },
      { q: `The circle C has equation \\(x^2 + y^2 - 6x - 4y + 8 = 0\\). The point A(4, 4) lies on C.\n\n(a) Find the centre and the exact radius of C. [2]\n(b) Show that the tangent to C at A has equation \\(x + 2y = 12\\). [3]\nThe tangent at A meets the x-axis at the point T.\n(c) Find the exact length AT. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 Centre \\((3, 2)\\)\nB1 Radius \\(\\sqrt{5}\\)\n(b)\nM1 Gradient of radius CA \\(= \\frac{4 - 2}{4 - 3} = 2\\)\nM1 Tangent gradient \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\) and line through A: \\(y - 4 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x - 4)\\)\nA1 \\(x + 2y = 12\\) AG\n(c)\nB1 T(12, 0) so \\(AT = \\sqrt{8^2 + 4^2} = 4\\sqrt{5}\\)` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = t^2,\\ y = 2t\\).\n\n(a) Find a cartesian equation of the curve. [2]\n(b) Find the coordinates of the points where the curve meets the line \\(y = x - 3\\). [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(t = \\frac{y}{2}\\) substituted into \\(x = t^2\\)\nA1 \\(y^2 = 4x\\)\n(b)\nM1 Substitutes parametric form into the line: \\(2t = t^2 - 3\\) (or uses their cartesian equation)\nA1 \\(t^2 - 2t - 3 = 0\\)\nM1 Solves: \\(t = 3\\) or \\(t = -1\\)\nA1 \\((9, 6)\\) and \\((1, -2)\\)` },
      { q: `A circle passes through the points A(1, 7) and B(7, 5). The centre of the circle lies on the line \\(y = x - 2\\).\n\nFind an equation of the circle.`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Midpoint of AB is \\((4, 6)\\)\nM1 Gradient AB \\(= -\\frac{1}{3}\\) so perpendicular gradient \\(3\\)\nA1 Perpendicular bisector \\(y = 3x - 6\\)\nM1 Solves with \\(y = x - 2\\)\nA1 Centre \\((2, 0)\\)\nA1 \\((x - 2)^2 + y^2 = 50\\)` },
      { q: `The circle C has equation \\((x - 4)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 25\\) and the line l has equation \\(y = 2x + 10\\).\n\n(a) Show that l does not meet C. [3]\n(b) Find the exact shortest distance between a point on C and a point on l. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = 2x + 10\\) into the equation of C\nA1 \\(5x^2 + 20x + 40 = 0\\), i.e. \\(x^2 + 4x + 8 = 0\\)\nA1 Discriminant \\(= 16 - 32 = -16 \\lt 0\\), so no real roots and l does not meet C\n(b)\nM1 Finds foot of perpendicular from centre (4, 3) to l, using gradient \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\): \\((-2, 6)\\)\nA1 Distance from centre to l \\(= \\sqrt{36 + 9} = 3\\sqrt{5}\\)\nA1 Shortest distance \\(= 3\\sqrt{5} - 5\\)` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations\n\\(x = 2\\cos\\theta,\\ y = \\cos 2\\theta,\\quad 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi\\).\n\n(a) Show that the cartesian equation of the curve is \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 - 1\\). [3]\n(b) Sketch the curve, stating the coordinates of its end-points and of the points where it crosses the axes. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Uses \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1\\)\nM1 Substitutes \\(\\cos\\theta = \\frac{x}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(y = 2\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2 - 1 = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 - 1\\) AG\n(b)\nB1 U-shaped parabola segment, symmetric about the y-axis, existing only for \\(-2 \\leq x \\leq 2\\)\nB1 End-points \\((2, 1)\\) and \\((-2, 1)\\)\nB1 Crosses y-axis at \\((0, -1)\\) and x-axis at \\((\\pm\\sqrt{2}, 0)\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nThe circle C has centre P(1, 2) and radius \\(\\sqrt{13}\\). The line \\(x + y = 6\\) meets C at the points A and B.\n\n(a) Find the exact x-coordinates of A and B. [3]\n(b) The point M is the midpoint of AB. Show that PM is perpendicular to AB. [2]\n(c) Find the exact length of AB. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `DR\n(a)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = 6 - x\\) into \\((x - 1)^2 + (y - 2)^2 = 13\\)\nA1 \\(x^2 - 5x + 2 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{17}}{2}\\) (must use formula with working shown, not calculator)\n(b)\nB1 M is \\(\\left(\\frac{5}{2}, \\frac{7}{2}\\right)\\)\nB1 Gradient PM \\(= \\frac{3/2}{3/2} = 1\\), gradient AB \\(= -1\\), product \\(-1\\) so perpendicular\n(c)\nM1 Differences in x and in y are both \\(\\sqrt{17}\\)\nA1 \\(AB = \\sqrt{34}\\)` },
      { q: `A golf ball is hit from a point O on horizontal ground. The position of the ball t seconds later is modelled by\n\\(x = 30t,\\ y = 20t - 5t^2,\\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 4\\),\nwhere x m and y m are the horizontal and vertical distances of the ball from O.\n\n(a) Find a cartesian equation for the path of the ball. [2]\n(b) Find the horizontal distance from O at which the ball lands. [2]\nA tree of height 18 m stands vertically at a horizontal distance of 90 m from O, in the plane of the ball's path.\n(c) Determine whether the model predicts that the ball passes over the tree. [2]\n(d) State one assumption made in this model. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Substitutes \\(t = \\frac{x}{30}\\)\nA1 \\(y = \\frac{2x}{3} - \\frac{x^2}{180}\\) oe\n(b)\nM1 Sets \\(y = 0\\) (in either form) and finds non-zero root\nA1 120 m\n(c)\nM1 Finds y when \\(x = 90\\) (or \\(t = 3\\)): \\(y = 60 - 45\\)\nA1 \\(y = 15\\), which is less than 18, so the ball does not pass over the tree\n(d)\nB1 e.g. no air resistance / ball modelled as a particle / no spin or wind` },
      { q: `An ellipse has parametric equations\n\\(x = 4\\cos t,\\ y = 2\\sin t,\\quad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\\).\n\n(a) Find a cartesian equation of the ellipse. [2]\n(b) Show that at any point where the ellipse meets the line \\(x + 2y = 4\\), \\(\\cos t + \\sin t = 1\\). [1]\n(c) By expressing \\(\\cos t + \\sin t\\) in the form \\(R\\sin(t + \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0 \\lt \\alpha \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), find the coordinates of the points where the ellipse meets the line. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Uses \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\) with \\(\\cos t = \\frac{x}{4}\\), \\(\\sin t = \\frac{y}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{x^2}{16} + \\frac{y^2}{4} = 1\\) oe\n(b)\nB1 \\(4\\cos t + 4\\sin t = 4\\) so \\(\\cos t + \\sin t = 1\\) AG\n(c)\nM1 \\(R = \\sqrt{2}\\), \\(\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)\nM1 \\(\\sin\\left(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\) and finds two values of \\(t + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) in range: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\)\nA1 \\(t = 0\\) or \\(t = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\nA1 \\((4, 0)\\) and \\((0, 2)\\)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nA radar station at the origin O detects aircraft within 50 km. Units are km. An aircraft flies in a straight line at a constant height from A(−60, 20) towards B(40, 70). The height of the aircraft may be ignored.\n\n(a) Find the equation of the line AB in the form \\(y = mx + c\\). [2]\n(b) Find the coordinates of the points at which the aircraft enters and leaves the region detected by the radar. [3]\n(c) The aircraft flies at a constant speed of 400 km h⁻¹. Find the length of time, in minutes, for which the aircraft is detected. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Gradient \\(= \\frac{50}{100} = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 50\\)\n(b)\nM1 Substitutes into \\(x^2 + y^2 = 2500\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{5}{4}x^2 + 50x = 0\\)\nA1 Enters at \\((-40, 30)\\), leaves at \\((0, 50)\\)\n(c)\nM1 Distance \\(= \\sqrt{40^2 + 20^2} = \\sqrt{2000} = 44.7\\) km and divides by 400\nA1 6.71 minutes (awrt 6.7)` },
      { q: `The circle C has equation \\(x^2 + y^2 - 10x - 6y + 9 = 0\\).\n\n(a) Find the centre and radius of C. [3]\n(b) Show that C touches the y-axis, and state the coordinates of the point of contact. [2]\n(c) The y-axis is one tangent to C which passes through the origin O. Find the equation of the other tangent to C which passes through O. [3]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Completes the square: \\((x - 5)^2 + (y - 3)^2 = 25 + 9 - 9\\)\nA1 Centre \\((5, 3)\\)\nA1 Radius 5\n(b)\nE1 Distance of centre from y-axis is 5, equal to the radius (or \\(x = 0\\) gives \\((y - 3)^2 = 0\\), a repeated root)\nB1 Point of contact \\((0, 3)\\)\n(c)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = mx\\): \\((1 + m^2)x^2 - (10 + 6m)x + 9 = 0\\)\nM1 Discriminant \\(= 0\\): \\((10 + 6m)^2 - 36(1 + m^2) = 0\\), giving \\(64 + 120m = 0\\)\nA1 \\(y = -\\frac{8}{15}x\\)` },
      { q: `(data written for this practice question)\nThe inside of a bridge over a canal is modelled by the curve with parametric equations\n\\(x = 20 - 20\\cos\\theta,\\ y = 8\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi\\),\nwhere x m is the horizontal distance from one foot of the arch and y m is the height above the water.\n\n(a) Find a cartesian equation of the curve. [3]\n(b) Find the width of the arch at a height of 6 m above the water. Give your answer to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(c) Explain why the model uses the restriction \\(0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi\\). [1]\n(d) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\cos\\theta = \\frac{20 - x}{20}\\), \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{y}{8}\\)\nM1 Uses \\(\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{(x - 20)^2}{400} + \\frac{y^2}{64} = 1\\) oe\n(b)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = 6\\): \\((x - 20)^2 = 400\\left(1 - \\frac{36}{64}\\right) = 175\\)\nA1 \\(x - 20 = \\pm 5\\sqrt{7}\\)\nA1 Width \\(= 10\\sqrt{7} = 26.5\\) m\n(c)\nE1 It gives \\(y \\geq 0\\), so only the upper half of the ellipse (the arch above the water) is modelled\n(d)\nE1 e.g. the water level varies / the real arch may not be exactly elliptical / the thickness of the bridge is ignored` },
      { q: `The points A(0, 3), B(6, 3) and C(8, −1) lie on the circle S.\n\n(a) By considering the perpendicular bisectors of AB and BC, find the equation of S. [5]\n(b) Find the equation of the tangent to S at C. [2]\n(c) The tangent to S at A meets the tangent to S at C at the point T. Find the coordinates of T. [2]`, marks: 9, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 Perpendicular bisector of AB is \\(x = 3\\)\nM1 Midpoint of BC \\((7, 1)\\) and gradient BC \\(= -2\\), so perpendicular gradient \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(y - 1 = \\frac{1}{2}(x - 7)\\)\nA1 Centre \\((3, -1)\\)\nA1 \\((x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 25\\)\n(b)\nM1 Radius to C is horizontal (gradient 0), so tangent is vertical\nA1 \\(x = 8\\)\n(c)\nM1 Gradient of radius to A is \\(-\\frac{4}{3}\\), so tangent at A is \\(y = \\frac{3}{4}x + 3\\)\nA1 T is \\((8, 9)\\)` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations\n\\(x = t + \\frac{1}{t},\\ y = t - \\frac{1}{t},\\quad t \\neq 0\\).\n\n(a) Show that a cartesian equation of the curve is \\(x^2 - y^2 = 4\\). [3]\n(b) Find the coordinates of the points where the curve meets the line \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\\). [4]\n(c) Find the value of t at each of these points. [2]\n(d) Explain why the curve does not meet the line \\(y = x\\). [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Forms \\(x + y = 2t\\) and \\(x - y = \\frac{2}{t}\\) (or squares both and subtracts)\nM1 Multiplies (or subtracts squares): \\((x + y)(x - y) = 4\\)\nA1 \\(x^2 - y^2 = 4\\) AG\n(b)\nM1 Substitutes \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}x + 1\\) into \\(x^2 - y^2 = 4\\)\nA1 \\(3x^2 - 4x - 20 = 0\\)\nM1 Solves: \\((3x - 10)(x + 2) = 0\\)\nA1 \\(\\left(\\frac{10}{3}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\) and \\((-2, 0)\\)\n(c)\nM1 Uses \\(t = \\frac{x + y}{2}\\) (or solves \\(t + \\frac{1}{t} = x\\) and checks y)\nA1 \\(t = 3\\) at \\(\\left(\\frac{10}{3}, \\frac{8}{3}\\right)\\) and \\(t = -1\\) at \\((-2, 0)\\)\n(d)\nE1 If \\(y = x\\) then \\(x^2 - y^2 = 0 \\neq 4\\) (or \\(\\frac{1}{t} = 0\\) has no solution), so no intersection` }
    ]
  },
  '1.4': {
    name: 'Sequences & Series',
    questions: [
      { q: `Find the coefficient of \\(x^3\\) in the expansion of \\((2 - x)^6\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Correct term \\(\\binom{6}{3} 2^3 (-x)^3\\) soi, allow sign error\nA1 \\(-160\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(-160\\)` },
      { q: `A sequence is defined by \\(u_1 = 5\\) and \\(u_{n+1} = 3u_n - 4\\) for \\(n \\geq 1\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(u_3\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(u_2 = 3(5) - 4 = 11\\)\nA1 \\(u_3 = 3(11) - 4 = 29\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(u_3 = 29\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nFind the value of \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{20} (3r + 2)\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Recognise arithmetic series with \\(a = 5\\), \\(l = 62\\), \\(n = 20\\), or use \\(3\\sum r + \\sum 2 = 3(210) + 40\\)\nA1 670 cao\nDR: answer from a calculator list with no method scores 0\nAnswer: 670` },
      { q: `The \\(n\\)th term of a sequence is given by \\(u_n = 5 - \\frac{2}{n}\\).\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is increasing or decreasing. Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `M1 Considers \\(u_{n+1} - u_n = \\frac{2}{n} - \\frac{2}{n+1}\\) or equivalent comparison of consecutive terms\nE1 \\(= \\frac{2}{n(n+1)} \\gt 0\\) for all \\(n \\geq 1\\), so the sequence is increasing\nListing a few terms only: M0 E0\nAnswer: increasing` },
      { q: `Find the binomial expansion of \\((1 + x)^{-2}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^3\\). State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the expansion is valid.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Correct structure, e.g. \\(1 + (-2)x + \\frac{(-2)(-3)}{2!}x^2 + \\frac{(-2)(-3)(-4)}{3!}x^3\\)\nA1 \\(1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3\\)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt 1\\) or \\(-1 \\lt x \\lt 1\\)\nAnswer: \\(1 - 2x + 3x^2 - 4x^3\\), valid for \\(|x| \\lt 1\\)` },
      { q: `The rows of seats in a theatre are numbered 1, 2, 3, …. The numbers of seats in the rows form an arithmetic sequence. Row 5 has 23 seats and row 12 has 51 seats. The theatre has 30 rows.\n\nFind the total number of seats in the theatre.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Forms \\(a + 4d = 23\\) and \\(a + 11d = 51\\) and solves\nA1 \\(d = 4\\), \\(a = 7\\)\nA1 \\(S_{30} = \\frac{30}{2}(2 \\times 7 + 29 \\times 4) = 1950\\) cao\nAnswer: 1950 seats` },
      { q: `In the expansion of \\((1 + ax)^8\\), where \\(a\\) is a constant, the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is 112.\n\nFind the possible values of \\(a\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\binom{8}{2}a^2 x^2\\) soi\nA1 \\(28a^2 = 112\\)\nA1 \\(a = \\pm 2\\) (both values needed)\nAnswer: \\(a = 2\\) or \\(a = -2\\)` },
      { q: `The recurring decimal \\(0.\\dot{3}\\dot{6} = 0.363636…\\) can be written as \\(0.36 + 0.0036 + 0.000036 + …\\)\n\nUse the sum to infinity of a geometric series to show that \\(0.\\dot{3}\\dot{6} = \\frac{4}{11}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `B1 \\(a = 0.36\\) and \\(r = 0.01\\)\nM1 \\(S_\\infty = \\frac{0.36}{1 - 0.01}\\), using \\(|r| \\lt 1\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{0.36}{0.99} = \\frac{36}{99} = \\frac{4}{11}\\) AG, with intermediate fraction seen\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{4}{11}\\) (given)` },
      { q: `A geometric series is \\(81 - 54 + 36 - 24 + …\\)\n\nExplain why the series is convergent and find its sum to infinity.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(r = -\\frac{2}{3}\\)\nE1 \\(|r| = \\frac{2}{3} \\lt 1\\) so the series converges\nB1 \\(S_\\infty = \\frac{81}{1 + \\frac{2}{3}} = 48.6\\) or \\(\\frac{243}{5}\\)\nAnswer: 48.6` },
      { q: `Kai invests £2000 in a savings account that pays 3% compound interest per year. No further money is added or withdrawn.\n\nFind the number of complete years after which the value of the investment first exceeds £3000.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(2000 \\times 1.03^n \\gt 3000\\) or \\(1.03^n \\gt 1.5\\)\nM1 Uses logarithms: \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln 1.5}{\\ln 1.03} = 13.7…\\) (or trial: \\(1.03^{13}\\) gives £2937.07, \\(1.03^{14}\\) gives £3025.18)\nA1 14 years cao\nAnswer: 14 years` },
      { q: `Show that \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (4r + 1) = n(2n + 3)\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 Recognises arithmetic series with \\(a = 5\\), \\(d = 4\\), or uses \\(4\\sum r + \\sum 1\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{n}{2}(10 + 4(n - 1))\\) or \\(4 \\times \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} + n\\)\nA1 \\(= 2n^2 + 3n = n(2n + 3)\\) AG, convincingly shown\nAnswer: given` },
      { q: `A sequence is defined by \\(u_1 = 2\\) and \\(u_{n+1} = \\frac{1}{1 - u_n}\\) for \\(n \\geq 1\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(u_2\\), \\(u_3\\) and \\(u_4\\), and describe the behaviour of the sequence. [2]\n(b) Find \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{100} u_r\\). [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(u_2 = -1\\), \\(u_3 = \\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(u_4 = 2\\)\nB1 Periodic with period 3\n(b)\nM1 Sum of one cycle \\(= 2 - 1 + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}\\) and \\(100 = 33 \\times 3 + 1\\)\nA1 \\(33 \\times \\frac{3}{2} + 2 = 51.5\\) cao\nAnswer: (a) \\(-1, \\frac{1}{2}, 2\\); periodic, period 3 (b) 51.5` },
      { q: `(a) Find the binomial expansion of \\(\\sqrt{1 + 4x}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^3\\). [3]\n(b) State the range of values of \\(x\\) for which this expansion is valid. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(1 + \\frac{1}{2}(4x) + \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2})}{2!}(4x)^2 + \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2})(-\\frac{3}{2})}{3!}(4x)^3\\), allow missing brackets on \\(4x\\) for M1 only\nA1 Two of \\(2x\\), \\(-2x^2\\), \\(4x^3\\) correct\nA1 \\(1 + 2x - 2x^2 + 4x^3\\) fully correct\n(b)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{1}{4}\\)\nAnswer: \\(1 + 2x - 2x^2 + 4x^3\\), \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{1}{4}\\)` },
      { q: `Priya is training for a race. On day 1 she runs 3 km. On each subsequent day she runs 0.4 km further than on the previous day.\n\n(a) Find the first day on which she runs more than 10 km. [2]\n(b) Find the total distance she runs in the first 20 days. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(3 + 0.4(n - 1) \\gt 10\\)\nA1 \\(n - 1 \\gt 17.5\\), so day 19 cao\n(b)\nM1 \\(S_{20} = \\frac{20}{2}(2 \\times 3 + 19 \\times 0.4)\\)\nA1 136 km\nAnswer: (a) day 19 (b) 136 km` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nThe geometric series \\(3 + 6 + 12 + 24 + …\\) has sum to \\(n\\) terms \\(S_n\\).\n\nFind the smallest value of \\(n\\) for which \\(S_n \\gt 1\\,000\\,000\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(S_n = \\frac{3(2^n - 1)}{2 - 1} = 3(2^n - 1)\\)\nM1 \\(3(2^n - 1) \\gt 10^6\\), so \\(2^n \\gt 333\\,334.3…\\)\nM1 Uses logarithms: \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\log 333\\,334.3}{\\log 2} = 18.3…\\)\nA1 \\(n = 19\\) cao (\\(S_{18} = 786\\,429\\), \\(S_{19} = 1\\,572\\,861\\))\nDR: trial of values without the inequality and logarithms: max B1 M0 M0 A0\nAnswer: \\(n = 19\\)` },
      { q: `A sequence is defined by \\(u_1 = 2\\) and \\(u_{n+1} = ku_n + 3\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant. It is given that \\(u_3 = 23\\).\n\nFind the possible values of \\(k\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(u_2 = 2k + 3\\)\nM1 \\(u_3 = k(2k + 3) + 3\\)\nA1 \\(2k^2 + 3k - 20 = 0\\)\nA1 \\((2k - 5)(k + 4) = 0\\), so \\(k = \\frac{5}{2}\\) or \\(k = -4\\)\nAnswer: \\(k = 2.5\\) or \\(k = -4\\)` },
      { q: `Find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=4}^{\\infty} 6\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^r\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Common ratio \\(\\frac{2}{3}\\) identified\nM1 First term \\(6\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^4 = \\frac{32}{27}\\)\nM1 \\(S_\\infty = \\frac{a}{1 - r}\\) with their first term and \\(r = \\frac{2}{3}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{32}{9}\\) cao\nAlternative: \\(\\sum_{r=1}^{\\infty} - \\sum_{r=1}^{3}\\) = \\(12 - \\frac{76}{9}\\): B1 M1 M1 A1\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{32}{9}\\)` },
      { q: `A lake is stocked with fish. The number of fish in the lake at the start of year \\(n\\) is modelled by \\(P_{n+1} = 0.8P_n + 500\\), with \\(P_0 = 1000\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(P_2\\). [2]\n(b) Assuming the sequence converges, find the long-term number of fish predicted by the model. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(P_1 = 0.8(1000) + 500 = 1300\\)\nA1 \\(P_2 = 0.8(1300) + 500 = 1540\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(L = 0.8L + 500\\)\nA1 \\(L = 2500\\) fish\nAnswer: (a) 1540 (b) 2500` },
      { q: `An arithmetic series has first term \\(a\\) and common difference \\(d\\).\n\nProve that the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of the series is \\(\\frac{1}{2}n\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1 \\(S_n = a + (a + d) + … + (a + (n - 1)d)\\)\nM1 Writes the series in reverse: \\(S_n = (a + (n - 1)d) + … + (a + d) + a\\)\nM1 Adds term by term: \\(2S_n = n\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}\\), each pair summing to \\(2a + (n - 1)d\\)\nA1 \\(S_n = \\frac{1}{2}n\\{2a + (n - 1)d\\}\\) AG, with complete argument\nAnswer: proof` },
      { q: `(a) Find the first three terms, in ascending powers of \\(x\\), of the expansion of \\((2 + 3x)^5\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) in the expansion of \\((1 - x)(2 + 3x)^5\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Correct binomial coefficients with powers of 2 and \\(3x\\), e.g. \\(\\binom{5}{2}2^3(3x)^2\\)\nA1 \\(32 + 240x\\)\nA1 \\(+ 720x^2\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(1 \\times 720 + (-1) \\times 240\\) (two products only)\nA1ft 480 (ft their (a))\nAnswer: (a) \\(32 + 240x + 720x^2\\) (b) 480` },
      { q: `The second term of a geometric series is 12 and the sum to infinity of the series is 64.\n\nFind the two possible values of the common ratio and the corresponding values of the first term.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(ar = 12\\) and \\(\\frac{a}{1 - r} = 64\\)\nM1 Eliminates \\(a\\): \\(\\frac{12}{r} = 64(1 - r)\\)\nA1 \\(16r^2 - 16r + 3 = 0\\) or equivalent three-term quadratic\nM1 Solves: \\((4r - 1)(4r - 3) = 0\\)\nA1 \\(r = \\frac{1}{4}, a = 48\\) and \\(r = \\frac{3}{4}, a = 16\\)\nAnswer: \\(r = \\frac{1}{4}\\), \\(a = 48\\); \\(r = \\frac{3}{4}\\), \\(a = 16\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Find the binomial expansion of \\((1 - 2x)^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^2\\). [3]\n(b) By substituting \\(x = 0.01\\) into your expansion, and noting that \\(\\sqrt{0.98} = 0.7\\sqrt{2}\\), find an approximation for \\(\\sqrt{2}\\). Give your answer to 6 decimal places. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(1 + \\frac{1}{2}(-2x) + \\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2})}{2}(-2x)^2\\)\nA1 \\(1 - x\\)\nA1 \\(- \\frac{1}{2}x^2\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\sqrt{0.98} \\approx 1 - 0.01 - 0.00005 = 0.98995\\), then divides by 0.7\nA1 1.414214 cao\nAnswer: (a) \\(1 - x - \\frac{1}{2}x^2\\) (b) 1.414214` },
      { q: `A new car is bought for £18 000. Its value is modelled as decreasing by 15% of its value at the start of each year.\n\n(a) Find the value of the car after 4 years, according to the model. [2]\n(b) Find the number of complete years after which the value of the car first falls below £5000. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(18\\,000 \\times 0.85^4\\)\nA1 £9396.11 (allow £9396)\n(b)\nM1 \\(18\\,000 \\times 0.85^n \\lt 5000\\)\nM1 \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln(5/18)}{\\ln 0.85} = 7.88…\\) (correct handling of inequality with negative log, or trial of \\(n = 7\\) giving £5770 and \\(n = 8\\) giving £4905)\nA1 8 years cao\nAnswer: (a) £9396.11 (b) 8 years` },
      { q: `The sum of the first 10 terms of an arithmetic series is 175 and the sum of the first 20 terms is 650.\n\nFind the 30th term of the series.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(2a + (n - 1)d)\\) for either sum\nA1 \\(2a + 9d = 35\\) and \\(2a + 19d = 65\\)\nM1 Solves simultaneously\nA1 \\(d = 3\\), \\(a = 4\\)\nA1ft \\(u_{30} = 4 + 29 \\times 3 = 91\\)\nAnswer: 91` },
      { q: `Find the binomial expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4 - x}}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^2\\). State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the expansion is valid.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Takes out factor: \\(4^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\left(1 - \\frac{x}{4}\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left(1 - \\frac{x}{4}\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\)\nM1 Expands \\(\\left(1 - \\frac{x}{4}\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}} = 1 + \\frac{x}{8} + \\frac{3x^2}{128}\\), correct structure\nA1 \\(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{x}{16}\\)\nA1 \\(+ \\frac{3x^2}{256}\\)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt 4\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{16}x + \\frac{3}{256}x^2\\), valid for \\(|x| \\lt 4\\)` },
      { q: `In the expansion of \\((1 + ax)^n\\), where \\(n\\) is a positive integer and \\(a\\) is a constant, the coefficient of \\(x\\) is 15 and the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is 90.\n\nFind the values of \\(a\\) and \\(n\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(na = 15\\)\nB1 \\(\\frac{n(n - 1)}{2}a^2 = 90\\)\nM1 Eliminates one unknown, e.g. \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2} \\times \\frac{225}{n^2} = 90\\)\nA1 \\(n = 5\\)\nA1 \\(a = 3\\)\nAnswer: \\(n = 5\\), \\(a = 3\\)` },
      { q: `A graduate is offered two salary schemes, each starting at £24 000 in year 1.\nScheme A: the salary increases by £1200 each year.\nScheme B: the salary increases by 4% of the previous year's salary each year.\n\n(a) Find the total amount earned in the first 10 years under each scheme. [4]\n(b) Find the first year in which the annual salary under Scheme B is greater than the annual salary under Scheme A. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Scheme A: \\(\\frac{10}{2}(2 \\times 24\\,000 + 9 \\times 1200)\\)\nA1 £294 000\nM1 Scheme B: \\(\\frac{24\\,000(1.04^{10} - 1)}{1.04 - 1}\\)\nA1 £288 147 (awrt)\n(b)\nM1 Compares \\(24\\,000 \\times 1.04^{n-1}\\) with \\(24\\,000 + 1200(n - 1)\\) for consecutive years, e.g. year 12: £36 946 vs £37 200\nA1 Year 13 (£38 425 vs £38 400)\nAnswer: (a) A £294 000, B £288 147 (b) year 13` },
      { q: `An arithmetic series has first term 50 and common difference \\(-3\\).\n\n(a) Find the greatest value of the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of the series, as \\(n\\) varies. [3]\n(b) Find the least value of \\(n\\) for which the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms is negative. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(50 - 3(n - 1) \\gt 0\\), so terms are positive up to \\(n = 17\\) (\\(u_{17} = 2\\), \\(u_{18} = -1\\))\nM1 \\(S_{17} = \\frac{17}{2}(50 + 2)\\)\nA1 442\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\frac{n}{2}(100 - 3(n - 1)) \\lt 0\\)\nA1 \\(n(103 - 3n) \\lt 0\\), so \\(n \\gt \\frac{103}{3} = 34.3…\\)\nA1 \\(n = 35\\) cao\nAnswer: (a) 442 (b) 35` },
      { q: `(a) Express \\(\\frac{7 + x}{(1 + x)(2 - x)}\\) in partial fractions. [2]\n(b) Hence find the expansion of \\(\\frac{7 + x}{(1 + x)(2 - x)}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^2\\). State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the expansion is valid. [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(7 + x \\equiv A(2 - x) + B(1 + x)\\) and substitutes or compares coefficients\nA1 \\(\\frac{2}{1 + x} + \\frac{3}{2 - x}\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(2(1 + x)^{-1} = 2 - 2x + 2x^2\\)\nM1 \\(3(2 - x)^{-1} = \\frac{3}{2}\\left(1 - \\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{3}{4}x + \\frac{3}{8}x^2\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{7}{2} - \\frac{5}{4}x + \\frac{19}{8}x^2\\)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt 1\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(\\frac{2}{1+x} + \\frac{3}{2-x}\\) (b) \\(\\frac{7}{2} - \\frac{5}{4}x + \\frac{19}{8}x^2\\), \\(|x| \\lt 1\\)` },
      { q: `A patient takes a 200 mg dose of a drug at the same time every day. The amount of drug in the body is modelled as follows: by the time of the next dose, 30% of the amount present just after the previous dose remains.\n\n(a) Show that the amount of drug in the body immediately after the third dose is 278 mg. [2]\n(b) Find, according to the model, the long-term amount of drug in the body immediately after a dose. Give your answer to 1 decimal place. [2]\n(c) A doctor wants the amount immediately after a dose never to exceed 280 mg. Find the largest daily dose, in mg, that meets this requirement under the model. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(200 + 200(0.3) + 200(0.3)^2\\) or \\(\\frac{200(1 - 0.3^3)}{1 - 0.3}\\)\nA1 \\(200 \\times 1.39 = 278\\) AG\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\frac{200}{1 - 0.3}\\)\nA1 285.7 mg\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\frac{D}{0.7} \\leq 280\\)\nA1 196 mg\nAnswer: (a) given (b) 285.7 mg (c) 196 mg` },
      { q: `A geometric series has first term \\(a\\) and common ratio \\(r\\), where \\(|r| \\lt 1\\). The sum of the first three terms is 19 and the sum to infinity is 27.\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(r\\). [3]\n(b) Find the least value of \\(n\\) for which the difference between the sum to infinity and the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms is less than 0.01. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(\\frac{a}{1 - r} = 27\\) and \\(\\frac{a(1 - r^3)}{1 - r} = 19\\)\nM1 Divides or substitutes to get \\(27(1 - r^3) = 19\\), \\(r^3 = \\frac{8}{27}\\)\nA1 \\(r = \\frac{2}{3}\\), \\(a = 9\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(S_\\infty - S_n = 27\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^n \\lt 0.01\\)\nM1 \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln(0.01/27)}{\\ln(2/3)} = 19.4…\\)\nA1 \\(n = 20\\) cao\nAnswer: (a) \\(a = 9\\), \\(r = \\frac{2}{3}\\) (b) 20` },
      { q: `The first, third and ninth terms of an arithmetic sequence with first term 2 and non-zero common difference \\(d\\) are the first three terms of a geometric sequence.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(d\\) and the common ratio of the geometric sequence. [4]\n(b) Find the sum of the first 10 terms of the geometric sequence. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 Terms \\(2\\), \\(2 + 2d\\), \\(2 + 8d\\)\nM1 \\((2 + 2d)^2 = 2(2 + 8d)\\) or equates ratios\nA1 \\(4d^2 = 8d\\), so \\(d = 2\\) (rejecting \\(d = 0\\))\nA1 Terms 2, 6, 18, so \\(r = 3\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\frac{2(3^{10} - 1)}{3 - 1}\\)\nA1 59 048\nAnswer: (a) \\(d = 2\\), \\(r = 3\\) (b) 59 048` },
      { q: `(a) Find the binomial expansion of \\((1 + 2x)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^2\\). [3]\n(b) In the expansion of \\(\\frac{1 + kx}{\\sqrt{1 + 2x}}\\), where \\(k\\) is a constant, the coefficient of \\(x^2\\) is zero. Find the value of \\(k\\) and the coefficient of \\(x\\). [3]\n(c) State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the expansion is valid. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(1 + (-\\frac{1}{2})(2x) + \\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})(-\\frac{3}{2})}{2}(2x)^2\\)\nA1 \\(1 - x\\)\nA1 \\(+ \\frac{3}{2}x^2\\)\n(b)\nM1 Multiplies \\((1 + kx)(1 - x + \\frac{3}{2}x^2)\\) and collects \\(x^2\\) terms: \\(\\frac{3}{2} - k\\)\nA1 \\(k = \\frac{3}{2}\\)\nA1ft Coefficient of \\(x\\): \\(k - 1 = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\n(c)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(1 - x + \\frac{3}{2}x^2\\) (b) \\(k = \\frac{3}{2}\\), coefficient \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) (c) \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{1}{2}\\)` },
      { q: `Sam pays £100 into a savings account at the start of every month. At the end of each month, interest of 0.5% of the amount in the account is added.\n\n(a) Show that the amount in the account at the end of 12 months is £1239.72, to the nearest penny. [2]\n(b) Show that the amount in the account at the end of \\(n\\) months is \\(£20\\,100(1.005^n - 1)\\). [2]\n(c) Find the number of months after which the amount in the account first exceeds £10 000. [2]\n(d) State one limitation of this model. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(100(1.005 + 1.005^2 + … + 1.005^{12})\\) or \\(\\frac{100 \\times 1.005(1.005^{12} - 1)}{0.005}\\)\nA1 £1239.72 AG\n(b)\nM1 Geometric series with \\(a = 100.5\\), \\(r = 1.005\\): \\(\\frac{100.5(1.005^n - 1)}{0.005}\\)\nA1 \\(= 20\\,100(1.005^n - 1)\\) AG\n(c)\nM1 \\(1.005^n \\gt 1 + \\frac{10\\,000}{20\\,100}\\), \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln 1.4975…}{\\ln 1.005} = 80.96…\\)\nA1 81 months\n(d)\nE1 e.g. the interest rate is unlikely to stay at 0.5% per month for this long; Sam may miss or change payments\nAnswer: (c) 81 months` },
      { q: `The amount of chlorine, in grams, in a swimming pool at the start of day \\(n\\) is denoted by \\(u_n\\). It is modelled by \\(u_{n+1} = pu_n + q\\), where \\(p\\) and \\(q\\) are constants. It is given that \\(u_1 = 4\\), \\(u_2 = 7\\) and \\(u_3 = 8.5\\). (data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). [3]\n(b) Describe how the amount of chlorine changes in the long term, giving the limiting value. [2]\n(c) Verify that \\(u_n = 10 - 12(0.5)^n\\) satisfies the recurrence relation and the condition \\(u_1 = 4\\). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(4p + q = 7\\) and \\(7p + q = 8.5\\)\nA1 \\(p = 0.5\\)\nA1 \\(q = 5\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(L = 0.5L + 5\\)\nA1 Increases and converges to 10 g (approaches 10 from below)\n(c)\nB1 \\(u_1 = 10 - 12(0.5) = 4\\)\nB1 \\(0.5(10 - 12(0.5)^n) + 5 = 10 - 6(0.5)^n = 10 - 12(0.5)^{n+1}\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(p = 0.5\\), \\(q = 5\\) (b) increases towards 10 g` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (2^r + 3r) = 2^{n+1} - 2 + \\frac{3}{2}n(n + 1)\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the least value of \\(n\\) for which \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=1}^{n} (2^r + 3r) \\gt 5000\\). [2]\n(c) Find the value of \\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{r=11}^{20} (2^r + 3r)\\). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Splits into \\(\\sum 2^r + 3\\sum r\\)\nA1 \\(\\sum_{r=1}^{n} 2^r = \\frac{2(2^n - 1)}{2 - 1} = 2^{n+1} - 2\\)\nA1 \\(3 \\times \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) and result AG\n(b)\nM1 Evaluates for consecutive values: \\(n = 11\\) gives 4292, \\(n = 12\\) gives 8424\nA1 \\(n = 12\\)\n(c)\nM1 Uses \\(S_{20} - S_{10}\\)\nA1 \\(2\\,097\\,780 - 2211 = 2\\,095\\,569\\)\nAnswer: (b) 12 (c) 2 095 569` },
      { q: `The population of a town was 40 000 at the start of 2020. Two models are proposed for the population \\(n\\) years after the start of 2020.\nModel A: the population increases by 600 people each year.\nModel B: the population increases by 1.4% each year.\n\n(a) Find the year in which Model B first predicts a population greater than 60 000. [3]\n(b) Find the first year in which Model B predicts a greater population than Model A. [3]\n(c) The population at the start of 2030 was 45 900. (data written for this practice question) Comment on the suitability of the two models for predicting the population in 2060. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(40\\,000 \\times 1.014^n \\gt 60\\,000\\)\nM1 \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln 1.5}{\\ln 1.014} = 29.2…\\)\nA1 \\(n = 30\\), so 2050\n(b)\nM1 Compares \\(40\\,000 \\times 1.014^n\\) with \\(40\\,000 + 600n\\)\nM1 Evaluates at consecutive \\(n\\): \\(n = 10\\): 45 966 vs 46 000; \\(n = 11\\): 46 610 vs 46 600\nA1 2031\n(c)\nE1 Both models give predictions close to 45 900 in 2030 (A: 46 000, B: 45 966), so the data cannot distinguish between them\nE1 The models diverge greatly by 2060, so the 2060 prediction is unreliable; extrapolating 40 years ahead assumes the growth pattern continues\nAnswer: (a) 2050 (b) 2031` },
      { q: `(a) Find the binomial expansion of \\((8 + 3x)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\) in ascending powers of \\(x\\), up to and including the term in \\(x^2\\). [4]\n(b) State the set of values of \\(x\\) for which this expansion is valid. [1]\n(c) Use your expansion with \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) to find an estimate for \\(\\sqrt[3]{9}\\). Give your answer to 5 significant figures, and find the percentage error in your estimate. [2]\n(d) Explain why the expansion should not be used with \\(x = \\frac{8}{3}\\) to estimate \\(\\sqrt[3]{16}\\). [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(8^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\left(1 + \\frac{3x}{8}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}} = 2\\left(1 + \\frac{3x}{8}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\)\nM1 \\(1 + \\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\frac{3x}{8}\\right) + \\frac{\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{2}{3})}{2}\\left(\\frac{3x}{8}\\right)^2\\)\nA1 \\(2 + \\frac{1}{4}x\\)\nA1 \\(- \\frac{1}{32}x^2\\)\n(b)\nB1 \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{8}{3}\\)\n(c)\nB1 \\(2 + \\frac{1}{12} - \\frac{1}{288} = 2.0799\\)\nB1 Percentage error 0.0107% (awrt 0.011%), using \\(\\sqrt[3]{9} = 2.080084…\\)\n(d)\nE1 \\(x = \\frac{8}{3}\\) is not in the interval of validity (\\(|x| \\lt \\frac{8}{3}\\) is strict), so the series does not give a reliable estimate\nAnswer: (a) \\(2 + \\frac{1}{4}x - \\frac{1}{32}x^2\\) (b) \\(|x| \\lt \\frac{8}{3}\\) (c) 2.0799, 0.0107%` },
      { q: `At the start of each year, Leah pays £1000 into an account. At the end of each year, interest of 3% of the amount in the account is added.\n\n(a) Show that the amount in the account at the end of the third year is £3183.63, to the nearest penny. [2]\n(b) Show that the amount in the account at the end of the \\(n\\)th year is \\(£\\frac{103\\,000}{3}(1.03^n - 1)\\). [3]\n(c) Find the number of complete years after which the amount in the account first exceeds £20 000. [3]\n(d) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(1000 \\times 1.03^3 + 1000 \\times 1.03^2 + 1000 \\times 1.03\\)\nA1 £3183.63 AG\n(b)\nM1 Recognises a geometric series with \\(a = 1030\\), \\(r = 1.03\\), \\(n\\) terms\nM1 \\(\\frac{1030(1.03^n - 1)}{1.03 - 1}\\)\nA1 \\(= \\frac{103\\,000}{3}(1.03^n - 1)\\) AG\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\frac{103\\,000}{3}(1.03^n - 1) \\gt 20\\,000\\), so \\(1.03^n \\gt 1.5825…\\)\nM1 \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln 1.5825…}{\\ln 1.03} = 15.5…\\)\nA1 16 years cao\n(d)\nE1 e.g. the interest rate may change; Leah may not be able to pay £1000 every year; tax on interest is ignored\nAnswer: (c) 16 years` },
      { q: `A ball is dropped from a height of 8 m above horizontal ground. After each bounce, it rises to a maximum height that is \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) of the maximum height before that bounce. The ball moves only vertically.\n\n(a) Find the maximum height reached after the third bounce. [1]\n(b) Show that the total distance travelled by the ball from when it is dropped to when it hits the ground for the fifth time is 40.8125 m. [3]\n(c) Find the total distance travelled by the ball according to the model. [3]\n(d) Find the number of the first bounce after which the maximum height is less than 1 cm. [2]\n(e) Give one reason why the model is not realistic. [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(8 \\times 0.75^3 = 3.375\\) m\n(b)\nM1 Heights after bounces 1 to 4: 6, 4.5, 3.375, 2.53125, each travelled up and down\nM1 \\(8 + 2 \\times \\frac{6(1 - 0.75^4)}{1 - 0.75}\\) or \\(8 + 2(16.40625)\\)\nA1 40.8125 m AG\n(c)\nM1 \\(S_\\infty\\) of upward distances \\(= \\frac{6}{1 - 0.75} = 24\\)\nM1 \\(8 + 2 \\times 24\\)\nA1 56 m\n(d)\nM1 \\(8 \\times 0.75^n \\lt 0.01\\), \\(n \\gt \\frac{\\ln 0.00125}{\\ln 0.75} = 23.2…\\)\nA1 24th bounce\n(e)\nE1 e.g. the model predicts infinitely many bounces, whereas a real ball stops bouncing; air resistance or the size of the ball is ignored\nAnswer: (a) 3.375 m (c) 56 m (d) 24th` }
    ]
  },
  '1.5': {
    name: 'Trigonometry',
    questions: [
      { q: `Given that \\(\\theta\\) is small, use small-angle approximations to show that \\(\\cos\\theta + \\sin 2\\theta \\approx 1 + 2\\theta - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\cos\\theta \\approx 1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2\\) and \\(\\sin 2\\theta \\approx 2\\theta\\)\nA1 \\(1 + 2\\theta - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2\\) AG, convincingly shown\nNote: \\(\\sin 2\\theta \\approx \\theta\\) or \\(\\cos\\theta \\approx 1 - \\theta^2\\) is M0.` },
      { q: `A sector of a circle has radius 6 cm and angle 1.2 radians.\n\nFind the perimeter of the sector.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Arc length \\(= r\\theta = 6 \\times 1.2 = 7.2\\) cm\nA1 Perimeter \\(= 7.2 + 2 \\times 6 = 19.2\\) cm cao` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nFind the exact value of \\(\\sec\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 DR: \\(\\cos\\frac{5\\pi}{6} = -\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{6} = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) seen\nA1 \\(\\sec\\frac{5\\pi}{6} = -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\) or \\(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\)\nA decimal answer only scores M0 A0 (DR).` },
      { q: `The curve C has equation \\(y = 3\\sin 2x + 1\\), where \\(x\\) is in radians.\n\nState\n(a) the maximum value of \\(y\\), [1]\n(b) the period of C. [1]`, marks: 2, command: 'State', markScheme: `(a) B1 Maximum \\(y = 4\\)\n(b) B1 Period \\(= \\pi\\) (accept 180° only if units stated)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(\\tan 2x = \\sqrt{3}\\) for \\(0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 \\(2x = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) seen as principal value, and further values found by adding multiples of \\(\\pi\\) to \\(2x\\)\nA1 Two correct values of \\(x\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{2\\pi}{3}, \\frac{7\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) and no others in range\nDecimals only (0.524, 2.09, 3.67, 5.24) without exact working: max M1 A1 A0.` },
      { q: `In triangle ABC, AB = 8 cm, BC = 6 cm and angle BAC = 40°.\n\nFind the two possible values of angle ACB, giving each to 1 decimal place.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Sine rule: \\(\\frac{\\sin C}{8} = \\frac{\\sin 40^\\circ}{6}\\)\nA1 \\(\\sin C = 0.857\\ldots\\), \\(C = 59.0^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(C = 180^\\circ - 59.0^\\circ = 121.0^\\circ\\)\nFinal answer: 59.0° or 121.0°` },
      { q: `Three villages P, Q and R are joined by straight roads. PQ = 5 km, QR = 7 km and PR = 9 km.\n\nFind the largest angle of triangle PQR.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Identifies the largest angle as angle PQR (opposite the 9 km side)\nM1 \\(\\cos Q = \\frac{5^2 + 7^2 - 9^2}{2 \\times 5 \\times 7}\\) \\(= -0.1\\)\nA1 Angle PQR = 95.7° (3 sf) cao` },
      { q: `Triangle XYZ has XY = 7 cm, XZ = 9 cm and an area of 20 cm². Angle YXZ is obtuse.\n\nFind angle YXZ.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 7 \\times 9 \\sin X = 20\\)\nA1 \\(\\sin X = \\frac{40}{63}\\) (= 0.6349…)\nA1 Obtuse angle: \\(X = 180^\\circ - 39.4^\\circ = 140.6^\\circ\\)\nAnswer 39.4° only: M1 A1 A0.` },
      { q: `Show that \\(\\tan(\\arcsin x) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\) for \\(0 \\lt x \\lt 1\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `B1 Lets \\(\\theta = \\arcsin x\\), so \\(\\sin\\theta = x\\) with \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\nM1 \\(\\cos\\theta = \\sqrt{1 - x^2}\\) using \\(\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta = 1\\) or a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse 1\nA1 \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 - x^2}}\\) AG, with the positive root justified by the range of \\(\\theta\\)` },
      { q: `Given that \\(\\theta\\) is small, use small-angle approximations to show that\n\\(\\frac{1 - \\cos 4\\theta}{\\theta \\sin 2\\theta} \\approx 4\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\cos 4\\theta \\approx 1 - \\frac{(4\\theta)^2}{2} = 1 - 8\\theta^2\\)\nM1 \\(\\sin 2\\theta \\approx 2\\theta\\) used in the denominator\nA1 \\(\\frac{8\\theta^2}{2\\theta^2} = 4\\) AG` },
      { q: `Prove that \\(\\frac{\\sin 2x}{1 + \\cos 2x} \\equiv \\tan x\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Prove that', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\sin 2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x\\) used\nM1 \\(1 + \\cos 2x = 2\\cos^2 x\\) (from \\(\\cos 2x = 2\\cos^2 x - 1\\))\nA1 \\(\\frac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{2\\cos^2 x} = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\tan x\\) AG, fully correct` },
      { q: `A decorative glass tile is in the shape of a segment of a circle. The segment is cut off by a chord AB of a circle with centre O and radius 10 cm, where angle AOB = 0.8 radians.\n\nFind the area of the tile, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Sector area \\(= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^2 \\times 0.8 = 40\\)\nM1 Triangle area \\(= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10^2 \\sin 0.8 = 35.87\\ldots\\) (calculator in radians)\nM1 Subtracts triangle from sector\nA1 4.13 cm² cao` },
      { q: `(a) Express \\(3\\cos\\theta + 4\\sin\\theta\\) in the form \\(R\\cos(\\theta - \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0^\\circ \\lt \\alpha \\lt 90^\\circ\\). Give \\(\\alpha\\) to 2 decimal places. [3]\n(b) Hence state the maximum value of \\(3\\cos\\theta + 4\\sin\\theta\\). [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(R = 5\\)\nM1 \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{4}{3}\\) (from \\(R\\cos\\alpha = 3\\), \\(R\\sin\\alpha = 4\\))\nA1 \\(\\alpha = 53.13^\\circ\\); \\(5\\cos(\\theta - 53.13^\\circ)\\)\n(b) B1ft Maximum value 5` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(2\\cos^2 x + 3\\sin x = 3\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 360^\\circ\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\cos^2 x = 1 - \\sin^2 x\\)\nA1 \\(2\\sin^2 x - 3\\sin x + 1 = 0\\)\nM1 Solves: \\((2\\sin x - 1)(\\sin x - 1) = 0\\), \\(\\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}\\) or 1\nA1 \\(x = 30^\\circ, 90^\\circ, 150^\\circ\\) and no others` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(\\sec^2 x - 3\\tan x + 1 = 0\\) for \\(-180^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 180^\\circ\\). Give your answers to 1 decimal place where appropriate.`, marks: 4, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\sec^2 x = 1 + \\tan^2 x\\)\nA1 \\(\\tan^2 x - 3\\tan x + 2 = 0\\), so \\(\\tan x = 1\\) or \\(\\tan x = 2\\)\nA1 \\(x = 45^\\circ, -135^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(x = 63.4^\\circ, -116.6^\\circ\\)\nExtra values in range: −1 from final A mark.` },
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nUse an appropriate compound-angle formula to show that \\(\\tan 15^\\circ = 2 - \\sqrt{3}\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\tan(45^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan 45^\\circ - \\tan 30^\\circ}{1 + \\tan 45^\\circ\\tan 30^\\circ}\\)\nA1 \\(= \\frac{1 - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}}{1 + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3} - 1}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}\\)\nM1 Multiplies numerator and denominator by \\(\\sqrt{3} - 1\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3} + 1}{2} = 2 - \\sqrt{3}\\) AG, all steps shown` },
      { q: `Describe a sequence of transformations that maps the curve \\(y = \\sin x\\) onto the curve \\(y = 3\\sin 2x + 1\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `B1 Stretch parallel to the x-axis, scale factor \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\)\nB1 Stretch parallel to the y-axis, scale factor 3\nB1 Translation by vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\)\nB1 Order correct: the translation comes after the y-stretch (the x-stretch may be at any stage)\nWords such as "move" or "enlarge" are not accepted for full marks.` },
      { q: `The depth of water, D metres, at the entrance to a harbour is modelled by\n\\(D = 5 + 2.4\\sin(30t)^\\circ\\),\nwhere t is the time in hours after midnight. (Model written for this practice question.)\n\n(a) Find the maximum depth predicted by the model and the first time after midnight at which it occurs. [2]\n(b) Find the two times between midnight and 06:00 at which the depth is 6 metres. Give your answers to the nearest minute. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Maximum depth 7.4 m\nB1 When \\(30t = 90\\), \\(t = 3\\): 03:00\n(b) M1 \\(\\sin(30t)^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2.4}\\) giving \\(30t = 24.62^\\circ\\) or \\(155.38^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(t = 0.821\\) and \\(t = 5.179\\): 00:49 and 05:11` },
      { q: `A car windscreen wiper consists of an arm OB of length 45 cm which rotates about the fixed point O. The rubber blade lies along OB from the point 15 cm from O to B. The arm turns through an angle of 110°.\n\nFind the area of windscreen cleaned by the blade, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(110^\\circ = \\frac{11\\pi}{18}\\) radians (1.920)\nM1 Area of a sector \\(\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta\\) used with r = 45 or r = 15\nM1 Subtracts: \\(\\frac{1}{2}(45^2 - 15^2) \\times \\frac{11\\pi}{18}\\)\nA1 1730 cm² (1727.9…)\nDegree formula \\(\\frac{110}{360}\\pi r^2\\) is acceptable for B1 M1.` },
      { q: `(a) Prove that \\(\\frac{1 + \\sec\\theta}{\\tan\\theta + \\sin\\theta} \\equiv \\csc\\theta\\). [3]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(\\frac{1 + \\sec\\theta}{\\tan\\theta + \\sin\\theta} = 3\\) for \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\\). Give your answers to 3 significant figures. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove that', markScheme: `(a) M1 Writes in terms of \\(\\sin\\theta\\) and \\(\\cos\\theta\\): \\(\\frac{1 + \\frac{1}{\\cos\\theta}}{\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} + \\sin\\theta}\\)\nM1 Multiplies through by \\(\\cos\\theta\\): \\(\\frac{\\cos\\theta + 1}{\\sin\\theta(1 + \\cos\\theta)}\\)\nA1 Cancels to \\(\\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta} = \\csc\\theta\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 0.340, 2.80\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(2\\cot^2\\theta - 5\\csc\\theta = 1\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq \\theta \\leq 360^\\circ\\). Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\cot^2\\theta = \\csc^2\\theta - 1\\)\nA1 \\(2\\csc^2\\theta - 5\\csc\\theta - 3 = 0\\)\nM1 \\((2\\csc\\theta + 1)(\\csc\\theta - 3) = 0\\)\nE1 \\(\\csc\\theta = -\\frac{1}{2}\\) rejected since \\(|\\csc\\theta| \\geq 1\\) (equivalently \\(\\sin\\theta = -2\\) impossible)\nA1 \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}\\): \\(\\theta = 19.5^\\circ, 160.5^\\circ\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(\\sin 2x = \\tan x\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq x \\leq 360^\\circ\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1 \\(2\\sin x\\cos x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\)\nM1 Rearranges to \\(\\sin x(2\\cos^2 x - 1) = 0\\) (not dividing by \\(\\sin x\\))\nA1 \\(\\sin x = 0\\): \\(x = 0^\\circ, 180^\\circ, 360^\\circ\\)\nM1 \\(\\cos x = \\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\)\nA1 \\(x = 45^\\circ, 135^\\circ, 225^\\circ, 315^\\circ\\)\nCancelling \\(\\sin x\\) loses the first A1.` },
      { q: `(a) Given that \\(\\theta\\) is small, use small-angle approximations to show that\n\\(\\frac{\\cos\\theta - 1}{\\sin 2\\theta\\tan 3\\theta} \\approx -\\frac{1}{12}\\). [3]\n(b) Find the percentage error when this approximation is used for \\(\\theta = 0.1\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\cos\\theta - 1 \\approx -\\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2\\)\nM1 \\(\\sin 2\\theta\\tan 3\\theta \\approx 2\\theta \\times 3\\theta = 6\\theta^2\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2}{6\\theta^2} = -\\frac{1}{12}\\) AG\n(b) M1 True value at \\(\\theta = 0.1\\): \\(-0.08129\\ldots\\) (radians)\nA1 Percentage error \\(= \\frac{0.08333 - 0.08129}{0.08129} \\times 100 = 2.5\\%\\) (accept 2.4% to 2.6%)` },
      { q: `A boat leaves harbour A and sails 12 km on a bearing of 060° to a buoy B. It then sails 8 km on a bearing of 170° to a lighthouse C.\n\n(a) Find the distance AC. [3]\n(b) Find the bearing of C from A. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Angle ABC = 70°\nM1 \\(AC^2 = 12^2 + 8^2 - 2 \\times 12 \\times 8\\cos 70^\\circ\\)\nA1 AC = 11.9 km (11.93…)\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{\\sin BAC}{8} = \\frac{\\sin 70^\\circ}{11.93}\\), giving angle BAC = 39.06°\nA1ft Bearing \\(60^\\circ + 39.1^\\circ = 099^\\circ\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Sketch the graph of \\(y = \\arccos x\\), stating the coordinates of the end-points of the graph. [3]\n(b) In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\nSolve the equation \\(\\arccos(2x - 1) = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1 Decreasing curve defined only for \\(-1 \\leq x \\leq 1\\)\nB1 End-points \\((-1, \\pi)\\) and \\((1, 0)\\)\nB1 Passes through \\((0, \\frac{\\pi}{2})\\) with correct shape (steep at the ends, point of inflection at the y-intercept)\n(b) M1 \\(2x - 1 = \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(x = \\frac{3}{4}\\)` },
      { q: `A flower bed is in the shape of a sector of a circle with radius r metres and angle \\(\\theta\\) radians. The perimeter of the flower bed is 20 m and its area is 16 m².\n\nFind the values of r and \\(\\theta\\). Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(2r + r\\theta = 20\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta = 16\\)\nM1 Eliminates \\(\\theta\\): \\(r\\theta = \\frac{32}{r}\\), so \\(2r + \\frac{32}{r} = 20\\)\nA1 \\(r^2 - 10r + 16 = 0\\), so r = 2 or r = 8\nE1 r = 2 gives \\(\\theta = 8\\), which exceeds \\(2\\pi\\), so it is rejected\nA1 r = 8, \\(\\theta = 0.5\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Express \\(2\\sin\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta\\) in the form \\(R\\sin(\\theta - \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0^\\circ \\lt \\alpha \\lt 90^\\circ\\). Give the exact value of R and the value of \\(\\alpha\\) to 2 decimal places. [3]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(2\\sin\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta = 1.5\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq \\theta \\leq 360^\\circ\\). [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(R = \\sqrt{13}\\)\nM1 \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{3}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(\\alpha = 56.31^\\circ\\)\n(b) M1 \\(\\sin(\\theta - 56.31^\\circ) = \\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{13}}\\), giving \\(\\theta - 56.31^\\circ = 24.58^\\circ\\)\nM1 Second value \\(\\theta - 56.31^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 24.58^\\circ = 155.42^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 80.9^\\circ, 211.7^\\circ\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Prove that \\(\\cos 3\\theta \\equiv 4\\cos^3\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta\\). [4]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(8\\cos^3\\theta - 6\\cos\\theta = 1\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ\\). [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\cos(2\\theta + \\theta) = \\cos 2\\theta\\cos\\theta - \\sin 2\\theta\\sin\\theta\\)\nM1 Substitutes \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1\\) and \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\)\nM1 Uses \\(\\sin^2\\theta = 1 - \\cos^2\\theta\\): \\(2\\cos^3\\theta - \\cos\\theta - 2\\cos\\theta(1 - \\cos^2\\theta)\\)\nA1 \\(4\\cos^3\\theta - 3\\cos\\theta\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\(2\\cos 3\\theta = 1\\), so \\(\\cos 3\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\), \\(3\\theta = 60^\\circ, 300^\\circ, 420^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 20^\\circ, 100^\\circ, 140^\\circ\\)` },
      { q: `A Ferris wheel has diameter 32 m. Its centre is 18 m above the ground and it rotates at a constant speed, making one revolution every 30 minutes. The height, h metres, of a passenger capsule above the ground t minutes after it passes the lowest point is modelled by\n\\(h = 18 - 16\\cos(12t)^\\circ\\).\n\n(a) Find the height of the capsule 5 minutes after it passes the lowest point. [1]\n(b) Find, to the nearest tenth of a minute, the length of time during one revolution for which the capsule is at least 30 m above the ground. [4]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(18 - 16\\cos 60^\\circ = 10\\) m\n(b) M1 \\(18 - 16\\cos(12t)^\\circ \\geq 30\\), so \\(\\cos(12t)^\\circ \\leq -0.75\\)\nA1 \\(12t = 138.59^\\circ\\), so \\(t = 11.55\\)\nM1 Second value \\(12t = 360^\\circ - 138.59^\\circ\\), so \\(t = 18.45\\)\nA1 Time \\(= 18.45 - 11.55 = 6.9\\) minutes\n(c) E1 e.g. the wheel may not rotate at constant speed (it stops to load passengers); the capsule height is measured to its centre, not the passenger's position` },
      { q: `A farmer's field is modelled as a triangle ABC with AB = 120 m, AC = 150 m and BC = 200 m.\n\n(a) Find angle BAC. [3]\n(b) Find the area of the field, giving your answer to 3 significant figures. [2]\n(c) State one assumption made in modelling the field as a triangle. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Cosine rule \\(\\cos A = \\frac{120^2 + 150^2 - 200^2}{2 \\times 120 \\times 150}\\)\nA1 \\(\\cos A = -0.0861\\ldots\\)\nA1 Angle BAC = 94.9°\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 120 \\times 150 \\sin 94.94^\\circ\\)\nA1 8970 m² (8966.6…)\n(c) E1 e.g. the boundaries are straight lines; the field is flat (horizontal)` },
      { q: `(a) Prove that \\(\\tan\\theta + \\cot\\theta \\equiv 2\\csc 2\\theta\\). [3]\n(b) In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\nHence solve the equation \\(\\tan\\theta + \\cot\\theta = 4\\) for \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\pi\\). [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Prove that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta} + \\frac{\\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta} = \\frac{\\sin^2\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}\\)\nM1 \\(= \\frac{1}{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta}\\) and uses \\(\\sin 2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta\\)\nA1 \\(= \\frac{2}{\\sin 2\\theta} = 2\\csc 2\\theta\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\(\\csc 2\\theta = 2\\), so \\(\\sin 2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nM1 \\(2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}, \\frac{5\\pi}{6}\\) (range \\(0 \\lt 2\\theta \\lt 2\\pi\\))\nA1 \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\)` },
      { q: `A logo is based on a sector OAB of a circle with centre O and radius 9 cm, where angle AOB = 1 radian. The tangent to the circle at A meets the line OB extended at the point T.\n\nThe shaded part of the logo is the region bounded by the line segments AT and BT and the arc AB.\n\n(a) Find the area of the shaded region. [3]\n(b) Find the perimeter of the shaded region. [3]\n\nGive your answers to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Angle OAT = \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), so \\(AT = 9\\tan 1\\) (14.02)\nM1 Area of triangle OAT − area of sector: \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times 9\\tan 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9^2 \\times 1\\)\nA1 22.6 cm² (22.575…)\n(b) M1 \\(OT = \\frac{9}{\\cos 1}\\) (16.66), so \\(BT = OT - 9\\) (7.66)\nB1 Arc AB = 9 cm\nA1 Perimeter \\(= 14.02 + 7.66 + 9 = 30.7\\) cm` },
      { q: `The depth, D metres, of water in a harbour t hours after midnight is modelled by\n\\(D = 14 + 5\\cos(30t)^\\circ - 12\\sin(30t)^\\circ\\).\n(Model written for this practice question.)\n\n(a) Express \\(5\\cos x - 12\\sin x\\) in the form \\(R\\cos(x + \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0^\\circ \\lt \\alpha \\lt 90^\\circ\\). Give \\(\\alpha\\) to 2 decimal places. [3]\n(b) Hence find the maximum depth of water and the time, to the nearest minute, at which it first occurs after midnight. [2]\n(c) Find the first time after midnight at which the depth of water is 20 m. Give your answer to the nearest minute. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(R = 13\\)\nM1 \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{12}{5}\\)\nA1 \\(\\alpha = 67.38^\\circ\\)\n(b) B1ft Maximum depth 27 m\nB1 \\(30t + 67.38 = 360\\), \\(t = 9.754\\): 09:45\n(c) M1 \\(\\cos(30t + 67.38)^\\circ = \\frac{6}{13}\\), first valid value \\(30t + 67.38 = 297.49\\)\nA1 \\(t = 7.670\\): 07:40\nNote: \\(30t + 67.38 = 62.51\\) gives negative t and is rejected.` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(2\\sin(\\theta + 30^\\circ) \\equiv \\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta\\). [2]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(2\\sin(\\theta + 30^\\circ) = 3\\cos\\theta\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq \\theta \\leq 360^\\circ\\). [3]\n(c) Hence solve the equation \\(2\\sin(2\\theta + 30^\\circ) = 3\\cos 2\\theta\\) for \\(0^\\circ \\leq \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ\\). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(2(\\sin\\theta\\cos 30^\\circ + \\cos\\theta\\sin 30^\\circ)\\)\nA1 \\(2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\sin\\theta + \\frac{1}{2}\\cos\\theta\\right) = \\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\(\\sqrt{3}\\sin\\theta = 2\\cos\\theta\\)\nA1 \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 49.1^\\circ, 229.1^\\circ\\)\n(c) M1 \\(2\\theta = 49.1^\\circ, 229.1^\\circ\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 24.6^\\circ, 114.6^\\circ\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Given that \\(\\theta\\) is small, use small-angle approximations and a binomial expansion to show that\n\\(\\frac{4\\cos 3\\theta - 2 + 5\\sin\\theta}{1 - \\sin 2\\theta} \\approx 2 + 9\\theta\\). [5]\n(b) Use this approximation to estimate the value of the expression when \\(\\theta = 0.05\\), and comment on its accuracy. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\cos 3\\theta \\approx 1 - \\frac{9}{2}\\theta^2\\) and \\(\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta\\)\nA1 Numerator \\(\\approx 2 + 5\\theta - 18\\theta^2\\)\nM1 Denominator \\(\\approx 1 - 2\\theta\\); writes \\((1 - 2\\theta)^{-1} \\approx 1 + 2\\theta + 4\\theta^2\\)\nM1 Multiplies out, collecting terms up to \\(\\theta^2\\)\nA1 \\(2 + 9\\theta + (8 + 10 - 18)\\theta^2 = 2 + 9\\theta\\) AG\n(b) B1 Estimate \\(2 + 9 \\times 0.05 = 2.45\\)\nE1 True value 2.4495… so the estimate is very accurate (error about 0.02%)` },
      { q: `A surveyor stands at a point A on level ground and measures the angle of elevation of the top T of a vertical tower as 32°. She walks 50 m directly towards the base of the tower to a point B, where the angle of elevation of T is 47°. Her measuring instrument is at ground level.\n\n(a) Find angle ATB. [1]\n(b) Show that BT = 102.4 m, correct to 1 decimal place. [2]\n(c) Find the height of the tower. [2]\n(d) In fact the instrument is 1.6 m above the ground at A and at B, and the angles were measured from that height. Find the revised height of the tower. [1]\n(e) State one other assumption made in the model. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Angle ATB = 47° − 32° = 15°\n(b) M1 Sine rule \\(\\frac{BT}{\\sin 32^\\circ} = \\frac{50}{\\sin 15^\\circ}\\)\nA1 BT = 102.37… = 102.4 m AG\n(c) M1 Height \\(= BT\\sin 47^\\circ\\)\nA1 74.9 m\n(d) B1ft 76.5 m\n(e) E1 e.g. the ground is horizontal; A, B and the base of the tower are in a straight line; the tower is exactly vertical` },
      { q: `(a) Express \\(3\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta\\) in the form \\(R\\sin(\\theta + \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0 \\lt \\alpha \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). Give the exact value of R and the value of \\(\\alpha\\) to 4 significant figures. [3]\n(b) Find the exact maximum value of \\(\\frac{1}{5 + 3\\sin\\theta + 2\\cos\\theta}\\). [2]\n(c) Solve the equation \\(3\\sin 2x + 2\\cos 2x = 1\\) for \\(0 \\leq x \\leq \\pi\\). Give your answers to 3 significant figures. [3]`, marks: 8, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(R = \\sqrt{13}\\)\nM1 \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{2}{3}\\)\nA1 \\(\\alpha = 0.5880\\)\n(b) M1 Minimum of denominator is \\(5 - \\sqrt{13}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{1}{5 - \\sqrt{13}}\\) or \\(\\frac{5 + \\sqrt{13}}{12}\\)\n(c) M1 \\(\\sin(2x + 0.5880) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\), principal value 0.2810\nM1 Uses range \\(0.588 \\leq 2x + \\alpha \\leq 2\\pi + 0.588\\): \\(2x + \\alpha = \\pi - 0.2810\\) or \\(2\\pi + 0.2810\\)\nA1 \\(x = 1.14, 2.99\\)` },
      { q: `The number of hours of daylight, H, in a town on day t of the year is modelled by\n\\(H = 12.3 + 4.4\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi(t - 80)}{365}\\right)\\),\nwhere t = 1 is 1 January. (Model written for this practice question.)\n\n(a) Find the maximum number of hours of daylight predicted by the model and the value of t at which it occurs. [2]\n(b) Find the number of days in the year for which the model predicts more than 15 hours of daylight. [4]\n(c) Comment on two features of the model, referring to its period and its use in a leap year. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Maximum H = 16.7 hours\nB1 \\(\\frac{2\\pi(t - 80)}{365} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), so t = 171 (171.25)\n(b) M1 \\(\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi(t - 80)}{365}\\right) \\gt \\frac{2.7}{4.4}\\)\nA1 Principal value 0.6607\nM1 Second value \\(\\pi - 0.6607 = 2.4809\\), and converts both: t = 118.4 and t = 224.1\nA1 Days 119 to 224: 106 days (accept 105 or 106)\n(c) E1 Period of the model is 365 days, matching one year, so the pattern repeats each year appropriately\nE1 In a leap year (366 days) the model drifts slightly / would need the period adjusting; or daylight also depends on factors not modelled, e.g. it is a symmetric sine curve` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(\\cos 4\\theta \\equiv 8\\cos^4\\theta - 8\\cos^2\\theta + 1\\). [4]\n(b) In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\nHence show that \\(\\cos\\frac{\\pi}{8} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\). [3]\n(c) Hence solve the equation \\(16\\cos^4\\theta - 16\\cos^2\\theta + 1 = 0\\) for \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), giving your answers in exact form. [2]`, marks: 9, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\cos 4\\theta = 2\\cos^2 2\\theta - 1\\)\nM1 Substitutes \\(\\cos 2\\theta = 2\\cos^2\\theta - 1\\)\nA1 \\(2(4\\cos^4\\theta - 4\\cos^2\\theta + 1) - 1\\)\nA1 \\(8\\cos^4\\theta - 8\\cos^2\\theta + 1\\) AG\n(b) M1 \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{8}\\) gives \\(\\cos 4\\theta = \\cos\\frac{\\pi}{2} = 0\\), so \\(8c^4 - 8c^2 + 1 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(c^2 = \\frac{8 \\pm \\sqrt{32}}{16} = \\frac{2 \\pm \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\)\nE1 Since \\(0 \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{8} \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\), \\(\\cos^2\\frac{\\pi}{8} \\gt \\frac{1}{2}\\), so the + root and positive square root: \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{2 + \\sqrt{2}}}{2}\\) AG\n(c) M1 \\(2(8\\cos^4\\theta - 8\\cos^2\\theta + 1) - 1 = 0\\), so \\(\\cos 4\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nA1 \\(4\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}, \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\): \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\)` },
      { q: `A robotic arm consists of two straight rods OA and AB, rigidly joined at A so that angle OAB is a right angle. OA = 5 m and AB = 3 m. The rod OA rotates about the fixed point O and makes an angle \\(\\theta\\) radians above the horizontal, where \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). The rod AB points downwards away from O, so that the horizontal distance of B from O is x metres, where\n\\(x = 5\\cos\\theta + 3\\sin\\theta\\).\n\n(a) Express x in the form \\(R\\cos(\\theta - \\alpha)\\), where \\(R \\gt 0\\) and \\(0 \\lt \\alpha \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). Give the exact value of R and the value of \\(\\alpha\\) to 4 significant figures. [3]\n(b) State the greatest value of x and find the value of \\(\\theta\\) at which it occurs. [2]\n(c) Find the value of \\(\\theta\\) for which x = 4.5. Fully justify your answer. [3]\n(d) Use small-angle approximations to find a quadratic approximation for x, and hence estimate the small value of \\(\\theta\\) for which x = 5.5. [2]`, marks: 10, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(R = \\sqrt{34}\\)\nM1 \\(\\tan\\alpha = \\frac{3}{5}\\)\nA1 \\(\\alpha = 0.5404\\)\n(b) B1ft Greatest x = \\(\\sqrt{34}\\) = 5.83 m\nB1ft \\(\\theta = \\alpha = 0.540\\)\n(c) M1 \\(\\cos(\\theta - 0.5404) = \\frac{4.5}{\\sqrt{34}}\\), giving \\(\\theta - 0.5404 = \\pm 0.6892\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 1.23\\)\nE1 \\(\\theta = 0.5404 - 0.6892 = -0.149\\) is rejected as it is not in \\(0 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\)\n(d) M1 \\(x \\approx 5\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}\\theta^2\\right) + 3\\theta\\); sets equal to 5.5: \\(5\\theta^2 - 6\\theta + 1 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(\\theta = 0.2\\) (\\(\\theta = 1\\) rejected as not small)` }
    ]
  },
  '1.6': {
    name: 'Exponentials & Logarithms',
    questions: [
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nSolve the equation \\(2\\ln x = \\ln 18 - \\ln 2\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: uses a law of logs correctly, e.g. \\(\\ln x^2 = \\ln 9\\) soi\nA1: \\(x = 3\\) only (cao; \\(x = -3\\) must be rejected or not given, since \\(\\ln x\\) needs \\(x \\gt 0\\))\nFinal answer: \\(x = 3\\)` },
      { q: `Express \\(\\log_a 8 + 2\\log_a 3 - \\log_a 6\\) as a single logarithm in its simplest form.`, marks: 2, command: 'Express', markScheme: `M1: uses the power law and at least one of the product or quotient laws correctly, e.g. \\(2\\log_a 3 = \\log_a 9\\)\nA1: \\(\\log_a 12\\) cao\nFinal answer: \\(\\log_a 12\\)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(\\log_2 p = k\\).\n\nExpress \\(\\log_2 (8p^2)\\) in terms of \\(k\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Express', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\log_2 8 + \\log_2 p^2\\) or \\(\\log_2 8 + 2\\log_2 p\\) soi\nA1: \\(3 + 2k\\) cao\nFinal answer: \\(3 + 2k\\)` },
      { q: `Sketch the curve \\(y = e^{-x} + 2\\), stating the coordinates of the point where it crosses the \\(y\\)-axis and the equation of the asymptote.`, marks: 2, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `B1: decreasing exponential shape, wholly above the asymptote, passing through \\((0, 3)\\)\nB1: asymptote \\(y = 2\\) stated or labelled, with the curve approaching it as \\(x \\to \\infty\\)\nAnswer: \\((0, 3)\\); \\(y = 2\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(5^{2x} = 3^{x+1}\\), giving your answer correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: takes logs of both sides and brings down powers: \\(2x\\ln 5 = (x+1)\\ln 3\\)\nM1: collects \\(x\\) terms: \\(x(2\\ln 5 - \\ln 3) = \\ln 3\\)\nA1: \\(x = 0.518\\) (3 sf) cao\nFinal answer: \\(x = 0.518\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(\\ln(2x + 3) = 1 + \\ln x\\), giving your answer in exact form.`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{2x+3}{x}\\right) = 1\\) or \\(\\ln(2x+3) = \\ln(ex)\\)\nM1: removes logs correctly: \\(2x + 3 = ex\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{3}{e - 2}\\) (or exact equivalent)\nFinal answer: \\(x = \\frac{3}{e-2}\\) (≈ 4.18)` },
      { q: `The value, £\\(V\\), of a car \\(t\\) years after it was bought is modelled by\n\\(V = 18000e^{-0.15t}\\).\n\n(a) State the value of the car when it was bought. [1]\n(b) Find the age of the car when the model predicts that its value first falls below £6000. Give your answer in years correct to 3 significant figures. [2]`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: £18000\n(b)\nM1: \\(e^{-0.15t} = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and takes logs: \\(-0.15t = \\ln\\frac{1}{3}\\)\nA1: \\(t = 7.32\\) years\nFinal answers: (a) £18000 (b) 7.32 years` },
      { q: `Show that the equation \\(2\\log_3 x - \\log_3 (x - 2) = 2\\) can be written in the form \\(x^2 - 9x + 18 = 0\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: power law and quotient law: \\(\\log_3 \\frac{x^2}{x - 2} = 2\\)\nM1: removes logs correctly: \\(\\frac{x^2}{x-2} = 3^2 = 9\\)\nA1: \\(x^2 = 9x - 18\\), so \\(x^2 - 9x + 18 = 0\\) AG, with no errors seen\nNote: answer given, so every step must be shown.` },
      { q: `The number of bacteria, \\(P\\), in a culture \\(t\\) hours after the start of an experiment is modelled by \\(P = 500e^{0.04t}\\).\n\nFind the rate at which the number of bacteria is increasing when \\(t = 10\\). Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{dP}{dt} = 500 \\times 0.04e^{0.04t}\\), i.e. uses gradient of \\(e^{kx}\\) is \\(ke^{kx}\\)\nM1: substitutes \\(t = 10\\): \\(20e^{0.4}\\)\nA1: 29.8 bacteria per hour\nFinal answer: 29.8 per hour` },
      { q: `The variables \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are related by \\(y = kb^x\\), where \\(k\\) and \\(b\\) are constants. A graph of \\(\\log_{10} y\\) against \\(x\\) is a straight line passing through the points \\((0, 0.7)\\) and \\((5, 2.2)\\).\n\nFind the values of \\(k\\) and \\(b\\), giving your answers correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\log_{10} y = \\log_{10} k + x\\log_{10} b\\) soi, with gradient \\(= \\log_{10} b = \\frac{2.2 - 0.7}{5} = 0.3\\)\nA1: \\(k = 10^{0.7} = 5.01\\)\nA1: \\(b = 10^{0.3} = 2.00\\) (1.995…)\nFinal answers: \\(k = 5.01\\), \\(b = 2.00\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(3^{2x} - 10 \\times 3^x + 9 = 0\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: uses \\(u = 3^x\\) to obtain \\(u^2 - 10u + 9 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(u = 1\\) or \\(u = 9\\)\nA1: \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\)\nDR: an answer from a calculator equation solver alone scores 0.\nFinal answer: \\(x = 0, x = 2\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(4^x = 3 \\times 2^x + 10\\), giving your answer correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: writes \\(4^x = (2^x)^2\\) and forms a quadratic in \\(u = 2^x\\): \\(u^2 - 3u - 10 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(u = 5\\) or \\(u = -2\\)\nE1: rejects \\(2^x = -2\\) because \\(2^x \\gt 0\\) for all real \\(x\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{\\ln 5}{\\ln 2} = 2.32\\)\nFinal answer: \\(x = 2.32\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the simultaneous equations\n\\(\\log_2 x + \\log_2 y = 5\\)\n\\(y = x - 4\\)`, marks: 4, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\log_2 (xy) = 5\\) so \\(xy = 32\\)\nM1: substitutes to obtain \\(x(x - 4) = 32\\), i.e. \\(x^2 - 4x - 32 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 8\\), \\(y = 4\\)\nE1: rejects \\(x = -4\\) (and \\(y = -8\\)) because logs of negative numbers are undefined\nFinal answer: \\(x = 8, y = 4\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the equation \\(2\\ln(x + 3) - \\ln x = \\ln 16\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\ln\\frac{(x+3)^2}{x} = \\ln 16\\)\nM1: \\((x + 3)^2 = 16x\\)\nA1: \\(x^2 - 10x + 9 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = 9\\) (both valid since \\(x \\gt 0\\))\nFinal answer: \\(x = 1, x = 9\\)` },
      { q: `A patient is given a dose of a drug. The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of the drug in the patient's blood \\(t\\) hours later is modelled by \\(C = 12e^{-0.2t}\\).\n\n(a) Find the concentration after 3 hours. [1]\n(b) Find the time taken for the concentration to fall to 3 mg per litre. [2]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: 6.59 mg per litre (6.585…)\n(b)\nM1: \\(e^{-0.2t} = 0.25\\) and takes logs: \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 4}{0.2}\\)\nA1: 6.93 hours\n(c)\nE1: e.g. the model assumes the drug enters the blood instantly at \\(t = 0\\) / concentration never reaches zero / the decay rate will vary between patients\nFinal answers: (a) 6.59 (b) 6.93 hours` },
      { q: `Sketch the curve \\(y = 3e^{-x} - 1\\). On your sketch, state the exact coordinates of the points where the curve meets the axes, and the equation of the asymptote.`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `B1: correct shape: decreasing, concave up, defined for all \\(x\\)\nB1: \\((0, 2)\\)\nB1: \\((\\ln 3, 0)\\)\nB1: asymptote \\(y = -1\\), approached as \\(x \\to \\infty\\)\nAnswer: \\((0, 2)\\), \\((\\ln 3, 0)\\), \\(y = -1\\)` },
      { q: `The number of cells, \\(N\\), in a laboratory culture \\(t\\) hours after it is set up is modelled by \\(N = Ae^{kt}\\), where \\(A\\) and \\(k\\) are constants. Initially there are 200 cells and after 6 hours there are 800 cells.\n\nFind the exact values of \\(A\\) and \\(k\\), and hence find the number of cells predicted by the model after 10 hours.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(A = 200\\)\nM1: \\(800 = 200e^{6k}\\) so \\(e^{6k} = 4\\)\nA1: \\(k = \\frac{1}{6}\\ln 4\\) (\\(= \\frac{1}{3}\\ln 2\\))\nA1: \\(N = 200e^{10k} = 2016\\) (accept 2020 or 2015.9)\nFinal answers: \\(A = 200\\), \\(k = \\frac{1}{3}\\ln 2\\), about 2016 cells` },
      { q: `The variables \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are thought to satisfy \\(y = ax^n\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(n\\) are constants. A graph of \\(\\log_{10} y\\) against \\(\\log_{10} x\\) is a straight line. When \\(\\log_{10} x = 0.5\\), \\(\\log_{10} y = 1.9\\) and when \\(\\log_{10} x = 1.5\\), \\(\\log_{10} y = 3.4\\).\n\nFind the values of \\(a\\) and \\(n\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\log_{10} y = \\log_{10} a + n\\log_{10} x\\) soi\nA1: \\(n = \\frac{3.4 - 1.9}{1.5 - 0.5} = 1.5\\)\nM1: \\(\\log_{10} a = 1.9 - 1.5 \\times 0.5 = 1.15\\)\nA1: \\(a = 10^{1.15} = 14.1\\) (3 sf)\nFinal answers: \\(n = 1.5\\), \\(a = 14.1\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(\\log_4 x = \\frac{1}{2}\\log_2 x\\). [2]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(\\log_2 x + \\log_4 x = 6\\). [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: lets \\(\\log_4 x = y\\), so \\(x = 4^y = 2^{2y}\\)\nA1: \\(\\log_2 x = 2y\\), so \\(y = \\frac{1}{2}\\log_2 x\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\frac{3}{2}\\log_2 x = 6\\), so \\(\\log_2 x = 4\\)\nA1: \\(x = 16\\)\nFinal answer: \\(x = 16\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nSolve the equation \\(e^x - 6e^{-x} = 1\\), giving your answer in exact form.`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: multiplies through by \\(e^x\\): \\(e^{2x} - e^x - 6 = 0\\)\nM1: solves the quadratic in \\(e^x\\): \\((e^x - 3)(e^x + 2) = 0\\)\nA1: \\(e^x = 3\\) or \\(e^x = -2\\)\nE1: rejects \\(e^x = -2\\) since \\(e^x \\gt 0\\) for all \\(x\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\ln 3\\) only\nFinal answer: \\(x = \\ln 3\\)` },
      { q: `A cup of coffee is left to cool in a room. Its temperature, \\(\\theta\\) °C, \\(t\\) minutes later is modelled by \\(\\theta = 20 + 75e^{-kt}\\), where \\(k\\) is a positive constant. After 5 minutes the temperature is 65 °C.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(k\\), giving your answer correct to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(b) Find the time taken for the temperature to fall to 30 °C. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(65 = 20 + 75e^{-5k}\\), so \\(e^{-5k} = 0.6\\)\nM1: takes logs: \\(-5k = \\ln 0.6\\)\nA1: \\(k = 0.102\\) (0.10216…)\n(b)\nM1: \\(e^{-kt} = \\frac{10}{75}\\), so \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 7.5}{k}\\)\nA1: 19.7 minutes (FT their \\(k\\))\nFinal answers: (a) 0.102 (b) 19.7 minutes` },
      { q: `The mass, \\(y\\) grams, of a growing crystal \\(t\\) days after it starts forming is modelled by \\(y = kb^t\\), where \\(k\\) and \\(b\\) are constants.\n\n(a) Show that this model gives a straight-line relationship between \\(\\log_{10} y\\) and \\(t\\). [2]\n(b) The line of best fit for a graph of \\(\\log_{10} y\\) against \\(t\\) passes through the points \\((2, 1.62)\\) and \\((8, 3.06)\\). Find the values of \\(k\\) and \\(b\\). [3]\n(data written for this practice question)`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: takes logs: \\(\\log_{10} y = \\log_{10} k + \\log_{10} b^t\\)\nA1: \\(\\log_{10} y = (\\log_{10} b)t + \\log_{10} k\\), which is of the form \\(Y = mX + c\\) with gradient \\(\\log_{10} b\\) and intercept \\(\\log_{10} k\\)\n(b)\nM1: gradient \\(= \\frac{3.06 - 1.62}{6} = 0.24\\) and intercept \\(= 1.62 - 2(0.24) = 1.14\\)\nA1: \\(b = 10^{0.24} = 1.74\\)\nA1: \\(k = 10^{1.14} = 13.8\\)\nFinal answers: \\(k = 13.8\\), \\(b = 1.74\\)` },
      { q: `Solve the simultaneous equations\n\\(2^x \\times 4^y = 64\\)\n\\(\\frac{3^x}{9^y} = \\frac{1}{3}\\)`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `M1: writes the first equation with base 2: \\(2^{x + 2y} = 2^6\\)\nA1: \\(x + 2y = 6\\)\nM1: writes the second equation with base 3: \\(3^{x - 2y} = 3^{-1}\\)\nA1: \\(x - 2y = -1\\)\nA1: \\(x = 2.5\\), \\(y = 1.75\\)\nFinal answer: \\(x = \\frac{5}{2}, y = \\frac{7}{4}\\)` },
      { q: `When light passes through a stack of \\(n\\) identical glass sheets, the intensity of the light that emerges is modelled by \\(I = I_0 (0.92)^n\\), where \\(I_0\\) is the intensity of the light before it enters the stack.\n\n(a) Find the percentage of the light intensity that is lost after passing through 5 sheets. [2]\n(b) Find the least number of sheets needed for the emerging intensity to be less than 10% of \\(I_0\\). [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(0.92^5 = 0.659\\)\nA1: 34.1% lost\n(b)\nM1: \\(0.92^n \\lt 0.1\\) and takes logs: \\(n\\ln 0.92 \\lt \\ln 0.1\\)\nM1: divides by \\(\\ln 0.92\\), reversing the inequality since \\(\\ln 0.92 \\lt 0\\): \\(n \\gt 27.6\\)\nA1: 28 sheets\nFinal answers: (a) 34.1% (b) 28` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(a = \\log_3 2\\).\n\n(a) Express \\(\\log_3 18\\) in terms of \\(a\\). [2]\n(b) Hence show that the solution of the equation \\(2^x = 18\\) is \\(x = \\frac{2 + a}{a}\\). [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\log_3 18 = \\log_3 9 + \\log_3 2\\)\nA1: \\(2 + a\\)\n(b)\nM1: takes logs to base 3 of both sides: \\(\\log_3 2^x = \\log_3 18\\)\nM1: \\(x\\log_3 2 = \\log_3 18\\), i.e. \\(xa = 2 + a\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{2 + a}{a}\\) AG with full working\nFinal answer: \\(\\log_3 18 = 2 + a\\)` },
      { q: `The populations of two towns, A and B, \\(t\\) years after 1st January 2020 are modelled by\n\\(P_A = 12000e^{0.02t}\\) and \\(P_B = 15000e^{0.01t}\\).\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(t\\) when the models predict the two towns have equal populations. [3]\n(b) Comment on the suitability of these models for predicting the populations in the year 2220. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(12000e^{0.02t} = 15000e^{0.01t}\\)\nM1: \\(e^{0.01t} = 1.25\\), so \\(0.01t = \\ln 1.25\\)\nA1: \\(t = 22.3\\)\n(b)\nE1: the models predict unlimited exponential growth (e.g. town A would be about 650 000), which is unrealistic\nE1: housing, land and resources limit growth / 200 years is a large extrapolation, so the models are not suitable\nFinal answer: (a) \\(t = 22.3\\)` },
      { q: `The mass of carbon-14 in a sample of dead organic material decreases according to the model \\(m = m_0 e^{-kt}\\), where \\(m_0\\) is the initial mass, \\(t\\) is the time in years and \\(k\\) is a positive constant. The half-life of carbon-14 is 5730 years.\n\n(a) Show that \\(k = \\frac{\\ln 2}{5730}\\). [2]\n(b) A wooden tool is found to contain 35% of the carbon-14 that it contained when the wood was cut. Estimate the age of the tool. [3]\n(c) Give one reason why the age found may not be accurate. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}m_0 = m_0e^{-5730k}\\)\nA1: \\(-5730k = \\ln\\frac{1}{2} = -\\ln 2\\), so \\(k = \\frac{\\ln 2}{5730}\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(0.35 = e^{-kt}\\)\nM1: \\(t = \\frac{\\ln(1/0.35)}{k} = \\frac{5730\\ln(1/0.35)}{\\ln 2}\\)\nA1: 8680 years (8678.5…)\n(c)\nE1: e.g. the 35% is a measured value with error / the initial amount of carbon-14 is assumed, not known / the half-life value is rounded\nFinal answer: (b) about 8680 years` },
      { q: `A student believes that the variables \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are related by \\(y = ax^n\\). She collects the following data.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n\\(x\\): 2, 4, 8, 16\n\\(y\\): 5.7, 13.9, 34.3, 84.5\n\n(a) Explain why, if the relationship is correct, a graph of \\(\\log_{10} y\\) against \\(\\log_{10} x\\) will be a straight line. [2]\n(b) Using the first and last data points, find estimates for \\(a\\) and \\(n\\). [3]\n(c) Use your values to estimate \\(y\\) when \\(x = 10\\). [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\log_{10} y = \\log_{10} a + n\\log_{10} x\\)\nE1: this is linear in the form \\(Y = nX + c\\) with \\(Y = \\log_{10} y\\), \\(X = \\log_{10} x\\), gradient \\(n\\), intercept \\(\\log_{10} a\\)\n(b)\nM1: uses \\((0.301, 0.756)\\) and \\((1.204, 1.927)\\): \\(n = \\frac{1.927 - 0.756}{1.204 - 0.301}\\)\nA1: \\(n = 1.30\\) (awrt 1.3)\nA1: \\(\\log_{10} a = 0.756 - 1.30 \\times 0.301 = 0.366\\), \\(a = 2.32\\) (accept 2.3)\n(c)\nB1: \\(y = 2.32 \\times 10^{1.30} = 45.9\\) (accept 46; FT their \\(a, n\\))\nFinal answers: \\(a \\approx 2.3\\), \\(n \\approx 1.3\\), \\(y \\approx 46\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Show that the equation \\(\\log_3 (x + 2) + \\log_3 (x - 4) = 3\\) can be written as \\(x^2 - 2x - 35 = 0\\). [3]\n(b) Hence solve the equation \\(\\log_3 (x + 2) + \\log_3 (x - 4) = 3\\), justifying your answer. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\log_3 [(x + 2)(x - 4)] = 3\\)\nM1: \\((x + 2)(x - 4) = 27\\)\nA1: \\(x^2 - 2x - 8 = 27\\), so \\(x^2 - 2x - 35 = 0\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\((x - 7)(x + 5) = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 7\\)\nE1: rejects \\(x = -5\\) since \\(x - 4\\) (and \\(x + 2\\)) would be negative and the log of a negative number is undefined\nFinal answer: \\(x = 7\\)` },
      { q: `The number of rabbits, \\(P\\), on an island \\(t\\) years after a survey began is modelled by \\(P = Ae^{kt}\\), where \\(A\\) and \\(k\\) are positive constants.\n\n(a) Show that, according to the model, the rate of increase of the population is proportional to the size of the population. [2]\n(b) At the start of the survey there are 4000 rabbits and the population is increasing at a rate of 120 rabbits per year. Find the value of \\(k\\). [1]\n(c) Find the time taken for the population to double. [2]\n(d) Give one reason why the model may not be suitable in the long term. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{dP}{dt} = kAe^{kt}\\)\nA1: \\(= kP\\), so rate is proportional to \\(P\\) AG\n(b)\nB1: \\(120 = 4000k\\), \\(k = 0.03\\)\n(c)\nM1: \\(e^{0.03t} = 2\\), \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 2}{0.03}\\)\nA1: 23.1 years\n(d)\nE1: e.g. the island has limited food and space so the population cannot grow without limit / predators or disease are ignored\nFinal answers: (b) 0.03 (c) 23.1 years` },
      { q: `(a) On the same axes, sketch the curves \\(y = 2^x\\) and \\(y = 3 \\times 2^{-x}\\), stating the coordinates of the points where each curve crosses the \\(y\\)-axis. [3]\n(b) Find the exact coordinates of the point of intersection of the two curves. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(y = 2^x\\) increasing exponential through \\((0, 1)\\), asymptotic to the negative \\(x\\)-axis\nB1: \\(y = 3 \\times 2^{-x}\\) decreasing exponential through \\((0, 3)\\), asymptotic to the positive \\(x\\)-axis\nB1: both curves above the \\(x\\)-axis, crossing once in the first quadrant\n(b)\nM1: \\(2^x = 3 \\times 2^{-x}\\), so \\(2^{2x} = 3\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\log_2 3\\) (or \\(\\frac{\\ln 3}{2\\ln 2}\\))\nA1: \\(y = 2^x = \\sqrt{3}\\)\nFinal answer: \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\log_2 3, \\sqrt{3}\\right)\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Given that \\(y = 3^x\\), express \\(3^{2x + 1}\\) in terms of \\(y\\). [1]\n(b) In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\nHence solve the equation \\(3^{2x + 1} - 28 \\times 3^x + 9 = 0\\). [5]`, marks: 6, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(3y^2\\)\n(b)\nM1: \\(3y^2 - 28y + 9 = 0\\)\nM1: factorises or uses the formula: \\((3y - 1)(y - 9) = 0\\)\nA1: \\(y = \\frac{1}{3}\\) or \\(y = 9\\)\nM1: solves \\(3^x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and \\(3^x = 9\\)\nA1: \\(x = -1\\) and \\(x = 2\\)\nDR: an answer from a calculator alone scores 0.\nFinal answer: \\(x = -1, x = 2\\)` },
      { q: `The value, £\\(V\\), of a machine \\(t\\) years after it was bought is modelled by \\(V = ab^t\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are constants. When \\(t = 2\\), \\(V = 12800\\) and when \\(t = 5\\), \\(V = 6553.60\\).\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). [4]\n(b) Explain what the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) mean in this context. [2]\n(c) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(ab^2 = 12800\\) and \\(ab^5 = 6553.6\\)\nM1: divides: \\(b^3 = 0.512\\)\nA1: \\(b = 0.8\\)\nA1: \\(a = \\frac{12800}{0.64} = 20000\\)\n(b)\nE1: \\(a\\): the machine cost £20000 when new\nE1: \\(b\\): the value falls by 20% each year (it keeps 80% of its value)\n(c)\nE1: e.g. the value never reaches zero / the machine may have a scrap value / depreciation is unlikely to be a constant percentage every year\nFinal answers: \\(a = 20000\\), \\(b = 0.8\\)` },
      { q: `Kepler suggested that the time, \\(T\\) years, that a planet takes to orbit the Sun is related to its mean distance, \\(r\\) astronomical units (AU), from the Sun by a model of the form \\(T = ar^n\\).\n\nFor Mars, \\(r = 1.524\\) and \\(T = 1.881\\). For Saturn, \\(r = 9.537\\) and \\(T = 29.46\\).\n\n(a) Explain how a graph of \\(\\log_{10} T\\) against \\(\\log_{10} r\\) could be used to test whether the model is suitable. [2]\n(b) Use the data for Mars and Saturn to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(n\\), giving \\(n\\) correct to 2 significant figures. [4]\n(c) The mean distance of Uranus from the Sun is 19.19 AU. Use your model to estimate the time Uranus takes to orbit the Sun. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\log_{10} T = \\log_{10} a + n\\log_{10} r\\)\nE1: if the points for the planets lie (close to) a straight line, the model is suitable; gradient \\(n\\), intercept \\(\\log_{10} a\\)\n(b)\nM1: finds logs: Mars \\((0.1830, 0.2744)\\), Saturn \\((0.9794, 1.4692)\\)\nM1: gradient \\(= \\frac{1.4692 - 0.2744}{0.9794 - 0.1830}\\)\nA1: \\(n = 1.5\\)\nA1: \\(\\log_{10} a \\approx 0\\), so \\(a = 1.0\\)\n(c)\nB1: \\(T = 19.19^{1.5} = 84.1\\) years (FT their \\(a, n\\))\nFinal answers: \\(a = 1\\), \\(n = 1.5\\), about 84 years` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(\\log_x 2 = \\frac{1}{\\log_2 x}\\). [2]\n(b) In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\nHence solve the equation \\(\\log_2 x - 3\\log_x 2 = 2\\). [5]`, marks: 7, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: lets \\(\\log_x 2 = y\\), so \\(x^y = 2\\), and takes logs to base 2: \\(y\\log_2 x = \\log_2 2 = 1\\)\nA1: \\(y = \\frac{1}{\\log_2 x}\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: lets \\(u = \\log_2 x\\): \\(u - \\frac{3}{u} = 2\\)\nM1: \\(u^2 - 2u - 3 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(u = 3\\) or \\(u = -1\\)\nM1: \\(x = 2^3\\) or \\(x = 2^{-1}\\)\nA1: \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nFinal answer: \\(x = 8, x = \\frac{1}{2}\\)` },
      { q: `A lake is restocked with fish. The number of fish, \\(N\\), in the lake \\(t\\) months after restocking is modelled by \\(N = 3000 - 2000e^{-0.05t}\\).\n\n(a) Find the number of fish in the lake immediately after restocking. [1]\n(b) Find the time taken for the number of fish to reach 2500. [3]\n(c) State the value that the number of fish approaches in the long term, according to the model. [1]\n(d) Show that the rate of increase in the number of fish when \\(t = 10\\) is about 61 fish per month. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: 1000\n(b)\nM1: \\(2000e^{-0.05t} = 500\\)\nM1: \\(-0.05t = \\ln 0.25\\)\nA1: \\(t = 20\\ln 4 = 27.7\\) months\n(c)\nB1: 3000\n(d)\nM1: \\(\\frac{dN}{dt} = 100e^{-0.05t}\\)\nA1: \\(100e^{-0.5} = 60.65…\\approx 61\\) AG\nFinal answers: (a) 1000 (b) 27.7 months (c) 3000` },
      { q: `A patient is given a dose of a drug at time \\(t = 0\\), where \\(t\\) is measured in hours. The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of the drug in the blood is modelled by \\(C = 8e^{-0.25t}\\). A second, identical dose is given at \\(t = 4\\), so that for \\(t \\geq 4\\)\n\\(C = 8e^{-0.25t} + 8e^{-0.25(t - 4)}\\).\n\n(a) Find the concentration immediately after the second dose is given. [2]\n(b) Show that, for \\(t \\geq 4\\), \\(C = 8(1 + e)e^{-0.25t}\\). [2]\n(c) The drug is ineffective when its concentration is below 2 mg per litre. Find the value of \\(t\\) when the concentration falls to 2 mg per litre. [3]\n(d) State one assumption made in this model. [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(8e^{-1} + 8\\)\nA1: 10.9 mg per litre (10.94…)\n(b)\nM1: \\(e^{-0.25(t - 4)} = e^{-0.25t}e^{1}\\)\nA1: \\(C = 8e^{-0.25t} + 8e \\cdot e^{-0.25t} = 8(1 + e)e^{-0.25t}\\) AG\n(c)\nM1: \\(8(1 + e)e^{-0.25t} = 2\\), so \\(e^{-0.25t} = \\frac{1}{4(1 + e)}\\)\nM1: \\(t = 4\\ln[4(1 + e)]\\)\nA1: \\(t = 10.8\\) hours\n(d)\nE1: e.g. each dose is absorbed instantly / the effects of the two doses simply add / the rate of decay is the same at all concentrations\nFinal answers: (a) 10.9 (c) 10.8 hours` },
      { q: `A biologist records the number of bacteria, \\(N\\), in a sample \\(t\\) hours after the start of an experiment.\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n\\(t\\): 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5\n\\(N\\): 210, 330, 520, 810, 1270, 2000\n\\(\\log_{10} N\\): 2.322, 2.519, 2.716, 2.908, 3.104, 3.301\n\nShe models the data by \\(N = kb^t\\), where \\(k\\) and \\(b\\) are constants.\n\n(a) Show that the model can be written as \\(\\log_{10} N = \\log_{10} k + t\\log_{10} b\\), and explain how the table supports the model. [2]\n(b) Using the values for \\(t = 0\\) and \\(t = 5\\), find estimates for \\(k\\) and \\(b\\). [3]\n(c) Explain what the value of \\(b\\) tells you about the bacteria. [1]\n(d) Use the model to estimate the time at which the number of bacteria reaches 5000, and comment on the reliability of your answer. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(\\log_{10} N = \\log_{10} k + \\log_{10} b^t = \\log_{10} k + t\\log_{10} b\\)\nE1: \\(\\log_{10} N\\) increases by roughly 0.2 each hour, so \\(\\log_{10} N\\) against \\(t\\) is (approximately) linear\n(b)\nM1: gradient \\(= \\frac{3.301 - 2.322}{5} = 0.1958\\)\nA1: \\(b = 10^{0.1958} = 1.57\\)\nA1: \\(k = 10^{2.322} = 210\\)\n(c)\nE1: the number of bacteria increases by about 57% each hour (multiplied by about 1.57)\n(d)\nM1: \\(t = \\frac{\\log_{10} 5000 - 2.322}{0.1958} = 7.0\\) hours\nE1: unreliable since it is extrapolation beyond the data / growth may slow as nutrients run out\nFinal answers: \\(k \\approx 210\\), \\(b \\approx 1.57\\), \\(t \\approx 7.0\\) hours` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(y = e^{2x} - 6e^x + 5\\).\n\n(a) Find the exact coordinates of the points where the curve meets the coordinate axes. [4]\n(b) Find the exact coordinates of the stationary point on the curve. [3]\n(c) State the equation of the asymptote to the curve. [1]\n(d) Sketch the curve. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Find', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a)\nB1: when \\(x = 0\\), \\(y = 1 - 6 + 5 = 0\\), so \\((0, 0)\\)\nM1: \\((e^x - 1)(e^x - 5) = 0\\)\nA1: \\(e^x = 1\\) or \\(e^x = 5\\)\nA1: \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((\\ln 5, 0)\\)\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = 2e^{2x} - 6e^x\\) and sets equal to 0\nA1: \\(2e^x(e^x - 3) = 0\\), so \\(e^x = 3\\), \\(x = \\ln 3\\)\nA1: \\(y = 9 - 18 + 5 = -4\\), so \\((\\ln 3, -4)\\)\n(c)\nB1: \\(y = 5\\) (as \\(x \\to -\\infty\\))\n(d)\nB1: curve from asymptote \\(y = 5\\) on the left, through the origin, minimum at \\((\\ln 3, -4)\\), through \\((\\ln 5, 0)\\) and increasing rapidly\nFinal answers: \\((0,0)\\), \\((\\ln 5, 0)\\); \\((\\ln 3, -4)\\); \\(y = 5\\)` },
      { q: `A mug of tea is made and left in a room. The temperature of the tea, \\(\\theta\\) °C, \\(t\\) minutes after it is made is modelled by \\(\\theta = 20 + Ae^{-kt}\\), where \\(A\\) and \\(k\\) are positive constants. The initial temperature of the tea is 90 °C and after 4 minutes it is 70 °C.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(A\\). [1]\n(b) Show that \\(k = \\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\frac{7}{5}\\). [3]\n(c) Find the temperature of the tea after 10 minutes. [2]\n(d) Find the time taken for the tea to cool to 40 °C. [2]\n(e) (i) State what the model predicts about the temperature of the tea in the long term.\n(ii) Give one reason why the model may not be accurate. [2]`, marks: 10, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(A = 70\\)\n(b)\nM1: \\(70 = 20 + 70e^{-4k}\\), so \\(e^{-4k} = \\frac{5}{7}\\)\nM1: \\(-4k = \\ln\\frac{5}{7}\\)\nA1: \\(k = \\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\frac{7}{5}\\) AG, with \\(\\ln\\frac{5}{7} = -\\ln\\frac{7}{5}\\) shown\n(c)\nM1: \\(\\theta = 20 + 70e^{-10k}\\) with \\(k = 0.0841…\\)\nA1: 50.2 °C\n(d)\nM1: \\(70e^{-kt} = 20\\), \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 3.5}{k}\\)\nA1: 14.9 minutes\n(e)\nB1: (i) the temperature approaches 20 °C, the room temperature\nE1: (ii) e.g. the room temperature may not stay constant / milk may be added / the cooling rate may change if the mug is moved or covered\nFinal answers: (a) 70 (c) 50.2 °C (d) 14.9 minutes` }
    ]
  },
  '1.7': {
    name: 'Differentiation',
    questions: [
      { q: `Find \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) when \\(y = 2x^5 - \\frac{3}{x}\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: writes \\(\\frac{3}{x}\\) as \\(3x^{-1}\\) and differentiates at least one term correctly (power reduced by 1)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 10x^4 + 3x^{-2}\\) oe, e.g. \\(10x^4 + \\frac{3}{x^2}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(10x^4 + \\frac{3}{x^2}\\)` },
      { q: `Given that \\(y = 3^{2x} + \\sin 4x\\), find \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(2 \\ln 3 \\times 3^{2x}\\) oe, e.g. \\(\\ln 9 \\times 3^{2x}\\)\nB1: \\(4\\cos 4x\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 2\\ln 3 \\times 3^{2x} + 4\\cos 4x\\)` },
      { q: `Show that \\(\\mathrm{f}(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + 6x - 1\\) is an increasing function for all real values of \\(x\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Show', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\mathrm{f}'(x) = 3x^2 + 6x + 6\\) and attempts to complete the square or uses the discriminant: \\(3(x+1)^2 + 3\\), or \\(b^2 - 4ac = 36 - 72 = -36\\)\nA1: \\(3(x+1)^2 + 3 \\gt 0\\) for all \\(x\\) (or discriminant negative and leading coefficient positive), so \\(\\mathrm{f}'(x) \\gt 0\\) and f is increasing AG\nConclusion must be stated.` },
      { q: `A spherical balloon is being inflated. The volume, \\(V\\) cm³, of the balloon when its radius is \\(r\\) cm is given by \\(V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}r}\\) when \\(r = 3\\), giving your answer in terms of \\(\\pi\\), and explain what this value represents in context.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 4\\pi r^2 = 36\\pi\\)\nE1: the rate at which the volume changes with respect to the radius when the radius is 3 cm (volume increases by about \\(36\\pi\\) cm³ per cm increase in radius); must refer to volume and radius, not time\nAnswer: \\(36\\pi\\)` },
      { q: `Given that \\(y = x^2 \\mathrm{e}^{-3x}\\), find \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\), giving your answer in the form \\(\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}(ax^2 + bx)\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are integers.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: attempts product rule, form \\(px\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} + qx^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}\\)\nA1: \\(2x\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} - 3x^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}\\)\nA1: \\(\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}(-3x^2 + 2x)\\), so \\(a = -3,\\ b = 2\\) cao` },
      { q: `Show that, if \\(y = \\frac{\\ln x}{x^2}\\), then \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{1 - 2\\ln x}{x^3}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `M1: attempts quotient rule (or product rule on \\(x^{-2}\\ln x\\)), form \\(\\frac{x^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - 2x\\ln x}{x^4}\\)\nA1: correct unsimplified derivative \\(\\frac{x - 2x\\ln x}{x^4}\\)\nA1: divides through by \\(x\\) to obtain \\(\\frac{1 - 2\\ln x}{x^3}\\) AG; no errors seen` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(y = (3x^2 - 5)^4\\).\n\nFind the gradient of the curve at the point where \\(x = 1\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: chain rule, form \\(k x (3x^2 - 5)^3\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 24x(3x^2 - 5)^3\\)\nA1: \\(-192\\) cao` },
      { q: `Find the equation of the tangent to the curve \\(y = 2x^3 - 5x + 4\\) at the point where \\(x = 1\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: differentiates to \\(6x^2 - 5\\) and substitutes \\(x = 1\\) to obtain gradient 1\nB1: \\(y = 1\\) when \\(x = 1\\)\nA1: \\(y - 1 = 1(x - 1)\\), i.e. \\(y = x\\) cao` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(y = x^4 - 6x^2 + 3\\).\n\nDetermine the set of values of \\(x\\) for which the curve is concave.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = 12x^2 - 12\\)\nM1: sets \\(12x^2 - 12 \\lt 0\\) (concave requires second derivative negative)\nA1: \\(-1 \\lt x \\lt 1\\) cao (allow \\(\\leq\\))` },
      { q: `The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of a drug in a patient's bloodstream \\(t\\) hours after the drug is taken is modelled by \\(C = 20t\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t}\\).\n\nShow that \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}C}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t}(2 - t)\\) and hence state the time at which the model predicts the concentration is greatest.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show', markScheme: `M1: product rule, form \\(20\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t} + kt\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t}\\)\nA1: \\(20\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t} - 10t\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t} = 10\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5t}(2 - t)\\) AG\nB1: \\(t = 2\\), i.e. 2 hours after the drug is taken (BC no justification of maximum required for "state")` },
      { q: `Given that \\(y = x\\tan x\\), find the exact value of \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) when \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: product rule, form \\(\\tan x + x \\times (\\text{derivative of } \\tan x)\\)\nA1: \\(\\tan x + x\\sec^2 x\\)\nA1: \\(1 + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\times 2 = 1 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) cao, exact` },
      { q: `Prove from first principles that the derivative of \\(3x^2 - x\\) is \\(6x - 1\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: correct use of \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{f}(x+h) - \\mathrm{f}(x)}{h}\\) with \\(\\mathrm{f}(x+h) = 3(x+h)^2 - (x+h)\\)\nM1: expands to \\(\\frac{6xh + 3h^2 - h}{h}\\)\nA1: simplifies to \\(6x + 3h - 1\\)\nE1: as \\(h \\to 0\\), \\(6x + 3h - 1 \\to 6x - 1\\), so derivative is \\(6x - 1\\) AG; must use limit notation or words "as \\(h\\) tends to 0" (not \\(h = 0\\))` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(x^2 + 3xy + y^2 = 11\\).\n\nFind the gradient of the curve at the point \\((1, 2)\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: implicit differentiation: \\(2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) term seen\nM1: product rule on \\(3xy\\): \\(3y + 3x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\)\nA1: \\(2x + 3y + 3x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0\\) oe\nA1: \\(-\\frac{8}{7}\\) cao` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = t^2 + 1,\\ y = 2t^3 - t\\).\n\nFind \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) in terms of \\(t\\), and hence find the gradient of the curve at the point where \\(t = 2\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 2t\\)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6t^2 - 1\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y/\\mathrm{d}t}{\\mathrm{d}x/\\mathrm{d}t} = \\frac{6t^2 - 1}{2t}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{23}{4}\\) cao` },
      { q: `Find the equation of the normal to the curve \\(y = 3\\sqrt{x} - \\frac{8}{x}\\) at the point where \\(x = 4\\). Give your answer in the form \\(ax + by + c = 0\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are integers.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: differentiates: \\(\\frac{3}{2}x^{-\\frac{1}{2}} + 8x^{-2}\\)\nA1: gradient of tangent \\(\\frac{3}{4} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{5}{4}\\), and \\(y = 4\\) at \\(x = 4\\)\nM1: normal gradient \\(-\\frac{4}{5}\\) used in \\(y - 4 = m(x - 4)\\)\nA1: \\(4x + 5y - 36 = 0\\) oe with integer coefficients` },
      { q: `Ink is dropped onto paper and spreads out as a circle. The area of the circle increases at a constant rate of 3 cm² per second.\n\nFind the rate at which the radius of the circle is increasing at the instant when the radius is 4 cm. Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(A = \\pi r^2\\), \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 2\\pi r\\)\nM1: chain rule \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\div \\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{3}{8\\pi}\\)\nA1: 0.119 cm s⁻¹ (3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `A small company models its annual profit, \\(P\\) thousand pounds, from making and selling \\(x\\) thousand items by\n\\(P = -x^3 + 3x^2 + 9x - 2\\), for \\(x \\geq 0\\).\n\nUse differentiation to find the maximum annual profit predicted by the model, justifying that it is a maximum.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -3x^2 + 6x + 9\\) set equal to 0\nA1: \\(x = 3\\) (\\(x = -1\\) rejected as \\(x \\geq 0\\))\nA1: \\(P = 25\\), i.e. maximum profit £25 000\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2P}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = -6x + 6 = -12 \\lt 0\\) at \\(x = 3\\), so maximum (or valid sign-change argument)` },
      { q: `The curve \\(y = \\mathrm{e}^{2x} - 6x\\) has one stationary point.\n\nFind the exact coordinates of the stationary point and determine its nature.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 2\\mathrm{e}^{2x} - 6 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 3\\)\nA1: \\(y = 3 - 3\\ln 3\\)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = 4\\mathrm{e}^{2x} \\gt 0\\) (= 12), so minimum point` },
      { q: `The depth of water, \\(d\\) metres, at the entrance to a harbour \\(t\\) hours after midnight is modelled by\n\\(d = 5 + 2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right)\\).\n\n(a) Find the rate at which the depth is changing at 2 am. Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(b) State one limitation of this model. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}d}{\\mathrm{d}t} = k\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{6}\\right)\\)\nA1: \\(k = \\frac{2\\pi}{6} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\)\nA1: at \\(t = 2\\): \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 0.524\\) m per hour (increasing)\n(b)\nB1: e.g. the tide pattern will not repeat exactly every 12 hours / the model ignores weather such as storms or wind / tides vary between spring and neap tides so the amplitude is not constant` },
      { q: `Prove from first principles that the derivative of \\(\\sin x\\) is \\(\\cos x\\).\n\nYou may use without proof the results \\(\\frac{\\sin h}{h} \\to 1\\) and \\(\\frac{\\cos h - 1}{h} \\to 0\\) as \\(h \\to 0\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{\\sin(x + h) - \\sin x}{h}\\) seen\nM1: uses \\(\\sin(x+h) = \\sin x\\cos h + \\cos x \\sin h\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\sin x(\\cos h - 1) + \\cos x\\sin h}{h}\\)\nM1: rewrites as \\(\\sin x \\cdot \\frac{\\cos h - 1}{h} + \\cos x \\cdot \\frac{\\sin h}{h}\\) and considers \\(h \\to 0\\)\nA1: limit \\(= \\sin x \\times 0 + \\cos x \\times 1 = \\cos x\\) AG; limit notation required` },
      { q: `(a) Show that, if \\(y = \\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}\\), then \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\cos x}\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the equation of the tangent to the curve \\(y = \\frac{\\sin x}{1 + \\cos x}\\) at the point where \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: quotient rule, form \\(\\frac{\\cos x(1 + \\cos x) \\pm \\sin x \\sin x}{(1 + \\cos x)^2}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\cos x + \\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x}{(1 + \\cos x)^2}\\)\nA1: uses \\(\\sin^2 x + \\cos^2 x = 1\\) to get \\(\\frac{1 + \\cos x}{(1 + \\cos x)^2} = \\frac{1}{1 + \\cos x}\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: at \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\): \\(y = 1\\) and gradient \\(= 1\\), used in straight-line equation\nA1: \\(y = x + 1 - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) oe exact` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(x^3 + y^3 = 9xy\\). The point \\(P(2, 4)\\) lies on the curve.\n\nFind the equation of the normal to the curve at \\(P\\), giving your answer in the form \\(ax + by + c = 0\\) where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are integers.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: implicit differentiation with product rule on \\(9xy\\)\nA1: \\(3x^2 + 3y^2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 9y + 9x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\)\nA1: gradient at \\(P\\) \\(= \\frac{36 - 12}{48 - 18} = \\frac{4}{5}\\)\nM1: normal gradient \\(-\\frac{5}{4}\\) with \\(y - 4 = m(x - 2)\\)\nA1: \\(5x + 4y - 26 = 0\\) oe` },
      { q: `An ellipse has parametric equations \\(x = 2\\cos\\theta,\\ y = 3\\sin\\theta\\), for \\(0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\\).\n\nFind the equation of the tangent to the ellipse at the point where \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\). Give your answer in the form \\(a\\sqrt{3}\\,x + by = c\\), where \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are integers.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{3\\cos\\theta}{-2\\sin\\theta}\\)\nA1: gradient at \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\) is \\(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)\nB1: point \\(\\left(\\sqrt{3}, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\)\nM1: \\(y - \\frac{3}{2} = -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}(x - \\sqrt{3})\\)\nA1: \\(3\\sqrt{3}\\,x + 2y = 12\\) cao` },
      { q: `A container is in the shape of a right circular cone of height 12 cm and base radius 6 cm, held with its vertex downwards and its axis vertical. Water is poured in at a constant rate of 5 cm³ per second. When the depth of water is \\(h\\) cm, the volume of water is \\(V\\) cm³.\n\n(a) Show that \\(V = \\frac{\\pi h^3}{12}\\). [2]\n(b) Find the rate at which the depth of water is increasing when \\(h = 4\\). Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: similar triangles: radius of water surface \\(r = \\frac{h}{2}\\)\nA1: \\(V = \\frac{1}{3}\\pi\\left(\\frac{h}{2}\\right)^2 h = \\frac{\\pi h^3}{12}\\) AG\n(b)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h} = \\frac{\\pi h^2}{4}\\) (= \\(4\\pi\\) when \\(h = 4\\))\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}h}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 5 \\div \\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}h}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{5}{4\\pi} = 0.398\\) cm s⁻¹` },
      { q: `An open box is made from a square sheet of card of side 30 cm by cutting a square of side \\(x\\) cm from each corner and folding up the sides. The volume of the box is \\(V\\) cm³, where \\(V = x(30 - 2x)^2\\).\n\nUse calculus to find the maximum volume of the box, justifying that it is a maximum.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: differentiates (expand to \\(4x^3 - 120x^2 + 900x\\) or product rule)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 12x^2 - 240x + 900\\) oe\nA1: \\(x = 5\\) (\\(x = 15\\) rejected since this gives zero volume)\nA1: \\(V = 2000\\) cm³\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2V}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = 24x - 240 = -120 \\lt 0\\) at \\(x = 5\\), so maximum` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(y = x^4 - 4x^3 + 10\\).\n\nFind the coordinates of the points of inflection of the curve. Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = 12x^2 - 24x\\)\nM1: sets \\(12x(x - 2) = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\)\nA1: \\((0, 10)\\) and \\((2, -6)\\)\nE1: shows second derivative changes sign at each, e.g. \\(y''(-1) = 36 \\gt 0,\\ y''(1) = -12 \\lt 0,\\ y''(3) = 36 \\gt 0\\); so both are points of inflection\n(\\((0, 10)\\) is also a stationary point of inflection; not required)` },
      { q: `Find the coordinates of the stationary points of the curve \\(y = 2x^3 + 3x^2 - 12x + 5\\) and determine their nature. Hence state the set of values of \\(x\\) for which \\(y\\) is a decreasing function of \\(x\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 6x^2 + 6x - 12\\) set equal to 0\nA1: \\(x = -2,\\ x = 1\\)\nA1: \\((-2, 25)\\) and \\((1, -2)\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = 12x + 6\\) evaluated at one point (or sign test)\nA1: \\(-18 \\lt 0\\) so \\((-2, 25)\\) maximum; \\(18 \\gt 0\\) so \\((1, -2)\\) minimum\nB1ft: decreasing for \\(-2 \\leq x \\leq 1\\) (allow strict)` },
      { q: `A closed cylindrical can is to hold 500 cm³ of soup. The can has radius \\(r\\) cm and height \\(h\\) cm, and its total surface area is \\(A\\) cm².\n\n(a) Show that \\(A = 2\\pi r^2 + \\frac{1000}{r}\\). [2]\n(b) Use calculus to find the value of \\(r\\) for which \\(A\\) is a minimum, and find this minimum value of \\(A\\). Give your answers correct to 3 significant figures. [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\pi r^2 h = 500\\) used to eliminate \\(h\\) in \\(A = 2\\pi r^2 + 2\\pi r h\\)\nA1: \\(2\\pi r \\times \\frac{500}{\\pi r^2} = \\frac{1000}{r}\\), giving AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 4\\pi r - \\frac{1000}{r^2} = 0\\)\nA1: \\(r^3 = \\frac{250}{\\pi}\\), \\(r = 4.30\\)\nA1: \\(A = 349\\) cm²\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2A}{\\mathrm{d}r^2} = 4\\pi + \\frac{2000}{r^3} \\gt 0\\), so minimum` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = t^2,\\ y = t^3 - 3t\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) in terms of \\(t\\). [2]\n(b) Find the coordinates of the points on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis. [3]\n(c) State the coordinates of the point on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t} \\div \\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\) with at least one derivative correct\nA1: \\(\\frac{3t^2 - 3}{2t}\\)\n(b)\nM1: sets \\(3t^2 - 3 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(t = \\pm 1\\)\nA1: \\((1, -2)\\) and \\((1, 2)\\)\n(c)\nB1: \\((0, 0)\\) (where \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0\\), \\(t = 0\\))` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nA curve has equation \\(x^2 + xy + y^2 = 12\\).\n\nFind the coordinates of the points on the curve at which \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `DR\nM1: implicit differentiation with product rule on \\(xy\\)\nA1: \\(2x + y + x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 2y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -\\frac{2x + y}{x + 2y}\\)\nM1: sets numerator to zero: \\(y = -2x\\)\nM1: substitutes into curve: \\(x^2 - 2x^2 + 4x^2 = 12\\), \\(3x^2 = 12\\)\nA1: \\((2, -4)\\) and \\((-2, 4)\\)` },
      { q: `A spherical snowball is melting. Its radius is \\(r\\) cm at time \\(t\\) hours. In a model, the volume of the snowball decreases at a rate proportional to its surface area, so that \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -kA\\), where \\(k\\) is a positive constant.\n[The volume of a sphere is \\(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\) and its surface area is \\(4\\pi r^2\\).]\n\n(a) Show that \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -k\\). [3]\n(b) The radius of the snowball decreases from 10 cm to 8 cm in 2 hours. Find how much longer it takes for the snowball to melt completely, according to the model. [2]\n(c) Suggest one reason why the model may not be realistic. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 4\\pi r^2\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}V}{\\mathrm{d}r} \\times \\frac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\) so \\(4\\pi r^2\\frac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -k \\times 4\\pi r^2\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}r}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -k\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: radius decreases at a constant rate, \\(k = 1\\) cm per hour\nA1: 8 more hours\n(c)\nB1: e.g. the snowball does not stay spherical / the rate of melting depends on air temperature, which will vary` },
      { q: `Find the exact coordinates of the stationary point on the curve \\(y = x\\mathrm{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}x}\\), and use the second derivative to determine its nature.`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: product rule\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\mathrm{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}x}\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2}x\\right)\\)\nA1: \\(x = 2\\), \\(y = \\frac{2}{\\mathrm{e}}\\) (or \\(2\\mathrm{e}^{-1}\\))\nM1: differentiates again using product rule\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = \\mathrm{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}x}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}x - 1\\right)\\)\nA1: at \\(x = 2\\), \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathrm{e}^{-1} \\lt 0\\), so maximum point` },
      { q: `A window is in the shape of a rectangle of width \\(2r\\) metres and height \\(h\\) metres, with a semicircle of radius \\(r\\) metres on top. The perimeter of the whole window is 6 m. The area of the window is \\(A\\) m².\n\n(a) Show that \\(A = 6r - \\left(2 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)r^2\\). [3]\n(b) Use calculus to find the maximum area of the window, giving your answer in exact form. [3]\n(c) Justify that this value is a maximum. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: perimeter \\(2r + 2h + \\pi r = 6\\)\nM1: \\(A = 2rh + \\frac{1}{2}\\pi r^2\\) with \\(h\\) substituted\nA1: \\(r(6 - 2r - \\pi r) + \\frac{1}{2}\\pi r^2 = 6r - \\left(2 + \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)r^2\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}A}{\\mathrm{d}r} = 6 - (4 + \\pi)r = 0\\)\nA1: \\(r = \\frac{6}{4 + \\pi}\\)\nA1: \\(A = \\frac{18}{4 + \\pi}\\) m² (≈ 2.52)\n(c)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2A}{\\mathrm{d}r^2} = -(4 + \\pi) \\lt 0\\), so maximum` },
      { q: `The function f is defined by \\(\\mathrm{f}(x) = x^2\\mathrm{e}^x\\) for all real \\(x\\).\n\n(a) Find the exact coordinates of the stationary points of the curve \\(y = \\mathrm{f}(x)\\) and determine their nature. [4]\n(b) Find the exact set of values of \\(x\\) for which the curve is convex. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: product rule: \\(\\mathrm{f}'(x) = \\mathrm{e}^x(x^2 + 2x)\\), set equal to 0\nA1: \\((0, 0)\\) and \\((-2, 4\\mathrm{e}^{-2})\\)\nM1: \\(\\mathrm{f}''(x) = \\mathrm{e}^x(x^2 + 4x + 2)\\) evaluated at a stationary point\nA1: \\(\\mathrm{f}''(0) = 2 \\gt 0\\), minimum at \\((0, 0)\\); \\(\\mathrm{f}''(-2) = -2\\mathrm{e}^{-2} \\lt 0\\), maximum at \\((-2, 4\\mathrm{e}^{-2})\\)\n(b)\nM1: convex requires \\(\\mathrm{f}''(x) \\gt 0\\), i.e. \\(x^2 + 4x + 2 \\gt 0\\) (since \\(\\mathrm{e}^x \\gt 0\\))\nA1: critical values \\(x = -2 \\pm \\sqrt{2}\\)\nA1: \\(x \\lt -2 - \\sqrt{2}\\) or \\(x \\gt -2 + \\sqrt{2}\\) (allow \\(\\leq, \\geq\\))` },
      { q: `A curve has parametric equations \\(x = t^2,\\ y = 2t\\).\n\nThe normal to the curve at the point where \\(t = 2\\) meets the curve again at the point \\(Q\\). Find the coordinates of \\(Q\\).`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{2}{2t} = \\frac{1}{t}\\)\nA1: gradient of tangent at \\(t = 2\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\); point \\((4, 4)\\)\nM1: normal gradient \\(-2\\) used with \\((4, 4)\\)\nA1: \\(y = -2x + 12\\) oe\nM1: substitutes parametric equations: \\(2t = -2t^2 + 12\\)\nA1: \\(t^2 + t - 6 = 0\\), \\(t = -3\\) (\\(t = 2\\) is the original point)\nA1: \\(Q(9, -6)\\)` },
      { q: `The temperature, \\(\\theta\\) °C, of a cup of tea \\(t\\) minutes after it is made is modelled by\n\\(\\theta = 20 + 70\\mathrm{e}^{-0.04t}\\).\n\n(a) Find the rate of change of the temperature 10 minutes after the tea is made. [3]\n(b) Find the temperature of the tea at the instant when it is cooling at a rate of 1.5 °C per minute. [3]\n(c) Comment on the long-term behaviour of the temperature predicted by the model. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} = k\\mathrm{e}^{-0.04t}\\)\nA1: \\(-2.8\\mathrm{e}^{-0.04t}\\)\nA1: \\(-1.88\\) °C per minute (i.e. falling at 1.88 °C per minute)\n(b)\nM1: \\(2.8\\mathrm{e}^{-0.04t} = 1.5\\) (or \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -0.04(\\theta - 20)\\))\nM1: \\(\\theta = 20 + 70 \\times \\frac{1.5}{2.8}\\) or finds \\(t = 15.6\\) and substitutes\nA1: 57.5 °C\n(c)\nB1: temperature tends to 20 °C, which represents room temperature; e.g. this assumes the room temperature stays constant` },
      { q: `A lorry makes a journey of 300 km at a constant speed of \\(v\\) km per hour. The total cost, £\\(C\\), of the journey is modelled by\n\\(C = \\frac{300}{v}\\left(16 + \\frac{v^2}{100}\\right)\\),\nwhere £16 per hour is the driver's wage and \\(\\frac{v^2}{100}\\) pounds per hour is the fuel cost.\n\n(a) Show that \\(C = \\frac{4800}{v} + 3v\\). [2]\n(b) Use calculus to find the speed that minimises the cost of the journey, and find this minimum cost. [4]\n(c) Show that this cost is a minimum. [1]\n(d) Comment on the suitability of the model. [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: multiplies out \\(\\frac{300}{v} \\times 16 + \\frac{300}{v} \\times \\frac{v^2}{100}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{4800}{v} + 3v\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}C}{\\mathrm{d}v} = -\\frac{4800}{v^2} + 3\\)\nM1: sets equal to 0: \\(v^2 = 1600\\)\nA1: \\(v = 40\\) km per hour\nA1: \\(C = 240\\), i.e. £240\n(c)\nB1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2C}{\\mathrm{d}v^2} = \\frac{9600}{v^3} \\gt 0\\), so minimum\n(d)\nE1: e.g. 40 km per hour is unrealistically slow for a long journey / speed cannot be constant throughout (traffic, junctions) / fuel cost is unlikely to follow this formula at all speeds` },
      { q: `A curve has equation \\(x^2 - 2xy + 3y^2 = 6\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{y - x}{3y - x}\\). [3]\n(b) Find the exact coordinates of the points on the curve where the tangent is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis. [3]\n(c) Show that the tangent to the curve at the point \\((3, 1)\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: implicit differentiation, \\(6y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\) seen\nM1: product rule on \\(2xy\\): \\(2y + 2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\)\nA1: \\(2x - 2y - 2x\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} + 6y\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 0\\) rearranged to AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(y - x = 0\\) substituted into curve\nA1: \\(2x^2 = 6\\)\nA1: \\((\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3})\\) and \\((-\\sqrt{3}, -\\sqrt{3})\\)\n(c)\nB1: verifies \\((3, 1)\\) on curve: \\(9 - 6 + 3 = 6\\)\nE1: denominator \\(3y - x = 0\\) and numerator \\(-2 \\neq 0\\), so gradient undefined: tangent is vertical (\\(x = 3\\))` },
      { q: `The function f is defined by \\(\\mathrm{f}(x) = \\frac{2x - 1}{x^2 + 2}\\) for all real \\(x\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(\\mathrm{f}'(x) = \\frac{-2(x - 2)(x + 1)}{(x^2 + 2)^2}\\). [3]\n(b) Find the coordinates of the stationary points of the curve \\(y = \\mathrm{f}(x)\\). [3]\n(c) Determine the nature of each stationary point. Fully justify your answer. [2]\n(d) Hence state the range of f. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Show', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: quotient rule, form \\(\\frac{2(x^2 + 2) - 2x(2x - 1)}{(x^2 + 2)^2}\\)\nA1: numerator \\(-2x^2 + 2x + 4\\)\nA1: \\(= -2(x^2 - x - 2) = -2(x - 2)(x + 1)\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: numerator \\(= 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 2\\), \\(x = -1\\)\nA1: \\(\\left(2, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) and \\((-1, -1)\\)\n(c)\nM1: sign of \\(\\mathrm{f}'(x)\\) either side (denominator always positive), e.g. \\(\\mathrm{f}'(0) = 1 \\gt 0\\), \\(\\mathrm{f}'(3) \\lt 0\\), \\(\\mathrm{f}'(-2) \\lt 0\\)\nA1: \\((-1, -1)\\) minimum, \\(\\left(2, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\) maximum\n(d)\nB1: \\(-1 \\leq \\mathrm{f}(x) \\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\) (since \\(\\mathrm{f}(x) \\to 0\\) as \\(x \\to \\pm\\infty\\))` },
      { q: `A section of a roller-coaster track is modelled by the curve\n\\(y = \\frac{1}{1000}(x^3 - 90x^2 + 2400x)\\), for \\(0 \\leq x \\leq 60\\),\nwhere \\(x\\) metres is the horizontal distance from the start of the section and \\(y\\) metres is the height of the track above the ground.\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of the stationary points of the curve and determine their nature. [5]\n(b) Show that the curve has a point of inflection at \\((30, 18)\\) and that the gradient of the track there is \\(-0.3\\). [3]\n(c) Find the angle that the track makes with the horizontal at this point of inflection. [1]\n(d) State one limitation of this model. [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{1}{1000}(3x^2 - 180x + 2400)\\) set equal to 0\nA1: \\(x^2 - 60x + 800 = 0\\), \\(x = 20,\\ x = 40\\)\nA1: \\((20, 20)\\) and \\((40, 16)\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2y}{\\mathrm{d}x^2} = \\frac{1}{1000}(6x - 180)\\) evaluated at a stationary point\nA1: \\(-0.06 \\lt 0\\) at \\(x = 20\\): maximum; \\(0.06 \\gt 0\\) at \\(x = 40\\): minimum\n(b)\nM1: \\(6x - 180 = 0\\) gives \\(x = 30\\), and \\(y = \\frac{27000 - 81000 + 72000}{1000} = 18\\)\nE1: second derivative changes sign at \\(x = 30\\) (negative for \\(x \\lt 30\\), positive for \\(x \\gt 30\\)), so point of inflection\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\frac{2700 - 5400 + 2400}{1000} = -0.3\\) AG\n(c)\nB1: \\(\\tan^{-1}0.3 = 16.7°\\) (below the horizontal)\n(d)\nB1: e.g. the track has width and the cars have size, so it is not a curve / the model may not join smoothly to the next section of track / the track is not necessarily in a single vertical plane` }
    ]
  },
  '1.8': {
    name: 'Integration',
    questions: [
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\left(6x^2 + \\frac{4}{x^3}\\right)dx\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: integrates at least one term correctly, e.g. \\(2x^3\\) or \\(-2x^{-2}\\) (power increased by 1 and divided by new power)\nA1: \\(2x^3 - 2x^{-2} + c\\) oe, including \\(+c\\)\nAnswer: \\(2x^3 - \\frac{2}{x^2} + c\\)` },
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\left(e^{4x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\right)dx\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{1}{4}e^{4x}\\)\nB1: \\(2\\ln|x| + c\\) (condone missing modulus; \\(+c\\) required somewhere)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{4}e^{4x} + 2\\ln|x| + c\\)` },
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\cos 2x + \\sec^2 3x\\right)dx\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x\\)\nB1: \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\tan 3x + c\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x + \\frac{1}{3}\\tan 3x + c\\)` },
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int (2x+1)^5\\,dx\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(k(2x+1)^6\\) for any constant k\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{12}(2x+1)^6 + c\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{(2x+1)^6}{12} + c\\)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^a (2x - 1)\\,dx = 6\\), where a is a constant greater than 1.\n\nFind the value of a.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: integrates to \\(x^2 - x\\)\nM1: substitutes limits correctly, \\((a^2 - a) - (1 - 1) = 6\\), and rearranges to a three-term quadratic \\(a^2 - a - 6 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(a = 3\\) only (must reject \\(a = -2\\) with reason, e.g. a greater than 1)\nAnswer: a = 3` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nShow that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^2 \\frac{x}{x^2 + 5}\\,dx = \\ln\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: integrates to \\(k\\ln(x^2+5)\\)\nA1: \\(k = \\frac{1}{2}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\ln 9 - \\frac{1}{2}\\ln 5 = \\frac{1}{2}\\ln\\frac{9}{5} = \\ln\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}\\) AG, with log laws seen (DR: numerical evaluation only scores 0)\nAnswer: \\(\\ln\\frac{3}{\\sqrt5}\\)` },
      { q: `Use a suitable trigonometric identity to find \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\sin^2 x\\,dx\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: uses \\(\\cos 2x = 1 - 2\\sin^2 x\\) to write \\(\\sin^2 x = \\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 2x)\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}x\\) term correct\nA1: \\(-\\frac{1}{4}\\sin 2x + c\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{2}x - \\frac{1}{4}\\sin 2x + c\\)` },
      { q: `Grain is being loaded into a storage silo. The volume of grain in the silo, V m³, t hours after loading begins is modelled by\n\\(\\dfrac{dV}{dt} = 6t^2 - 2t + 1\\).\nWhen t = 1, V = 5.\n\nFind an expression for V in terms of t.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: integrates, at least two terms correct: \\(2t^3 - t^2 + t\\)\nM1: uses t = 1, V = 5 to find c: \\(5 = 2 - 1 + 1 + c\\)\nA1: \\(V = 2t^3 - t^2 + t + 3\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(V = 2t^3 - t^2 + t + 3\\)` },
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nFind the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^4 \\frac{x + 2}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: splits into \\(x^{\\frac{1}{2}} + 2x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\) and integrates at least one term correctly\nA1: \\(\\frac{2}{3}x^{\\frac{3}{2}} + 4x^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)\nA1: \\(\\left(\\frac{16}{3} + 8\\right) - \\left(\\frac{2}{3} + 4\\right) = \\frac{26}{3}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{26}{3}\\)` },
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int x e^{2x}\\,dx\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: integration by parts with u = x, \\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = e^{2x}\\), giving \\(\\frac{1}{2}xe^{2x} - \\int \\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}xe^{2x}\\) and \\(-\\frac{1}{4}e^{2x}\\) correct\nA1: fully correct with \\(+c\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{2}xe^{2x} - \\frac{1}{4}e^{2x} + c\\)` },
      { q: `A water tank has a leak. The rate at which water is lost from the tank, t hours after the leak starts, is modelled as \\(40e^{-0.2t}\\) litres per hour.\n\nFind the total volume of water lost in the first 5 hours. Give your answer correct to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\int_0^5 40e^{-0.2t}\\,dt\\) attempted, integrand integrated to \\(ke^{-0.2t}\\)\nA1: \\(\\left[-200e^{-0.2t}\\right]_0^5\\)\nA1: \\(200(1 - e^{-1}) = 126\\) litres (126.42…)\nAnswer: 126 litres (3 sf)` },
      { q: `A drainage channel has a uniform cross-section. Relative to axes with the x-axis along ground level, the bed of the channel is modelled by the curve \\(y = \\frac{1}{9}x(x - 6)\\) for \\(0 \\leq x \\leq 6\\), where x and y are in metres.\n\n(a) Find the cross-sectional area of the channel. [3]\n(b) State one limitation of this model. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\int_0^6 \\frac{1}{9}(x^2 - 6x)\\,dx\\), integrates to \\(\\frac{1}{9}\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3} - 3x^2\\right]\\)\nA1: value of integral \\(= \\frac{1}{9}(72 - 108) = -4\\)\nA1: area = 4 m² (must be positive, with recognition that the region is below the axis)\n(b)\nE1: e.g. a real channel bed is not a perfect smooth curve / the cross-section will not be the same along the whole channel / erosion or silt changes the shape\nAnswer: 4 m²` },
      { q: `The curve \\(y = x^2 - 4x\\) meets the x-axis at the origin and at the point A.\n\nFind the total area of the regions enclosed by the curve, the x-axis and the line x = 5.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: A is (4, 0)\nM1: integrates separately over [0, 4] and [4, 5]: \\(\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3} - 2x^2\\right]\\)\nA1: \\(-\\frac{32}{3}\\) and \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\)\nA1: total area \\(= \\frac{32}{3} + \\frac{7}{3} = 13\\)\nNote: \\(\\int_0^5 = -\\frac{25}{3}\\) used as the area scores B1 M0 A0 A0\nAnswer: 13` },
      { q: `Use the substitution u = 2x + 1 to find \\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\sqrt{2x + 1}\\,dx\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Use', markScheme: `B1: \\(x = \\frac{u - 1}{2}\\) and \\(dx = \\frac{1}{2}du\\)\nM1: obtains integral in u only: \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\int (u^{\\frac{3}{2}} - u^{\\frac{1}{2}})\\,du\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\left(\\frac{2}{5}u^{\\frac{5}{2}} - \\frac{2}{3}u^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{10}(2x+1)^{\\frac{5}{2}} - \\frac{1}{6}(2x+1)^{\\frac{3}{2}} + c\\) (must be in terms of x)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{10}(2x+1)^{\\frac{5}{2}} - \\frac{1}{6}(2x+1)^{\\frac{3}{2}} + c\\)` },
      { q: `Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{5}{(x - 2)(2x + 1)}\\,dx\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{A}{x - 2} + \\frac{B}{2x + 1}\\) and a correct method for A or B\nA1: A = 1, B = −2\nM1: integrates to \\(p\\ln|x - 2| + q\\ln|2x + 1|\\)\nA1: \\(\\ln|x - 2| - \\ln|2x + 1| + c\\) oe, e.g. \\(\\ln\\left|\\frac{x-2}{2x+1}\\right| + c\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\ln|x - 2| - \\ln|2x + 1| + c\\)` },
      { q: `Water flows into a reservoir. Between t = 1 and t = 3, where t is the time in hours, the rate of flow is modelled as \\(2t + \\dfrac{1}{t}\\) thousand m³ per hour. The total volume entering the reservoir in this time is approximated by splitting the time into small intervals of length δt, giving\n\\(\\displaystyle\\sum_{t=1}^{3}\\left(2t + \\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\delta t\\).\n\n(a) Write \\(\\displaystyle\\lim_{\\delta t \\to 0}\\sum_{t=1}^{3}\\left(2t + \\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\delta t\\) as an integral. [1]\n(b) Hence find the exact total volume, in thousand m³, entering the reservoir between t = 1 and t = 3. [3]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(\\int_1^3 \\left(2t + \\frac{1}{t}\\right)dt\\)\n(b)\nM1: integrates to the form \\(t^2 + k\\ln t\\)\nA1: \\(\\left[t^2 + \\ln t\\right]_1^3\\)\nA1: \\(8 + \\ln 3\\) thousand m³ cao (exact)\nAnswer: \\(8 + \\ln 3\\)` },
      { q: `Do not use a calculator in this question.\n\nFind the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 x^2(x^3 + 1)^4\\,dx\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: recognises the form \\(f'(x)[f(x)]^n\\), integral of the form \\(k(x^3 + 1)^5\\) (or uses u = x³ + 1)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{15}(x^3 + 1)^5\\)\nM1: substitutes limits correctly: \\(\\frac{1}{15}(2^5 - 1^5)\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{31}{15}\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{31}{15}\\)` },
      { q: `A small company launches a new product. Its profit, £P thousand, t months after launch is modelled by\n\\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt} = 12 - 3\\sqrt{t}\\).\nAt launch, P = −20.\n\n(a) Find an expression for P in terms of t. [3]\n(b) Explain why, according to the model, the profit will eventually start to decrease. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: integrates, with \\(t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\) becoming \\(kt^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\)\nA1: \\(12t - 2t^{\\frac{3}{2}} + c\\)\nA1: \\(P = 12t - 2t^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 20\\)\n(b)\nE1: \\(\\frac{dP}{dt} \\lt 0\\) when \\(3\\sqrt{t} \\gt 12\\), i.e. for t greater than 16 months, so P decreases after 16 months\nAnswer: \\(P = 12t - 2t^{\\frac{3}{2}} - 20\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nFind the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} \\tan^2 x\\,dx\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: uses \\(\\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x - 1\\)\nA1: \\(\\tan x - x\\)\nM1: substitutes limits: \\(\\left(1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) - (0 - 0)\\)\nA1: \\(1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)\nAnswer: \\(1 - \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)` },
      { q: `A garden designer models the edge of a pond by the curve \\(y = x^2 - 2x + 3\\) and a straight path by the line y = x + 3, where x and y are measured in units of 10 metres. The region enclosed between the curve and the line is to be planted with grass.\n\nIn this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nFind the area to be planted, giving your answer in m².`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: equates \\(x^2 - 2x + 3 = x + 3\\)\nA1: x = 0 and x = 3\nM1: \\(\\int_0^3 \\left((x + 3) - (x^2 - 2x + 3)\\right)dx = \\int_0^3 (3x - x^2)\\,dx\\) and integrates\nA1: \\(\\left[\\frac{3}{2}x^2 - \\frac{1}{3}x^3\\right]_0^3 = \\frac{9}{2}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{9}{2} \\times 100 = 450\\) m²\nAnswer: 450 m²` },
      { q: `Find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^e x^2\\ln x\\,dx\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: parts with \\(u = \\ln x\\), \\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = x^2\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{3}x^3\\ln x - \\int \\frac{1}{3}x^2\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{3}x^3\\ln x - \\frac{1}{9}x^3\\)\nM1: substitutes limits, using \\(\\ln e = 1\\), \\(\\ln 1 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{2e^3 + 1}{9}\\) oe exact\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{2e^3 + 1}{9}\\)` },
      { q: `Use the substitution \\(u = e^x + 1\\) to show that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\ln 3} \\frac{e^{2x}}{e^x + 1}\\,dx = 2 - \\ln 2\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `B1: \\(\\frac{du}{dx} = e^x\\)\nM1: writes \\(e^{2x}\\,dx = e^x \\cdot e^x\\,dx = (u - 1)\\,du\\), integral \\(\\int \\frac{u - 1}{u}\\,du\\)\nB1: limits u = 2 and u = 4\nM1: \\(\\int \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{u}\\right)du = u - \\ln u\\)\nA1: \\((4 - \\ln 4) - (2 - \\ln 2) = 2 - \\ln 2\\) AG, with \\(\\ln 4 = 2\\ln 2\\) seen\nAnswer: \\(2 - \\ln 2\\)` },
      { q: `Given that \\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = 6x\\sqrt{y}\\), and that y = 4 when x = 1, find y in terms of x.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: separates variables: \\(\\int y^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,dy = \\int 6x\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(2y^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)\nA1: \\(3x^2 + c\\)\nM1: uses x = 1, y = 4: \\(4 = 3 + c\\), c = 1\nA1: \\(y = \\frac{1}{4}(3x^2 + 1)^2\\) oe\nAnswer: \\(y = \\left(\\frac{3x^2 + 1}{2}\\right)^2\\)` },
      { q: `Water drains from a tank through a hole in its base. The depth of water in the tank is h metres, t minutes after draining begins. The depth is modelled by the differential equation\n\\(\\dfrac{dh}{dt} = -0.1\\sqrt{h}\\).\nInitially the depth is 4 metres.\n\n(a) Solve the differential equation to find h in terms of t. [4]\n(b) Find the time taken for the tank to empty, according to the model. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Solve', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: separates: \\(\\int h^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\,dh = \\int -0.1\\,dt\\)\nA1: \\(2\\sqrt{h} = -0.1t + c\\)\nM1: uses t = 0, h = 4 to find c = 4\nA1: \\(h = (2 - 0.05t)^2\\)\n(b)\nB1: t = 40 minutes (FT their h)\nAnswer: \\(h = (2 - 0.05t)^2\\); 40 minutes` },
      { q: `Use the substitution \\(x = 2\\sin\\theta\\) to find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\frac{x^2}{\\sqrt{4 - x^2}}\\,dx\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Use', markScheme: `M1: \\(dx = 2\\cos\\theta\\,d\\theta\\) and \\(\\sqrt{4 - 4\\sin^2\\theta} = 2\\cos\\theta\\)\nA1: integral becomes \\(\\int 4\\sin^2\\theta\\,d\\theta\\)\nB1: limits \\(\\theta = 0\\) and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\)\nM1: uses \\(\\sin^2\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}(1 - \\cos 2\\theta)\\) and integrates to \\(2\\theta - \\sin 2\\theta\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3} - \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nShow that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_2^4 \\frac{x + 5}{(x - 1)(x + 2)}\\,dx\\) can be written in the form \\(\\ln a\\), where a is an integer to be determined.`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: \\(\\frac{A}{x - 1} + \\frac{B}{x + 2}\\), correct method for A or B\nA1: A = 2, B = −1\nM1: integrates to \\(2\\ln|x - 1| - \\ln|x + 2|\\) (FT their A, B)\nM1: substitutes limits and combines logs: \\((2\\ln 3 - \\ln 6) - (0 - \\ln 4) = \\ln\\frac{9 \\times 4}{6}\\)\nA1: \\(\\ln 6\\), so a = 6\nAnswer: a = 6` },
      { q: `After a patient is given a dose of a drug, the concentration of the drug in the blood is C mg per litre, t hours later. The rate of decrease of C is proportional to C. Initially C = 8, and when t = 3, C = 5.\n\n(a) Write down a differential equation connecting C, t and a positive constant k. [1]\n(b) By solving your differential equation, show that \\(C = 8e^{-kt}\\) and find the value of k. [3]\n(c) The drug is no longer effective when the concentration falls below 1 mg per litre. Find, according to the model, how long the drug remains effective. [1]\n(d) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(\\frac{dC}{dt} = -kC\\)\n(b)\nM1: separates and integrates: \\(\\ln C = -kt + c\\)\nA1: uses C = 8 at t = 0 to obtain \\(C = 8e^{-kt}\\) AG\nA1: \\(5 = 8e^{-3k}\\), \\(k = \\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\frac{8}{5} = 0.157\\) (0.15667…)\n(c)\nB1: \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 8}{k} = 13.3\\) hours (FT their k)\n(d)\nE1: e.g. the model assumes the drug is absorbed instantly / ignores further doses / the rate may depend on the patient, so k varies between people\nAnswer: k = 0.157; 13.3 hours` },
      { q: `Find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} x^2\\sin x\\,dx\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: first application of parts with \\(u = x^2\\): \\(-x^2\\cos x + \\int 2x\\cos x\\,dx\\)\nA1: correct\nM1: second application of parts with u = 2x\nA1: \\(-x^2\\cos x + 2x\\sin x + 2\\cos x\\)\nM1: substitutes both limits correctly: \\((0 + \\pi + 0) - (0 + 0 + 2)\\)\nA1: \\(\\pi - 2\\)\nAnswer: \\(\\pi - 2\\)` },
      { q: `The curves \\(y = \\sin x\\) and \\(y = \\cos x\\) are drawn for \\(0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi\\).\n\n(a) Show that the curves intersect when \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and when \\(x = \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\). [2]\n(b) In this part you must show detailed reasoning.\nFind the exact area of the region enclosed between the two curves for \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4} \\leq x \\leq \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\). [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\sin x = \\cos x \\Rightarrow \\tan x = 1\\)\nA1: \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(x = \\pi + \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{5\\pi}{4}\\), both in range, AG\n(b)\nB1: \\(\\sin x \\geq \\cos x\\) on this interval, so area \\(= \\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}^{\\frac{5\\pi}{4}} (\\sin x - \\cos x)\\,dx\\)\nM1: integrates to \\(-\\cos x - \\sin x\\)\nM1: substitutes limits with exact values: \\(\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) - \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\)\nA1: \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\)\nAnswer: \\(2\\sqrt{2}\\)` },
      { q: `Find the particular solution of the differential equation\n\\(\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = e^{x - y}\\)\nfor which y = 1 when x = 0. Give your answer in the form y = f(x), and determine the limit of y as \\(x \\to -\\infty\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: writes \\(e^{x - y} = e^x e^{-y}\\) and separates: \\(\\int e^y\\,dy = \\int e^x\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(e^y = e^x + c\\)\nM1: uses x = 0, y = 1\nA1: \\(c = e - 1\\)\nA1: \\(y = \\ln(e^x + e - 1)\\)\nB1: as \\(x \\to -\\infty\\), \\(e^x \\to 0\\) so \\(y \\to \\ln(e - 1)\\)\nAnswer: \\(y = \\ln(e^x + e - 1)\\); limit \\(\\ln(e - 1)\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Express \\(\\dfrac{5x^2 + 4x + 2}{x^2(x + 1)}\\) in partial fractions. [3]\n(b) Hence show that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^2 \\frac{5x^2 + 4x + 2}{x^2(x + 1)}\\,dx = 1 + \\ln\\frac{27}{2}\\). [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Express', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{A}{x} + \\frac{B}{x^2} + \\frac{C}{x + 1}\\) and a correct method for at least one constant\nA1: two of A = 2, B = 2, C = 3\nA1: all three correct\n(b)\nM1: integrates to \\(A\\ln x - \\frac{B}{x} + C\\ln(x + 1)\\) (FT)\nM1: substitutes limits: \\(2\\ln 2 + (-1 + 2) + 3\\ln 3 - 3\\ln 2\\)\nA1: \\(1 + 3\\ln 3 - \\ln 2 = 1 + \\ln\\frac{27}{2}\\) AG, log laws seen\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{2}{x} + \\frac{2}{x^2} + \\frac{3}{x + 1}\\)` },
      { q: `The front face of a stone bridge is a rectangle 24 m wide and 8 m high with an arch-shaped opening cut out of it. With the x-axis along the ground and the y-axis through the centre of the arch, the arch is modelled by\n\\(y = 6 - 0.06x^2\\) for \\(y \\geq 0\\),\nwhere x and y are in metres. The bridge is 3 m thick, with the same cross-section throughout.\n\n(a) Show that the arch meets the ground where x = ±10. [1]\n(b) Find the area of the opening under the arch. [3]\n(c) Hence find the volume of stone in the bridge. [1]\n(d) Comment on the suitability of the model for finding the volume of stone needed. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(6 - 0.06x^2 = 0 \\Rightarrow x^2 = 100\\), x = ±10 AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\int_{-10}^{10} (6 - 0.06x^2)\\,dx\\) and integrates\nA1: \\(\\left[6x - 0.02x^3\\right]_{-10}^{10}\\)\nA1: 80 m²\n(c)\nB1: \\((24 \\times 8 - 80) \\times 3 = 336\\) m³ (FT their area)\n(d)\nE1: e.g. a real arch may not be exactly parabolic / the face may not be a perfect rectangle / ignores mortar, hollow fill or decorative stonework, so the volume is only an estimate\nAnswer: 80 m²; 336 m³` },
      { q: `An ecologist models the population, P, of an insect species t months after the start of a study. The model assumes that\n\\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt} = 0.2P\\cos(0.5t)\\),\nand that P = 500 when t = 0.\n\n(a) Show that \\(P = 500e^{0.4\\sin(0.5t)}\\). [4]\n(b) Find the maximum population predicted by the model. [2]\n(c) Comment on the suitability of this model for predicting the population over many years. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: separates: \\(\\int \\frac{1}{P}\\,dP = \\int 0.2\\cos(0.5t)\\,dt\\)\nA1: \\(\\ln P = 0.4\\sin(0.5t) + c\\)\nM1: uses t = 0, P = 500: \\(c = \\ln 500\\)\nA1: \\(P = 500e^{0.4\\sin(0.5t)}\\) AG, with correct exponential step\n(b)\nM1: maximum when \\(\\sin(0.5t) = 1\\)\nA1: \\(500e^{0.4} = 746\\) (745.9…)\n(c)\nE1: e.g. the model predicts the population oscillating between about 335 and 746 forever, which ignores long-term changes such as disease, predators or habitat loss; reasonable only for a short period\nAnswer: maximum 746` },
      { q: `The curve C has equation \\(y = xe^{-x}\\).\n\n(a) Find the exact coordinates of the stationary point of C. [3]\n(b) In this part you must show detailed reasoning.\nFind the exact area of the region bounded by C, the x-axis and the line x = 2. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: product rule: \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{-x} - xe^{-x}\\)\nM1: sets \\(e^{-x}(1 - x) = 0\\)\nA1: \\((1, e^{-1})\\)\n(b)\nM1: parts with u = x, \\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = e^{-x}\\)\nA1: \\(-xe^{-x} - e^{-x}\\)\nM1: substitutes limits 0 and 2 correctly\nA1: \\(1 - 3e^{-2}\\)\nAnswer: \\((1, \\frac{1}{e})\\); area \\(1 - 3e^{-2}\\)` },
      { q: `(a) Use the substitution \\(u = 1 + \\sqrt{x}\\) to show that \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 \\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}}\\,dx = 4 - 2\\ln 3\\). [5]\n(b) Hence find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^4 \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1 + \\sqrt{x}}\\,dx\\). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(x = (u - 1)^2\\), \\(dx = 2(u - 1)\\,du\\) oe\nB1: limits u = 1 and u = 3\nM1: integral in u only: \\(\\int \\frac{2(u - 1)}{u}\\,du = \\int \\left(2 - \\frac{2}{u}\\right)du\\)\nA1: \\(2u - 2\\ln u\\)\nA1: \\((6 - 2\\ln 3) - (2 - 0) = 4 - 2\\ln 3\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: writes \\(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{1 + \\sqrt{x}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1 + \\sqrt{x}}\\)\nA1: \\(4 - (4 - 2\\ln 3) = 2\\ln 3\\) (or ln 9)\nAnswer: \\(2\\ln 3\\)` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nThe curve \\(y = x^2 - 6x + 5\\) and the line y = x − 1 intersect at two points.\n\nFind the exact area of the region enclosed between the curve and the line.`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1: equates and rearranges: \\(x^2 - 7x + 6 = 0\\)\nA1: x = 1 and x = 6\nM1: area \\(= \\int_1^6 \\left((x - 1) - (x^2 - 6x + 5)\\right)dx\\) (line above curve)\nA1: integrand \\(-x^2 + 7x - 6\\)\nM1: integrates: \\(\\left[-\\frac{x^3}{3} + \\frac{7x^2}{2} - 6x\\right]\\)\nA1: \\(18 - \\left(-\\frac{17}{6}\\right)\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{125}{6}\\)\nNote: no need to split at the x-axis, since the difference of the two functions is used\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{125}{6}\\)` },
      { q: `A biologist models the population of bacteria in a culture, P thousand, t hours after the start of an experiment, by\n\\(\\dfrac{dP}{dt} = \\dfrac{1}{8}P(4 - P)\\).\nInitially P = 1.\n\n(a) Express \\(\\dfrac{1}{P(4 - P)}\\) in partial fractions. [2]\n(b) Hence show that \\(P = \\dfrac{4}{1 + 3e^{-\\frac{1}{2}t}}\\). [5]\n(c) State what happens to the population in the long term, according to the model. [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: \\(\\frac{A}{P} + \\frac{B}{4 - P}\\), correct method\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{4P} + \\frac{1}{4(4 - P)}\\)\n(b)\nM1: separates: \\(\\int \\frac{1}{P(4 - P)}\\,dP = \\int \\frac{1}{8}\\,dt\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\ln P - \\frac{1}{4}\\ln(4 - P) = \\frac{1}{8}t + c\\) (FT their partial fractions)\nM1: uses t = 0, P = 1: \\(c = \\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\frac{1}{3}\\)\nM1: combines logs and exponentiates: \\(\\frac{P}{4 - P} = \\frac{1}{3}e^{\\frac{1}{2}t}\\)\nA1: rearranges to \\(P = \\frac{4}{1 + 3e^{-\\frac{1}{2}t}}\\) AG\n(c)\nB1: as \\(t \\to \\infty\\), \\(P \\to 4\\): the population levels off at 4000 bacteria\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{1}{4P} + \\frac{1}{4(4-P)}\\); P tends to 4 thousand` },
      { q: `(a) Find \\(\\displaystyle\\int x\\cos 2x\\,dx\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}} x\\cos^2 x\\,dx\\). [5]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1: parts with u = x, \\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = \\cos 2x\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}x\\sin 2x - \\int \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2x\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}x\\sin 2x + \\frac{1}{4}\\cos 2x + c\\)\n(b)\nM1: uses \\(\\cos^2 x = \\frac{1}{2}(1 + \\cos 2x)\\) to write integrand as \\(\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2}x\\cos 2x\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{4}x^2\\) for the first term\nM1: uses their answer to (a) with limits 0 and \\(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\left[\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{8} + 0\\right) - \\left(0 + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\right] = \\frac{\\pi}{16} - \\frac{1}{8}\\)\nA1: \\(\\frac{\\pi^2}{64} + \\frac{\\pi}{16} - \\frac{1}{8}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{\\pi^2}{64} + \\frac{\\pi}{16} - \\frac{1}{8}\\)` },
      { q: `A cup of coffee is left to cool in a room where the temperature is a constant 20 °C. The temperature of the coffee is θ °C, t minutes after it is made. The rate of decrease of θ is proportional to the difference between θ and the room temperature. When t = 0, θ = 90, and when t = 5, θ = 60.\n\n(a) By setting up and solving a differential equation, show that \\(\\theta = 20 + 70e^{-kt}\\), where k is a positive constant. [4]\n(b) Find the exact value of k. [2]\n(c) Find the time taken for the coffee to cool to 40 °C. Give your answer to the nearest tenth of a minute. [2]\n(d) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: \\(\\frac{d\\theta}{dt} = -k(\\theta - 20)\\)\nM1: separates and integrates: \\(\\ln|\\theta - 20| = -kt + c\\)\nM1: uses t = 0, θ = 90: \\(c = \\ln 70\\)\nA1: \\(\\theta = 20 + 70e^{-kt}\\) AG\n(b)\nM1: \\(60 = 20 + 70e^{-5k}\\), so \\(e^{-5k} = \\frac{4}{7}\\)\nA1: \\(k = \\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\frac{7}{4}\\) (0.1119…)\n(c)\nM1: \\(40 = 20 + 70e^{-kt}\\), \\(e^{-kt} = \\frac{2}{7}\\), \\(t = \\frac{\\ln 3.5}{k}\\)\nA1: 11.2 minutes (11.19…)\n(d)\nE1: e.g. room temperature will not stay exactly constant / the coffee does not cool uniformly / stirring, adding milk or the cup itself are ignored\nAnswer: \\(k = \\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\frac{7}{4}\\); 11.2 minutes` },
      { q: `(a) Show that \\(\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x\\ln x - x) = \\ln x\\), and hence write down \\(\\displaystyle\\int \\ln x\\,dx\\). [2]\n(b) Show that the curves \\(y = \\ln x\\) and \\(y = 2 - \\ln x\\) meet at exactly one point, and find its exact coordinates. [2]\n(c) In this part you must show detailed reasoning.\nFind the exact area of the region bounded by the curves \\(y = \\ln x\\), \\(y = 2 - \\ln x\\) and the line x = 1. [3]\n(d) Use integration by parts to find the exact value of \\(\\displaystyle\\int_1^e (\\ln x)^2\\,dx\\). [3]`, marks: 10, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1: product rule: \\(\\ln x + x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - 1 = \\ln x\\) AG\nB1: \\(\\int \\ln x\\,dx = x\\ln x - x + c\\)\n(b)\nM1: \\(\\ln x = 2 - \\ln x \\Rightarrow \\ln x = 1\\); one solution since ln is one-one\nA1: \\((e, 1)\\)\n(c)\nM1: area \\(= \\int_1^e \\left((2 - \\ln x) - \\ln x\\right)dx = \\int_1^e (2 - 2\\ln x)\\,dx\\)\nM1: uses (a): \\(\\left[2x - 2(x\\ln x - x)\\right]_1^e = \\left[4x - 2x\\ln x\\right]_1^e\\)\nA1: \\(2e - 4\\)\n(d)\nM1: parts with \\(u = (\\ln x)^2\\), \\(\\frac{dv}{dx} = 1\\): \\(x(\\ln x)^2 - \\int 2\\ln x\\,dx\\)\nA1: \\(x(\\ln x)^2 - 2x\\ln x + 2x\\)\nA1: \\((e - 2e + 2e) - 2 = e - 2\\)\nAnswer: (e, 1); area \\(2e - 4\\); \\(e - 2\\)` }
    ]
  },
  '1.9': {
    name: 'Numerical Methods',
    questions: [
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = 9x^2 - 9x + 2\\). A student calculates \\(f(0) = 2\\) and \\(f(1) = 2\\) and concludes that the equation \\(f(x) = 0\\) has no roots in the interval \\(0 < x < 1\\).\n\nBy evaluating \\(f(0.5)\\), explain why the student's conclusion is incorrect.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `B1: \\(f(0.5) = 2.25 - 4.5 + 2 = -0.25\\)\nE1: f is continuous and changes sign between 0 and 0.5 and again between 0.5 and 1, so there are (at least) two roots in \\(0 < x < 1\\); no sign change at the end-points does not mean no root\nAnswer: roots are in fact \\(x = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and \\(x = \\frac{2}{3}\\)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 - 4x - 1 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha\\) between 2 and 3. The iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt[3]{4x_n + 1}\\) is used with \\(x_1 = 2\\).\n\nFind the values of \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\), giving each to 4 decimal places.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1: \\(x_2 = \\sqrt[3]{9} = 2.0801\\)\nB1: \\(x_3 = \\sqrt[3]{9.32034\\ldots} = 2.1045\\)\nAnswer: \\(x_2 = 2.0801\\), \\(x_3 = 2.1045\\) (cao, 4 d.p.)` },
      { q: `The trapezium rule with 4 strips gives 1.4907 (to 4 d.p.) as an approximation to \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 e^{x^2}\\,dx\\).\n\nExplain, with reference to the shape of the curve \\(y = e^{x^2}\\), whether 1.4907 is an over-estimate or an under-estimate of the integral.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `B1: The curve is convex for \\(0 \\le x \\le 1\\) (e.g. \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = (2 + 4x^2)e^{x^2} > 0\\), or gradient increasing)\nE1: So the tops of the trapezia lie above the curve, and 1.4907 is an over-estimate\nAnswer: over-estimate (true value 1.4627)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = x^3 - 3x + 1\\). A student attempts to use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 1\\) to find a root of \\(f(x) = 0\\).\n\nExplain why the method fails with this starting value.`, marks: 2, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `B1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3\\), so \\(f'(1) = 0\\)\nE1: The tangent at \\(x = 1\\) is horizontal (x = 1 is a stationary point), so it never meets the x-axis; \\(x_1 = x_0 - \\frac{f(x_0)}{f'(x_0)}\\) requires division by zero and cannot be found` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 - 2x - 5 = 0\\) has exactly one real root \\(\\alpha\\).\n\nShow that \\(\\alpha = 2.09\\) correct to 2 decimal places.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: Let \\(f(x) = x^3 - 2x - 5\\) and evaluate f at both 2.085 and 2.095\nA1: \\(f(2.085) = -0.106\\ldots < 0\\) and \\(f(2.095) = 0.00500\\ldots > 0\\)\nE1: Sign change and f is continuous, so \\(2.085 < \\alpha < 2.095\\), hence \\(\\alpha = 2.09\\) to 2 d.p. AG\nNote: testing f(2.09) and f(2.10) alone scores M0` },
      { q: `The curves \\(y = \\ln x\\) and \\(y = 3 - x\\) meet at a single point whose x-coordinate is \\(\\alpha\\).\n\nShow that \\(2.2 < \\alpha < 2.3\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: Form a single function, e.g. \\(f(x) = \\ln x + x - 3\\), and evaluate at 2.2 and 2.3\nA1: \\(f(2.2) = -0.0115\\ldots\\) and \\(f(2.3) = 0.133\\ldots\\)\nE1: Change of sign and f continuous for \\(x > 0\\), so \\(2.2 < \\alpha < 2.3\\) AG` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^4 - 3x - 5 = 0\\) has a positive root \\(\\beta\\).\n\nUse the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 1.8\\) to find \\(x_1\\), giving your answer to 4 decimal places.`, marks: 3, command: 'Use', markScheme: `M1: \\(f'(x) = 4x^3 - 3\\)\nM1: \\(x_1 = 1.8 - \\frac{1.8^4 - 3(1.8) - 5}{4(1.8)^3 - 3} = 1.8 - \\frac{0.0976}{20.328}\\)\nA1: \\(x_1 = 1.7952\\) cao\nAnswer: 1.7952` },
      { q: `(a) Show that the equation \\(x^3 + x - 7 = 0\\) can be written in the form \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{7 - x}\\). [1]\n(b) Use the iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt[3]{7 - x_n}\\) with \\(x_1 = 2\\) to find \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\), giving each to 4 decimal places. [2]`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) B1: \\(x^3 = 7 - x\\) so \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{7 - x}\\) AG\n(b) B1: \\(x_2 = \\sqrt[3]{5} = 1.7100\\)\nB1: \\(x_3 = 1.7424\\)\nAnswer: 1.7100, 1.7424` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 + x - 7 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha = 1.739\\) correct to 3 decimal places. A student suggests rearranging the equation as \\(x = 7 - x^3\\) and using the iteration \\(x_{n+1} = 7 - x_n^3\\).\n\nExplain why this iteration will not converge to \\(\\alpha\\), however close to \\(\\alpha\\) the starting value is.`, marks: 3, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `M1: \\(g(x) = 7 - x^3\\) so \\(g'(x) = -3x^2\\)\nA1: \\(g'(1.739) = -9.07\\ldots\\)\nE1: \\(|g'(\\alpha)| > 1\\), so successive iterates move further from \\(\\alpha\\) (diverging cobweb); the iteration does not converge` },
      { q: `Water flows into a tank. The rate of flow, \\(r\\) litres per minute, is measured every 2 minutes. (data written for this practice question)\n\nt (minutes): 0, 2, 4, 6\nr (litres per minute): 5.0, 6.2, 6.8, 7.0\n\nThe volume of water that flows in during the 6 minutes is given by \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^6 r\\,dt\\). Use the trapezium rule with 3 strips to estimate this volume.`, marks: 3, command: 'Use', markScheme: `B1: \\(h = 2\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{2}{2}\\left[5.0 + 7.0 + 2(6.2 + 6.8)\\right]\\) (correct structure)\nA1: 38 litres\nAnswer: 38 litres` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 + 8x - 4\\). A student finds that \\(f(1.5)\\) and \\(f(2.5)\\) are both positive and concludes that there is no root of \\(f(x) = 0\\) between 1.5 and 2.5.\n\nShow that the student is wrong, and explain why the change of sign method did not detect the root.`, marks: 3, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `M1: \\(f(2) = 8 - 20 + 16 - 4 = 0\\), or factorise \\(f(x) = (x - 1)(x - 2)^2\\)\nA1: So \\(x = 2\\) is a root in the interval (with \\(f(1.5) = 0.125\\), \\(f(2.5) = 0.375\\))\nE1: \\(x = 2\\) is a repeated root: the curve touches the x-axis without crossing it, so there is no change of sign` },
      { q: `A designer models the shape of a logo as the region bounded by the curve \\(y = \\sqrt{1 + x^3}\\), the x-axis and the lines \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\), where units are centimetres.\n\nUse the trapezium rule with 4 strips to estimate the area of the logo, giving your answer to 3 significant figures.`, marks: 4, command: 'Use', markScheme: `B1: \\(h = 0.5\\)\nB1: y-values 1, 1.0607, 1.4142, 2.0917, 3 (at least 3 correct)\nM1: \\(\\frac{0.5}{2}\\left[1 + 3 + 2(1.0607 + 1.4142 + 2.0917)\\right]\\)\nA1: 3.28 cm\\(^2\\) cao\nAnswer: 3.28 cm\\(^2\\)` },
      { q: `An open box is made from a rectangular sheet of card 20 cm by 12 cm by cutting a square of side \\(x\\) cm from each corner and folding up the sides. The volume of the box is to be 200 cm\\(^3\\). This leads to the equation \\(x^3 - 16x^2 + 60x - 50 = 0\\).\n\nUse the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 1\\) to find a value of \\(x\\) correct to 3 decimal places. Show the result of each iteration.`, marks: 4, command: 'Use', markScheme: `M1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 - 32x + 60\\)\nM1: Correct use of \\(x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\\) at least once\nA1: \\(x_1 = 1.16129\\ldots\\), \\(x_2 = 1.17371\\ldots\\)\nA1: \\(x_3 = 1.17379\\ldots\\), so \\(x = 1.174\\) (3 d.p.) cao\nAnswer: 1.174 cm` },
      { q: `The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of a drug in a patient's blood \\(t\\) hours after a dose is modelled by \\(C = 12te^{-0.5t}\\). The concentration rises to a maximum and then falls. The drug is effective while \\(C \\ge 4\\).\n\n(a) Show that the concentration falls to 4 mg per litre at a time between 5.6 hours and 5.7 hours. [3]\n(b) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: Evaluate C (or \\(C - 4\\)) at both 5.6 and 5.7\nA1: \\(C(5.6) = 4.086\\ldots\\), \\(C(5.7) = 3.956\\ldots\\)\nE1: \\(C - 4\\) changes sign and C is continuous, so \\(C = 4\\) for some t between 5.6 and 5.7 AG\n(b) B1: e.g. the model gives C never exactly 0 / ignores further doses / assumes the same response for every patient` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 + x - 7 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha = 1.739\\) correct to 3 d.p. The iteration \\(x_{n+1} = g(x_n)\\), where \\(g(x) = \\sqrt[3]{7 - x}\\), is used with \\(x_1 = 2\\).\n\n(a) By finding \\(g'(\\alpha)\\), explain whether the iteration converges and whether it produces a staircase or a cobweb diagram. [2]\n(b) Sketch the graphs of \\(y = x\\) and \\(y = g(x)\\) for \\(1.5 \\le x \\le 2\\), and show on your sketch how \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\) are obtained. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(g'(x) = -\\frac{1}{3}(7 - x)^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\), \\(g'(1.739) = -0.110\\ldots\\)\nE1: \\(|g'(\\alpha)| < 1\\) so it converges; \\(g'(\\alpha) < 0\\) so a cobweb diagram\n(b) B1: Line \\(y = x\\) and decreasing curve \\(y = g(x)\\) crossing at \\(\\alpha\\)\nB1: Vertical/horizontal steps from \\(x_1 = 2\\) spiralling inwards, with \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\) on opposite sides of \\(\\alpha\\)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 - 4x + 1 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha \\approx 1.861\\).\n\n(a) Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 1.2\\) to find \\(x_1\\). [2]\n(b) Explain why \\(x_1\\) is a poor approximation to \\(\\alpha\\), referring to the curve \\(y = x^3 - 4x + 1\\). [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 - 4\\), \\(x_1 = 1.2 - \\frac{-2.072}{0.32}\\)\nA1: \\(x_1 = 7.675\\)\n(b) E1: \\(f'(1.2) = 0.32\\) is close to zero: \\(x_0\\) is near the stationary point at \\(x = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\approx 1.155\\)\nE1: The tangent at \\(x_0\\) is nearly horizontal, so it meets the x-axis far from the root` },
      { q: `A cyclist starts from rest. Her speed, \\(v\\) m s\\(^{-1}\\), is recorded every 10 seconds for one minute. (data written for this practice question)\n\nt (s): 0, 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60\nv (m s\\(^{-1}\\)): 0, 4.2, 6.8, 8.4, 9.3, 9.8, 10.0\n\nHer speed increases at a decreasing rate throughout.\n\n(a) Use the trapezium rule with 6 strips to estimate the distance she travels in the minute. [3]\n(b) State, with a reason, whether your answer is an over-estimate or an under-estimate. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: \\(h = 10\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{10}{2}\\left[0 + 10.0 + 2(4.2 + 6.8 + 8.4 + 9.3 + 9.8)\\right]\\)\nA1: 435 m\n(b) E1: Under-estimate, because the velocity–time graph is concave (gradient decreasing), so the chords lie below the curve` },
      { q: `The equation \\(\\ln x + x - 3 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha\\). The iteration \\(x_{n+1} = 3 - \\ln x_n\\) is used with \\(x_1 = 2.2\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(\\alpha\\) correct to 3 decimal places, showing the result of each iteration to 5 decimal places. [3]\n(b) State, with a reason, whether the iteration gives a staircase or a cobweb diagram. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1: Correct use of the iteration at least once\nA1: \\(x_2 = 2.21154\\), \\(x_3 = 2.20631\\), \\(x_4 = 2.20868\\), \\(x_5 = 2.20761\\), \\(x_6 = 2.20809\\), \\(x_7 = 2.20787\\)\nA1: \\(\\alpha = 2.208\\) (two successive iterates agree to 3 d.p., or sign change on [2.2075, 2.2085])\n(b) E1: Cobweb, since \\(g'(x) = -\\frac{1}{x} < 0\\) (iterates alternate either side of \\(\\alpha\\))` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = \\tan x - x\\), where \\(x\\) is in radians.\n\n(a) Show that \\(f(1.5)\\) and \\(f(1.6)\\) have opposite signs. [2]\n(b) Explain why this does not show that the equation \\(f(x) = 0\\) has a root between 1.5 and 1.6. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Explain', markScheme: `(a) M1: Evaluate both in radians\nA1: \\(f(1.5) = 12.6\\ldots > 0\\), \\(f(1.6) = -35.8\\ldots < 0\\)\n(b) E1: \\(\\tan x\\) is undefined at \\(x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\approx 1.571\\), which lies in the interval\nE1: f is not continuous on [1.5, 1.6]; the sign change is caused by the vertical asymptote, not a root` },
      { q: `(a) Show that the equation \\(x^5 + x - 3 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\alpha\\) in the interval \\(1 < x < 1.2\\). [2]\n(b) Use the iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt[5]{3 - x_n}\\) with \\(x_1 = 1\\) to find \\(\\alpha\\) correct to 3 decimal places, showing the result of each iteration to 5 decimal places. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(1) = -1\\) and \\(f(1.2) = 0.688\\ldots\\)\nA1: Sign change and f continuous, so root in (1, 1.2) AG\n(b) M1: Correct use of the iteration\nA1: \\(x_2 = 1.14870\\), \\(x_3 = 1.13109\\), \\(x_4 = 1.13323\\), \\(x_5 = 1.13297\\), \\(x_6 = 1.13300\\)\nA1: \\(\\alpha = 1.133\\) cao` },
      { q: `(a) Show that applying the Newton–Raphson method to \\(f(x) = x^2 - 10\\) gives the iterative formula \\(x_{n+1} = \\frac{x_n^2 + 10}{2x_n}\\). [2]\n(b) Use this formula with \\(x_0 = 3\\) to find \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\), and compare \\(x_2\\) with the value of \\(\\sqrt{10}\\). [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f'(x) = 2x\\), \\(x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{x_n^2 - 10}{2x_n}\\)\nA1: \\(= \\frac{2x_n^2 - x_n^2 + 10}{2x_n} = \\frac{x_n^2 + 10}{2x_n}\\) AG\n(b) M1: \\(x_1 = \\frac{19}{6}\\)\nA1: \\(x_1 = 3.16667\\), \\(x_2 = 3.16228\\) (\\(\\frac{721}{228}\\))\nB1: \\(\\sqrt{10} = 3.162278\\ldots\\), so \\(x_2\\) is correct to 5 d.p. (error about \\(3 \\times 10^{-6}\\))` },
      { q: `(a) Use the trapezium rule with 4 strips to find an approximation to \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^1 \\frac{4}{1 + x^2}\\,dx\\), giving your answer to 4 decimal places. [3]\n(b) Given that the exact value of the integral is \\(\\pi\\), find the percentage error in your approximation. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: y-values 4, 3.76471, 3.2, 2.56, 2\nM1: \\(\\frac{0.25}{2}\\left[4 + 2 + 2(3.76471 + 3.2 + 2.56)\\right]\\)\nA1: 3.1312\n(b) M1: \\(\\frac{3.1312 - \\pi}{\\pi} \\times 100\\)\nA1: \\(-0.332\\%\\) (an under-estimate by 0.332%; accept 0.33%)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 - 6x + 2 = 0\\) has a root \\(\\beta\\) with \\(\\beta \\approx 2.26\\). Two rearrangements are suggested:\n\nA: \\(x = \\frac{x^3 + 2}{6}\\)\nB: \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{6x - 2}\\)\n\nDetermine which of the iterations \\(x_{n+1} = \\frac{x_n^3 + 2}{6}\\) and \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt[3]{6x_n - 2}\\) will converge to \\(\\beta\\) from a starting value close to \\(\\beta\\). Fully justify your answer.`, marks: 5, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `M1: For A, \\(g'(x) = \\frac{x^2}{2}\\)\nA1: \\(g'(2.26) = 2.55\\ldots > 1\\), so A diverges\nM1: For B, \\(g'(x) = 2(6x - 2)^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\)\nA1: \\(g'(2.26) = 0.391\\ldots\\)\nE1: \\(|g'(\\beta)| < 1\\) for B only, so B converges to \\(\\beta\\) (staircase, since \\(g' > 0\\)) and A does not` },
      { q: `The opening of a stone bridge arch is 12 m wide. Its height, \\(h\\) m, above the water is measured at 2 m intervals across the opening. (data written for this practice question)\n\nx (m): 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\nh (m): 0, 3.1, 4.6, 5.0, 4.6, 3.1, 0\n\n(a) Use the trapezium rule with all the values in the table to estimate the area of the opening. [3]\n(b) The arch is a smooth curve which is concave throughout. State, with a reason, whether your estimate is too large or too small. [1]\n(c) State how the estimate could be made more accurate. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: \\(h = 2\\) (strip width)\nM1: \\(\\frac{2}{2}\\left[0 + 0 + 2(3.1 + 4.6 + 5.0 + 4.6 + 3.1)\\right]\\)\nA1: 40.8 m\\(^2\\)\n(b) E1: Too small: the curve is concave so the straight tops of the trapezia lie below the arch\n(c) B1: Measure heights at smaller intervals (use more strips)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = x^3 - 2x + 2\\).\n\n(a) Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 0\\) to find \\(x_1\\) and \\(x_2\\). [3]\n(b) Explain what will happen if the method is continued. [1]\n(c) Show that \\(f(x) = 0\\) has a root between \\(-2\\) and \\(-1\\). [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 - 2\\) and correct use of formula\nA1: \\(x_1 = 0 - \\frac{2}{-2} = 1\\)\nA1: \\(x_2 = 1 - \\frac{1}{1} = 0\\)\n(b) E1: The iterates oscillate between 0 and 1 for ever, so the method never converges to the root\n(c) B1: \\(f(-2) = -2 < 0\\), \\(f(-1) = 3 > 0\\), sign change and f continuous, so a root in \\((-2, -1)\\)` },
      { q: `Two populations of bacteria are grown. After \\(t\\) hours the number in population A is modelled by \\(500e^{0.1t}\\) and the number in population B by \\(2000 + 300t\\).\n\n(a) Show that the populations are equal when \\(t = 10\\ln(4 + 0.6t)\\). [2]\n(b) Use the iteration \\(t_{n+1} = 10\\ln(4 + 0.6t_n)\\) with \\(t_1 = 20\\) to find the value of \\(t\\) at which the populations are equal, correct to 1 decimal place. [2]\n(c) State one limitation of the model for population A. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(500e^{0.1t} = 2000 + 300t \\Rightarrow e^{0.1t} = 4 + 0.6t\\)\nA1: \\(0.1t = \\ln(4 + 0.6t)\\), so \\(t = 10\\ln(4 + 0.6t)\\) AG\n(b) M1: \\(t_2 = 27.726\\), \\(t_3 = 30.270\\), \\(t_4 = 30.984\\), …\nA1: \\(t_6 = 31.226\\), \\(t_7 = 31.239\\), so \\(t = 31.2\\) hours\n(c) B1: e.g. exponential growth cannot continue indefinitely (limited food/space)` },
      { q: `The curves \\(y = e^x\\) and \\(y = 4 - x^2\\) meet at a point with positive x-coordinate \\(\\alpha\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(1 < \\alpha < 1.1\\). [2]\n(b) Show that \\(\\alpha\\) satisfies the equation \\(x = \\ln(4 - x^2)\\). [1]\n(c) Use the iteration \\(x_{n+1} = \\ln(4 - x_n^2)\\) with \\(x_1 = 1\\) to find \\(x_2\\), \\(x_3\\) and \\(x_4\\), giving each to 4 decimal places. [2]\n(d) Explain, with reference to your values, whether the iteration would be shown by a staircase or a cobweb diagram. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(x) = e^x + x^2 - 4\\); \\(f(1) = -0.282\\), \\(f(1.1) = 0.214\\)\nA1: Sign change and f continuous, so \\(1 < \\alpha < 1.1\\) AG\n(b) B1: \\(e^x = 4 - x^2 \\Rightarrow x = \\ln(4 - x^2)\\) AG\n(c) M1: \\(x_2 = \\ln 3 = 1.0986\\)\nA1: \\(x_3 = 1.0271\\), \\(x_4 = 1.0801\\)\n(d) E1: Cobweb, since the iterates lie alternately above and below \\(\\alpha\\) (\\(\\alpha = 1.058\\))` },
      { q: `The cross-section of a skateboard ramp is modelled by the region between the curve \\(y = \\ln(1 + x)\\), the x-axis and the line \\(x = 2\\), where x and y are in metres.\n\n(a) Use the trapezium rule with 4 strips to estimate the area of the cross-section, giving your answer to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(b) By considering \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}\\), explain whether your answer to (a) is an over-estimate or an under-estimate. [2]\n(c) State how the trapezium rule could be used to obtain a better estimate. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: y-values 0, 0.4055, 0.6931, 0.9163, 1.0986\nM1: \\(\\frac{0.5}{2}\\left[0 + 1.0986 + 2(0.4055 + 0.6931 + 0.9163)\\right]\\)\nA1: 1.28 m\\(^2\\)\n(b) M1: \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = -\\frac{1}{(1 + x)^2}\\)\nE1: Negative for \\(0 \\le x \\le 2\\), so the curve is concave and the trapezia lie below it: under-estimate (exact \\(3\\ln 3 - 2 = 1.296\\))\n(c) B1: Use more strips (smaller h)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(xe^x = 2\\) has a single root \\(\\alpha\\).\n\n(a) Show that the Newton–Raphson iteration for this equation can be written as \\(x_{n+1} = \\frac{x_n^2e^{x_n} + 2}{(1 + x_n)e^{x_n}}\\). [2]\n(b) Use this iteration with \\(x_0 = 1\\) to find \\(\\alpha\\) correct to 4 decimal places. [3]\n(c) Explain why \\(x_0 = -1\\) cannot be used as a starting value. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(x) = xe^x - 2\\), \\(f'(x) = (1 + x)e^x\\) (product rule)\nA1: \\(x_n - \\frac{x_ne^{x_n} - 2}{(1 + x_n)e^{x_n}} = \\frac{x_n(1 + x_n)e^{x_n} - x_ne^{x_n} + 2}{(1 + x_n)e^{x_n}}\\) leading to given result AG\n(b) M1: \\(x_1 = 0.86788\\)\nA1: \\(x_2 = 0.85278\\), \\(x_3 = 0.85261\\)\nA1: \\(\\alpha = 0.8526\\) cao\n(c) E1: \\(f'(-1) = 0\\) (denominator zero; stationary point), so \\(x_1\\) is undefined` },
      { q: `The concentration, \\(C\\) mg per litre, of a medicine in the bloodstream \\(t\\) hours after it is taken is modelled by \\(C = 20(e^{-0.2t} - e^{-t})\\).\n\n(a) Find the exact time at which the concentration is greatest. [3]\n(b) The concentration falls to 4 mg per litre at time \\(T\\) hours, where \\(T > 7\\). Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(t_0 = 7\\) to find \\(T\\) correct to 3 significant figures. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(\\frac{dC}{dt} = 20(-0.2e^{-0.2t} + e^{-t})\\)\nM1: Set to 0: \\(e^{-0.8t} = 0.2\\)\nA1: \\(t = \\frac{5}{4}\\ln 5\\) (\\(= 2.01\\) hours)\n(b) M1: \\(f(t) = 20(e^{-0.2t} - e^{-t}) - 4\\) with \\(f'(t) = \\frac{dC}{dt}\\); \\(t_1 = 7 - \\frac{0.9137}{-0.9682}\\)\nA1: \\(t_1 = 7.9438\\), \\(t_2 = 8.0383\\)\nA1: \\(T = 8.04\\) hours` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x = \\cos x\\), where \\(x\\) is in radians, has a single root \\(\\alpha\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(\\alpha = 0.739\\) correct to 3 decimal places. [3]\n(b) The iteration \\(x_{n+1} = \\cos x_n\\) is used with \\(x_1 = 0.7\\). Find \\(x_2\\) and \\(x_3\\) to 5 decimal places. [1]\n(c) By considering the derivative of \\(\\cos x\\), explain why this iteration converges to \\(\\alpha\\), and state whether it gives a staircase or a cobweb diagram. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(x) = x - \\cos x\\), evaluate at 0.7385 and 0.7395\nA1: \\(f(0.7385) = -0.00098\\), \\(f(0.7395) = 0.00069\\)\nE1: Sign change and f continuous, so \\(0.7385 < \\alpha < 0.7395\\), \\(\\alpha = 0.739\\) AG\n(b) B1: \\(x_2 = 0.76484\\), \\(x_3 = 0.72149\\)\n(c) M1: \\(g'(x) = -\\sin x\\), \\(g'(\\alpha) = -0.674\\)\nE1: \\(|g'(\\alpha)| < 1\\) so it converges; negative so cobweb` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = \\frac{1}{x - 2} - x\\). A student calculates \\(f(1) = -2\\) and \\(f(3) = -2\\) and concludes that \\(f(x) = 0\\) has no root in the interval \\(1 \\le x \\le 3\\).\n\nIn this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nShow that the student's conclusion is wrong, and explain why the change of sign method has failed.`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `DR\nM1: \\(\\frac{1}{x - 2} = x \\Rightarrow x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0\\)\nA1: \\(x = 1 \\pm \\sqrt{2}\\)\nB1: \\(1 + \\sqrt{2} = 2.414\\ldots\\) lies in \\(1 \\le x \\le 3\\), so there is a root\nE1: f is not continuous: it has a vertical asymptote at \\(x = 2\\)\nE1: f changes sign across the asymptote (from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(+\\infty\\)) and again at the root, e.g. \\(f(2.5) = -0.5\\), \\(f(2.1) = 7.9\\)\nE1: Two sign changes cancel, so equal signs at the end-points do not show there is no root` },
      { q: `During a storm, the rate of rainfall, \\(R\\) mm per hour, \\(t\\) hours after the start is modelled by \\(R = 2te^{-0.25t}\\) for \\(0 \\le t \\le 8\\).\n\n(a) Use the trapezium rule with 4 strips to estimate the total rainfall, \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^8 R\\,dt\\), giving your answer to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(b) Use integration by parts to show that the exact total rainfall is \\(32 - 96e^{-2}\\) mm. [3]\n(c) Find the percentage error in your answer to (a). [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: R-values 0, 2.4261, 2.9430, 2.6776, 2.1654\nM1: \\(\\frac{2}{2}\\left[0 + 2.1654 + 2(2.4261 + 2.9430 + 2.6776)\\right]\\)\nA1: 18.3 mm\n(b) M1: \\(u = 2t\\), \\(\\frac{dv}{dt} = e^{-0.25t}\\), \\(v = -4e^{-0.25t}\\)\nA1: \\(\\left[-8te^{-0.25t} - 32e^{-0.25t}\\right]_0^8\\)\nA1: \\(-64e^{-2} - 32e^{-2} + 32 = 32 - 96e^{-2}\\) AG\n(c) B1: \\(\\frac{19.008 - 18.259}{19.008} \\times 100 = 3.94\\%\\) (under-estimate)` },
      { q: `(a) Sketch, on the same axes, the graphs of \\(y = \\ln x\\) and \\(y = 4 - x^2\\) for \\(x > 0\\), and hence explain why the equation \\(\\ln x = 4 - x^2\\) has exactly one root, \\(\\alpha\\). [2]\n(b) Show that \\(1.8 < \\alpha < 1.9\\). [2]\n(c) Show that the equation can be rearranged to \\(x = \\sqrt{4 - \\ln x}\\). [1]\n(d) Use the iteration \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt{4 - \\ln x_n}\\) with \\(x_1 = 1.8\\) to find \\(\\alpha\\) correct to 3 decimal places. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Sketch', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1: Increasing log curve through (1, 0) with asymptote \\(x = 0\\); decreasing parabola through (0, 4) and (2, 0)\nE1: Graphs cross exactly once (one increasing, one decreasing), so exactly one root\n(b) M1: \\(f(x) = \\ln x + x^2 - 4\\); \\(f(1.8) = -0.172\\), \\(f(1.9) = 0.252\\)\nA1: Sign change, f continuous for \\(x > 0\\) AG\n(c) B1: \\(x^2 = 4 - \\ln x\\), take positive square root since \\(x > 0\\) AG\n(d) M1: \\(x_2 = 1.84722\\), \\(x_3 = 1.84020\\), \\(x_4 = 1.84123\\)\nA1: \\(x_5 = 1.84108\\), \\(x_6 = 1.84110\\), so \\(\\alpha = 1.841\\)` },
      { q: `It is given that \\(f(x) = 2x^3 - 9x^2 + 12x - 4.2\\).\n\n(a) Show that the equation \\(f(x) = 0\\) has a root \\(\\gamma\\) in the interval \\(2 < x < 3\\). [2]\n(b) Explain why \\(x_0 = 2\\) cannot be used as a starting value for the Newton–Raphson method. [1]\n(c) Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 2.5\\) to find \\(\\gamma\\) correct to 3 decimal places. Show the result of each iteration. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(2) = -0.2\\), \\(f(3) = 4.8\\)\nA1: Sign change and f continuous, so root in (2, 3) AG\n(b) E1: \\(f'(x) = 6x^2 - 18x + 12\\), \\(f'(2) = 0\\): tangent horizontal, division by zero\n(c) M1: Correct use of \\(x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\\)\nA1: \\(x_1 = 2.32222\\)\nA1: \\(x_2 = 2.25244\\), \\(x_3 = 2.24013\\), \\(x_4 = 2.23975\\)\nA1: \\(\\gamma = 2.240\\) cao` },
      { q: `The curve \\(y = e^x - x^3\\) has a stationary point with x-coordinate \\(\\beta\\), where \\(\\beta > 3\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(3.7 < \\beta < 3.8\\). [2]\n(b) Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 4\\) to find \\(\\beta\\) correct to 4 decimal places. [4]\n(c) Determine the nature of this stationary point. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^x - 3x^2\\); at 3.7: \\(-0.623\\), at 3.8: \\(1.381\\)\nA1: Sign change and \\(\\frac{dy}{dx}\\) continuous, so \\(3.7 < \\beta < 3.8\\) AG\n(b) M1: \\(g(x) = e^x - 3x^2\\), \\(g'(x) = e^x - 6x\\)\nM1: \\(x_{n+1} = x_n - \\frac{e^{x_n} - 3x_n^2}{e^{x_n} - 6x_n}\\) used\nA1: \\(x_1 = 3.78436\\), \\(x_2 = 3.73538\\), \\(x_3 = 3.73308\\)\nA1: \\(x_4 = 3.73308\\), \\(\\beta = 3.7331\\)\n(c) B1: \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = e^\\beta - 6\\beta = 19.4 > 0\\), so minimum` },
      { q: `(a) Use the trapezium rule with 4 strips to find an approximation to \\(\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\sec x\\,dx\\), giving your answer to 4 significant figures. [4]\n(b) Explain, with reference to the graph of \\(y = \\sec x\\), whether your answer to (a) is an over-estimate or an under-estimate. [2]\n(c) Given that the exact value of the integral is \\(\\ln(2 + \\sqrt{3})\\), find the percentage error in your approximation. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: \\(h = \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\)\nB1: y-values 1, 1.0353, 1.1547, 1.4142, 2\nM1: \\(\\frac{\\pi}{24}\\left[1 + 2 + 2(1.0353 + 1.1547 + 1.4142)\\right]\\)\nA1: 1.336\n(b) B1: The graph of \\(y = \\sec x\\) is convex on \\(0 \\le x \\le \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) (e.g. \\(\\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} = \\sec x(\\sec^2 x + \\tan^2 x) > 0\\))\nE1: The trapezia lie above the curve, so over-estimate\n(c) M1: \\(\\frac{1.3363 - 1.3170}{1.3170} \\times 100\\)\nA1: 1.47%` },
      { q: `A river is 12 m wide. The depth of the river, \\(d\\) m, is measured at 2 m intervals across a straight cross-section. (data written for this practice question)\n\nDistance from bank (m): 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\nDepth (m): 0, 1.1, 1.9, 2.4, 2.2, 1.5, 0\n\n(a) Use the trapezium rule with all the values in the table to estimate the area of the cross-section. [3]\n(b) The water flows through the cross-section at a constant speed of 0.8 m s\\(^{-1}\\). Estimate the volume of water passing through the cross-section in one minute. [2]\n(c) Assuming that the river bed forms a smooth concave curve, state with a reason whether your answer to (a) is an over-estimate or an under-estimate. [2]\n(d) State one limitation of the model in (b). [1]`, marks: 8, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) B1: \\(h = 2\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{2}{2}\\left[0 + 0 + 2(1.1 + 1.9 + 2.4 + 2.2 + 1.5)\\right]\\)\nA1: 18.2 m\\(^2\\)\n(b) M1: \\(18.2 \\times 0.8 \\times 60\\)\nA1: 874 m\\(^3\\) (873.6) ft their (a)\n(c) B1: The depth graph is concave (curves downwards)\nE1: So the straight lines lie below the true profile, and 18.2 is an under-estimate\n(d) B1: e.g. water does not flow at the same speed at all depths (slower near the bed and banks)` },
      { q: `The equation \\(x^3 - 3x - 5 = 0\\) has a single real root \\(\\alpha\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(\\alpha\\) lies between 2 and 3. [2]\n(b) Show that the equation can be written as \\(x = \\sqrt[3]{3x + 5}\\). [1]\n(c) Use the iteration \\(x_{n+1} = \\sqrt[3]{3x_n + 5}\\) with \\(x_1 = 2\\) to find \\(x_2\\), \\(x_3\\) and \\(x_4\\), giving each to 4 decimal places. [2]\n(d) Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 2\\) to find \\(\\alpha\\) correct to 5 decimal places. [3]\n(e) Given that \\(g(x) = \\sqrt[3]{3x + 5}\\), show that \\(g'(\\alpha) = \\frac{1}{\\alpha^2}\\) and hence explain why the iteration in (c) converges. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) M1: \\(f(2) = -3\\), \\(f(3) = 13\\)\nA1: Sign change and f continuous, so root in (2, 3) AG\n(b) B1: \\(x^3 = 3x + 5 \\Rightarrow x = \\sqrt[3]{3x + 5}\\) AG\n(c) M1: \\(x_2 = \\sqrt[3]{11} = 2.2240\\)\nA1: \\(x_3 = 2.2684\\), \\(x_4 = 2.2770\\)\n(d) M1: \\(f'(x) = 3x^2 - 3\\), \\(x_1 = 2 - \\frac{-3}{9} = 2.33333\\)\nA1: \\(x_2 = 2.28056\\), \\(x_3 = 2.27902\\)\nA1: \\(x_4 = 2.27902\\), \\(\\alpha = 2.27902\\)\n(e) E1: \\(g'(x) = (3x + 5)^{-\\frac{2}{3}}\\) and \\(3\\alpha + 5 = \\alpha^3\\), so \\(g'(\\alpha) = \\alpha^{-2} \\approx 0.193\\); \\(|g'(\\alpha)| < 1\\) so converges` },
      { q: `A small company makes \\(x\\) hundred items per month. Its monthly profit, \\(P\\) thousand pounds, is modelled by \\(P = 10xe^{-0.2x} - 6\\) for \\(x \\ge 0\\).\n\n(a) Find the exact value of \\(x\\) that gives the maximum profit, and find the maximum monthly profit to the nearest £100. [4]\n(b) Show that the company breaks even (\\(P = 0\\)) for a value of \\(x\\) between 0.68 and 0.69. [2]\n(c) The company also breaks even at a larger value of \\(x\\). Use the Newton–Raphson method with \\(x_0 = 15\\) to find this value correct to 3 significant figures. [3]\n(d) Comment on the suitability of the model for very large values of \\(x\\). [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1: Product rule: \\(\\frac{dP}{dx} = 10e^{-0.2x} - 2xe^{-0.2x}\\)\nA1: \\(= 10e^{-0.2x}(1 - 0.2x)\\)\nM1: \\(\\frac{dP}{dx} = 0 \\Rightarrow x = 5\\)\nA1: \\(P = 50e^{-1} - 6 = 12.39\\), so maximum profit £12 400\n(b) M1: \\(P(0.68) = -0.0647\\), \\(P(0.69) = 0.0106\\)\nA1: Sign change and P continuous, so break-even between 0.68 and 0.69 AG\n(c) M1: \\(x_1 = 15 - \\frac{1.4681}{-0.9957}\\)\nA1: \\(x_1 = 16.474\\), \\(x_2 = 16.601\\)\nA1: \\(x = 16.6\\) (1660 items)\n(d) E1: e.g. for large x the model gives P tending to \\(-6\\), a loss of £6000 however many are made, which is unrealistic as costs would grow with output` }
    ]
  },
  '1.10': {
    name: 'Vectors',
    questions: [
      { q: `The vectors \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) are given by \\(\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}\\).\n\nFind \\(|2\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b}|\\), giving your answer in the form \\(p\\sqrt{2}\\), where \\(p\\) is an integer.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(2\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b} = 3\\mathbf{i} - 5\\mathbf{j} + 4\\mathbf{k}\\) soi, allow one slip\nA1 \\(\\sqrt{9 + 25 + 16} = \\sqrt{50} = 5\\sqrt{2}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(5\\sqrt{2}\\)` },
      { q: `The vector \\(\\mathbf{p}\\) is given by \\(\\mathbf{p} = 5\\mathbf{i} - 12\\mathbf{j}\\).\n\nFind the unit vector in the direction of \\(\\mathbf{p}\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(|\\mathbf{p}| = 13\\)\nB1 \\(\\frac{1}{13}(5\\mathbf{i} - 12\\mathbf{j})\\) or \\(\\frac{5}{13}\\mathbf{i} - \\frac{12}{13}\\mathbf{j}\\) oe, FT their magnitude\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{5}{13}\\mathbf{i} - \\frac{12}{13}\\mathbf{j}\\)` },
      { q: `A particle of mass 4 kg moves on a smooth horizontal surface. The only horizontal force acting on the particle is \\((6\\mathbf{i} - 8\\mathbf{j})\\) N.\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\mathbf{F} = m\\mathbf{a}\\): \\(\\mathbf{a} = 1.5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j}\\), or \\(|\\mathbf{F}| = 10\\) and divides by 4\nA1 \\(2.5\\) m s⁻² cao\nAnswer: 2.5 m s⁻²` },
      { q: `The vectors \\(4\\mathbf{i} + k\\mathbf{j}\\) and \\(6\\mathbf{i} - 9\\mathbf{j}\\) are parallel.\n\nFind the value of the constant \\(k\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(4\\mathbf{i} + k\\mathbf{j} = \\lambda(6\\mathbf{i} - 9\\mathbf{j})\\) with \\(\\lambda = \\frac{2}{3}\\), or \\(\\frac{k}{4} = \\frac{-9}{6}\\)\nA1 \\(k = -6\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(k = -6\\)` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively.\n\nA ship moves with constant velocity \\((-5\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j})\\) km h⁻¹.\n\nFind the speed of the ship and the bearing on which it is moving.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Speed \\(= \\sqrt{25 + 144} = 13\\) km h⁻¹\nM1 Uses \\(\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{12}\\right)\\) (22.6°) or \\(\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right)\\) (67.4°) with a correct quadrant\nA1 Bearing 337° (337.4°) cao\nAnswer: 13 km h⁻¹ on a bearing of 337°` },
      { q: `The points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) have position vectors \\(\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 4\\mathbf{k}\\) and \\(3\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} - 2\\mathbf{k}\\) respectively. The point \\(P\\) lies on the line segment \\(AB\\) and \\(AP : PB = 2 : 1\\).\n\nFind the position vector of \\(P\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = 2\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j} - 6\\mathbf{k}\\)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\overrightarrow{OA} + \\frac{2}{3}\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) oe, e.g. \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\mathbf{i}\\) (i.e. \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\mathbf{i} + 0\\mathbf{j} + 0\\mathbf{k}\\)) cao\nAnswer: \\(\\frac{7}{3}\\mathbf{i}\\)` },
      { q: `The vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is given by \\(\\mathbf{a} = t\\mathbf{i} + (t + 1)\\mathbf{j} + 4\\mathbf{k}\\), where \\(t\\) is a constant. It is given that \\(|\\mathbf{a}| = \\sqrt{29}\\).\n\nFind the possible values of \\(t\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(t^2 + (t + 1)^2 + 16 = 29\\)\nA1 \\(t^2 + t - 6 = 0\\) oe three-term quadratic\nA1 \\(t = 2\\) or \\(t = -3\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(t = 2\\) or \\(t = -3\\)` },
      { q: `Three forces \\((3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) N, \\((p\\mathbf{i} - 5\\mathbf{j})\\) N and \\((-4\\mathbf{i} + q\\mathbf{j})\\) N act on a particle. The particle is in equilibrium.\n\nFind the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), and hence find the magnitude of the force \\((p\\mathbf{i} - 5\\mathbf{j})\\) N.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Resultant set to zero: \\(3 + p - 4 = 0\\) and \\(2 - 5 + q = 0\\)\nA1 \\(p = 1\\), \\(q = 3\\)\nA1 \\(\\sqrt{26} = 5.10\\) N (3 sf) FT their \\(p\\)\nAnswer: \\(p = 1\\), \\(q = 3\\), magnitude \\(\\sqrt{26} \\approx 5.10\\) N` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) moves with constant velocity. At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds, \\(P\\) is at the point with position vector \\((2\\mathbf{i} - 3\\mathbf{j})\\) m. At time \\(t = 4\\) seconds, \\(P\\) is at the point with position vector \\((14\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j})\\) m.\n\nFind the velocity of \\(P\\) and the speed of \\(P\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\frac{(14\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j}) - (2\\mathbf{i} - 3\\mathbf{j})}{4}\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = (3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹\nA1 Speed \\(\\sqrt{13} = 3.61\\) m s⁻¹ (3 sf) FT their \\(\\mathbf{v}\\)\nAnswer: \\((3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹; speed \\(\\sqrt{13} \\approx 3.61\\) m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `In the triangle \\(OAB\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}\\). The point \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). The point \\(N\\) lies on \\(OB\\) such that \\(ON : NB = 1 : 2\\).\n\nFind \\(\\overrightarrow{MN}\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), simplifying your answer.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OM} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b})\\)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{ON} = \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b}\\)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{MN} = -\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} - \\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{b}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} - \\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{b}\\)` },
      { q: `The vectors \\(\\mathbf{p}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{q}\\) are given by \\(\\mathbf{p} = 2\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{q} = \\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j}\\).\n\nFind the value of the constant \\(\\lambda\\) for which \\(\\mathbf{p} + \\lambda\\mathbf{q}\\) is parallel to \\(\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\).`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\mathbf{p} + \\lambda\\mathbf{q} = (2 + \\lambda)\\mathbf{i} + (3 - 4\\lambda)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 Sets \\(2 + \\lambda = 3 - 4\\lambda\\) (equal components)\nA1 \\(\\lambda = \\frac{1}{5}\\) cao\nAnswer: \\(\\lambda = \\frac{1}{5}\\)` },
      { q: `A triangular sail is modelled as the triangle \\(ABC\\), where the corners have position vectors, in metres, \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = 2\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k}\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = 4\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OC} = 5\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}\\) relative to a fixed origin \\(O\\).\n\nFind the angle \\(BAC\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Finds at least two of \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = 2\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{AC} = 3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{BC} = \\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}\\)\nA1 \\(AB = 3\\), \\(AC = \\sqrt{17}\\), \\(BC = \\sqrt{18}\\)\nM1 Cosine rule: \\(\\cos BAC = \\frac{9 + 17 - 18}{2 \\times 3 \\times \\sqrt{17}}\\)\nA1 \\(71.1°\\) (3 sf) cao\nAnswer: \\(71.1°\\)` },
      { q: `The points \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) have coordinates \\((1, 2, 3)\\), \\((4, 0, 5)\\) and \\((6, -1, 2)\\) respectively. \\(ABCD\\) is a parallelogram.\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of \\(D\\). [2]\n\n(b) Show that \\(ABCD\\) is not a rhombus. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OD} = \\overrightarrow{OA} + \\overrightarrow{BC}\\) or \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} + \\overrightarrow{OC} - \\overrightarrow{OB}\\)\nA1 \\(D(3, 1, 0)\\)\n(b)\nM1 Finds \\(|\\overrightarrow{AB}| = \\sqrt{17}\\) and \\(|\\overrightarrow{BC}| = \\sqrt{14}\\)\nE1 Adjacent sides unequal so not a rhombus\nAnswer: \\(D(3, 1, 0)\\); \\(AB = \\sqrt{17} \\neq BC = \\sqrt{14}\\)` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively, and the origin \\(O\\) is a fixed point.\n\nA particle \\(P\\) moves with constant velocity \\((2\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹. At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds, \\(P\\) is at the point with position vector \\((-3\\mathbf{i} + 7\\mathbf{j})\\) m.\n\nFind the value of \\(t\\) when \\(P\\) is due east of \\(O\\), and find the distance \\(OP\\) at this time.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = (-3\\mathbf{i} + 7\\mathbf{j}) + t(2\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j})\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = (-3 + 2t)\\mathbf{i} + (7 - t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 Sets \\(\\mathbf{j}\\)-component equal to 0\nA1 \\(t = 7\\) and \\(OP = 11\\) m (with \\(\\mathbf{i}\\)-component positive)\nAnswer: \\(t = 7\\), distance 11 m` },
      { q: `A particle of mass 2 kg is acted on by two forces only. One force has magnitude 26 N and acts in the direction of the vector \\(5\\mathbf{i} - 12\\mathbf{j}\\). The other force is \\((-4\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j})\\) N.\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Unit vector \\(\\frac{1}{13}(5\\mathbf{i} - 12\\mathbf{j})\\) multiplied by 26\nA1 \\((10\\mathbf{i} - 24\\mathbf{j})\\) N\nM1 Resultant \\((6\\mathbf{i} - 18\\mathbf{j})\\) N divided by 2\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{a} = 3\\mathbf{i} - 9\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(|\\mathbf{a}| = \\sqrt{90} = 9.49\\) m s⁻² (3 sf)\nAnswer: \\(3\\sqrt{10} \\approx 9.49\\) m s⁻²` },
      { q: `The points \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) have position vectors \\(2\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j} + 5\\mathbf{k}\\), \\(5\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 4\\mathbf{k}\\) and \\(11\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}\\) respectively.\n\nShow that \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) are collinear, and find the ratio \\(AB : BC\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = 3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k}\\)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{BC} = 6\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j} - 2\\mathbf{k}\\) (or \\(\\overrightarrow{AC} = 9\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j} - 3\\mathbf{k}\\))\nE1 \\(\\overrightarrow{BC} = 2\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) so parallel, and \\(B\\) is a common point, so collinear\nB1 \\(AB : BC = 1 : 2\\)\nGuidance: the E1 requires mention of the common point\nAnswer: collinear; \\(AB : BC = 1 : 2\\)` },
      { q: `A drone flies in a straight line from point \\(A\\) to point \\(B\\). Relative to a fixed origin \\(O\\) on horizontal ground, the position vectors of \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are \\((5\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 3\\mathbf{k})\\) m and \\((17\\mathbf{i} + 14\\mathbf{j} + 18\\mathbf{k})\\) m, where \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are horizontal and \\(\\mathbf{k}\\) is vertically upwards.\n\nFind the distance \\(AB\\) and the angle that the path of the drone makes with the horizontal.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = 12\\mathbf{i} + 16\\mathbf{j} + 15\\mathbf{k}\\)\nA1 \\(AB = \\sqrt{144 + 256 + 225} = 25\\) m\nM1 Horizontal distance \\(\\sqrt{12^2 + 16^2} = 20\\) and uses \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{15}{20}\\) or \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{15}{25}\\)\nA1 \\(36.9°\\) (3 sf)\nAnswer: 25 m; \\(36.9°\\)` },
      { q: `\\(OABC\\) is a parallelogram with \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OC} = \\mathbf{c}\\). The point \\(X\\) is the midpoint of \\(BC\\). The point \\(Y\\) lies on \\(OX\\) such that \\(OY : YX = 2 : 1\\).\n\nShow that \\(Y\\) lies on the diagonal \\(AC\\) and find the ratio \\(AY : YC\\).`, marks: 4, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\mathbf{c} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}\\)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OY} = \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{c} + \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\\)\nE1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AY} = \\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a}) = \\frac{2}{3}\\overrightarrow{AC}\\), so \\(Y\\) lies on \\(AC\\) AG\nB1 \\(AY : YC = 2 : 1\\)\nAnswer: \\(AY : YC = 2 : 1\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) moves in a horizontal plane. At time \\(t\\) seconds, the velocity of \\(P\\) is \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) m s⁻¹, where\n\\(\\mathbf{v} = (3t^2 - 4)\\mathbf{i} + 2t\\mathbf{j}\\).\n\n(a) Find the magnitude of the acceleration of \\(P\\) when \\(t = 2\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find the value of \\(t\\), where \\(t > 0\\), at which \\(P\\) is moving parallel to \\(\\mathbf{j}\\). [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Differentiates: \\(\\mathbf{a} = 6t\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 \\(12\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}\\), magnitude \\(\\sqrt{148} = 12.2\\) m s⁻² (3 sf)\n(b)\nM1 Sets \\(3t^2 - 4 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = 1.15\\) (3 sf)\nAnswer: (a) \\(2\\sqrt{37} \\approx 12.2\\) m s⁻² (b) \\(t = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) is moving with constant acceleration \\((-\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻². At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds, \\(P\\) is at the point \\(O\\) and has velocity \\((4\\mathbf{i} - 3\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(t\\) at which \\(P\\) is moving parallel to the vector \\(\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the distance of \\(P\\) from \\(O\\) when \\(t = 3\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{a}t = (4 - t)\\mathbf{i} + (-3 + 2t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 Sets \\(4 - t = -3 + 2t\\)\nA1 \\(t = \\frac{7}{3}\\) (components then both \\(\\frac{5}{3}\\), positive)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{s} = \\mathbf{u}t + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}t^2 = 3(4\\mathbf{i} - 3\\mathbf{j}) + 4.5(-\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{s} = 7.5\\mathbf{i}\\), distance 7.5 m\nAnswer: (a) \\(t = \\frac{7}{3}\\) (b) 7.5 m` },
      { q: `A particle of mass 0.5 kg is in equilibrium under the action of three forces \\((5\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) N, \\((-3\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})\\) N and \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) N.\n\n(a) Find \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) and its magnitude. [3]\n\nThe force \\(\\mathbf{F}\\) is now removed. The other two forces are unchanged.\n\n(b) Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{F} = -[(5\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}) + (-3\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})]\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{F} = -2\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 \\(|\\mathbf{F}| = \\sqrt{40} = 6.32\\) N (3 sf)\n(b)\nM1 Resultant \\((2\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j})\\) N and \\(\\mathbf{F} = m\\mathbf{a}\\) with \\(m = 0.5\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{a} = 4\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(|\\mathbf{a}| = \\sqrt{160} = 12.6\\) m s⁻² (3 sf)\nAnswer: (a) \\(-2\\mathbf{i} - 6\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(2\\sqrt{10} \\approx 6.32\\) N (b) \\(4\\sqrt{10} \\approx 12.6\\) m s⁻²` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively.\n\nAt 12:00 a ship \\(A\\) is at the point with position vector \\((2\\mathbf{i} + 10\\mathbf{j})\\) km and a ship \\(B\\) is at the point with position vector \\((-4\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})\\) km, relative to a fixed origin. Ship \\(A\\) moves with constant velocity \\((6\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j})\\) km h⁻¹ and ship \\(B\\) moves with constant velocity \\((8\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})\\) km h⁻¹.\n\nFind the time at which \\(B\\) is due north of \\(A\\), and find the distance between the ships at this time.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Position vectors at time \\(t\\) hours after 12:00 for either ship\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_A = (2 + 6t)\\mathbf{i} + (10 - 2t)\\mathbf{j}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{r}_B = (-4 + 8t)\\mathbf{i} + (4 + 4t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 Equates \\(\\mathbf{i}\\)-components\nA1 \\(t = 3\\), i.e. 15:00\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_A = 20\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(\\mathbf{r}_B = 20\\mathbf{i} + 16\\mathbf{j}\\); distance 12 km (B north of A confirmed)\nAnswer: 15:00, 12 km` },
      { q: `In the triangle \\(OAB\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}\\). The point \\(P\\) lies on \\(OA\\) with \\(OP : PA = 1 : 2\\) and the point \\(Q\\) lies on \\(OB\\) with \\(OQ : QB = 1 : 2\\).\n\n(a) Show that \\(PQ\\) is parallel to \\(AB\\) and state the ratio \\(PQ : AB\\). [3]\n\nThe point \\(R\\) is the midpoint of \\(PQ\\) and the point \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\).\n\n(b) Show that \\(O\\), \\(R\\) and \\(M\\) are collinear. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ} = \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b} - \\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\\)\nE1 \\(= \\frac{1}{3}(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) = \\frac{1}{3}\\overrightarrow{AB}\\), so parallel\nB1 \\(PQ : AB = 1 : 3\\)\n(b)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{1}{6}(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b})\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OM} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b})\\)\nE1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OM} = 3\\overrightarrow{OR}\\), parallel with common point \\(O\\), so collinear\nAnswer: \\(PQ : AB = 1 : 3\\); \\(\\overrightarrow{OM} = 3\\overrightarrow{OR}\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) moves in a horizontal plane. At time \\(t\\) seconds, where \\(t \\geq 0\\), the velocity of \\(P\\) is \\(\\mathbf{v}\\) m s⁻¹, where\n\\(\\mathbf{v} = (6t - 2)\\mathbf{i} + (3t^2 - 12)\\mathbf{j}\\).\nWhen \\(t = 0\\), \\(P\\) is at the origin \\(O\\).\n\nFind the distance of \\(P\\) from \\(O\\) at the instant when \\(P\\) is moving parallel to \\(\\mathbf{i}\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Sets \\(3t^2 - 12 = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = 2\\) (rejecting \\(t = -2\\))\nM1 Integrates \\(\\mathbf{v}\\): \\(\\mathbf{r} = (3t^2 - 2t)\\mathbf{i} + (t^3 - 12t)\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{c}\\), with \\(\\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{0}\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = 8\\mathbf{i} - 16\\mathbf{j}\\) at \\(t = 2\\)\nA1 \\(\\sqrt{320} = 8\\sqrt{5} = 17.9\\) m (3 sf)\nAnswer: \\(8\\sqrt{5} \\approx 17.9\\) m` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively, and distances are in metres.\n\nA remote-controlled boat moves on a lake with constant speed 10 m s⁻¹ in the direction of the vector \\(3\\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j}\\). At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds the boat is at the point with position vector \\((\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j})\\) m relative to a fixed point \\(O\\).\n\nFind the value of \\(t\\) when the boat is south-east of \\(O\\).`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Velocity \\(= 10 \\times \\frac{1}{5}(3\\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j})\\)\nA1 \\((6\\mathbf{i} - 8\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = (1 + 6t)\\mathbf{i} + (12 - 8t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 South-east of \\(O\\): \\(\\mathbf{j}\\)-component \\(= -(\\mathbf{i}\\)-component\\()\\), i.e. \\(12 - 8t = -(1 + 6t)\\)\nA1 \\(t = 6.5\\) (position \\(40\\mathbf{i} - 40\\mathbf{j}\\))\nAnswer: \\(t = 6.5\\)` },
      { q: `A surveyor models two survey markers as the points \\(A(1, 2, -1)\\) and \\(B(3, -2, 3)\\), with distances in metres. A third marker \\(C\\) is to be placed on the \\(x\\)-axis so that \\(C\\) is equidistant from \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of \\(C\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the exact area of triangle \\(ABC\\). [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(C(c, 0, 0)\\) and \\((c - 1)^2 + 4 + 1 = (c - 3)^2 + 4 + 9\\)\nA1 \\(4c = 16\\) oe linear equation\nA1 \\(C(4, 0, 0)\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(AC = BC = \\sqrt{14}\\), \\(AB = 6\\); perpendicular height \\(\\sqrt{14 - 9} = \\sqrt{5}\\) (or cosine rule and \\(\\frac{1}{2}ab\\sin C\\))\nA1 Area \\(= 3\\sqrt{5}\\) m² cao\nAnswer: (a) \\((4, 0, 0)\\) (b) \\(3\\sqrt{5}\\) m²` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively.\n\nA boat moves with constant acceleration \\((0.5\\mathbf{i} - 0.2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻². At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds its velocity is \\((2\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the speed of the boat when \\(t = 10\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the value of \\(t\\) at which the boat is moving due east. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = (2\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j}) + t(0.5\\mathbf{i} - 0.2\\mathbf{j})\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = 7\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}\\) at \\(t = 10\\)\nA1 Speed \\(\\sqrt{58} = 7.62\\) m s⁻¹ (3 sf)\n(b)\nM1 Sets \\(5 - 0.2t = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = 25\\)\nB1 Checks \\(\\mathbf{i}\\)-component \\(2 + 0.5(25) = 14.5 > 0\\), so the boat is moving east (not west)\nAnswer: (a) 7.62 m s⁻¹ (b) \\(t = 25\\)` },
      { q: `In the triangle \\(OAB\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}\\). The point \\(P\\) is the midpoint of \\(OA\\) and the point \\(Q\\) lies on \\(AB\\) such that \\(AQ : QB = 2 : 1\\). The lines \\(OQ\\) and \\(BP\\) meet at the point \\(R\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(\\overrightarrow{OQ}\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find \\(\\overrightarrow{OR}\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\), and hence find the ratio \\(OR : RQ\\). [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OQ} = \\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}(\\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a})\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}\\)\n(b)\nM1 Two expressions: \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\lambda\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}\\right)\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\mathbf{b} + \\mu\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a} - \\mathbf{b}\\right)\\)\nM1 Equates coefficients of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) (as \\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) not parallel): \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{3} = \\frac{\\mu}{2}\\), \\(\\frac{2\\lambda}{3} = 1 - \\mu\\)\nA1 \\(\\lambda = \\frac{3}{4}\\), so \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}\\)\nA1 \\(OR : RQ = 3 : 1\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{b}\\) (b) \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}\\), \\(3 : 1\\)` },
      { q: `A particle of mass 2 kg is acted on by three forces only.\n• A force of magnitude 10 N acts in the direction of the vector \\(3\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j}\\).\n• A force of magnitude 13 N acts in the direction of the vector \\(-5\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j}\\).\n• The third force is \\((-4\\mathbf{i} - 16\\mathbf{j})\\) N.\n\nFind the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle and the angle between the acceleration and the vector \\(\\mathbf{i}\\).`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses unit vectors to write either force in component form\nA1 \\((6\\mathbf{i} + 8\\mathbf{j})\\) N and \\((-5\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j})\\) N\nM1 Adds all three forces\nA1 Resultant \\((-3\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})\\) N\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\frac{1}{2}(-3\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j})\\), magnitude \\(2.5\\) m s⁻²\nA1 Angle with \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) is \\(126.9°\\) (i.e. \\(180° - \\tan^{-1}\\frac{4}{3}\\))\nAnswer: 2.5 m s⁻², at \\(126.9°\\) to \\(\\mathbf{i}\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) moves in a horizontal plane. At time \\(t\\) seconds its acceleration is \\((2\\mathbf{i} - 6t\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻². At time \\(t = 0\\), \\(P\\) is at the origin \\(O\\) and has velocity \\((-4\\mathbf{i} + 9\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the speed of \\(P\\) when \\(t = 1\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find the distance of \\(P\\) from \\(O\\) when \\(P\\) next crosses the \\(\\mathbf{j}\\)-axis (the line through \\(O\\) parallel to \\(\\mathbf{j}\\)). [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Integrates with \\(+\\mathbf{c}\\): \\(\\mathbf{v} = (2t - 4)\\mathbf{i} + (9 - 3t^2)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 At \\(t = 1\\): \\(-2\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j}\\), speed \\(\\sqrt{40} = 6.32\\) m s⁻¹ (3 sf)\n(b)\nM1 Integrates again: \\(\\mathbf{r} = (t^2 - 4t)\\mathbf{i} + (9t - t^3)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 Sets \\(t^2 - 4t = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = 4\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = -28\\mathbf{j}\\), distance 28 m\nAnswer: (a) \\(2\\sqrt{10} \\approx 6.32\\) m s⁻¹ (b) 28 m` },
      { q: `A rectangular room is modelled as a cuboid. Relative to an origin \\(O\\) at one corner of the floor, the corners of the floor are \\(O(0, 0, 0)\\), \\(A(6, 0, 0)\\), \\(B(6, 4, 0)\\) and \\(C(0, 4, 0)\\), and the ceiling is the plane \\(z = 3\\). Units are metres. A light fitting is at the point \\(L\\) in the centre of the ceiling. Straight taut wires run from \\(L\\) to \\(O\\) and from \\(L\\) to \\(A\\).\n\n(a) Find the length of the wire \\(OL\\). [2]\n\n(b) Find the angle that the wire \\(OL\\) makes with the floor. [2]\n\n(c) Find the angle \\(OLA\\). [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(L(3, 2, 3)\\)\nB1 \\(OL = \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 9} = \\sqrt{22} = 4.69\\) m (3 sf)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{22}}\\) or \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\)\nA1 \\(39.8°\\) (3 sf)\n(c)\nM1 \\(AL = \\sqrt{22}\\), \\(OA = 6\\) and cosine rule \\(\\cos OLA = \\frac{22 + 22 - 36}{2 \\times 22}\\)\nA1 \\(79.5°\\) (3 sf)\nAnswer: (a) 4.69 m (b) 39.8° (c) 79.5°` },
      { q: `The points \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) have coordinates \\((1, 0, 2)\\), \\((3, 4, -2)\\) and \\((7, 2, -2)\\) respectively. \\(ABCD\\) is a parallelogram.\n\n(a) Find the coordinates of \\(D\\). [2]\n\n(b) By considering the lengths of \\(AB\\), \\(BC\\) and \\(AC\\), show that \\(ABCD\\) is a rectangle. [3]\n\n(c) Find the exact area of \\(ABCD\\). [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OD} = \\overrightarrow{OA} + \\overrightarrow{OC} - \\overrightarrow{OB}\\) oe\nA1 \\(D(5, -2, 2)\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = 2\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{BC} = 4\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{AC} = 6\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} - 4\\mathbf{k}\\)\nA1 \\(AB^2 = 36\\), \\(BC^2 = 20\\), \\(AC^2 = 56\\)\nE1 \\(AB^2 + BC^2 = AC^2\\), so angle \\(ABC = 90°\\) (converse of Pythagoras); a parallelogram with a right angle is a rectangle AG\n(c)\nB1 \\(6 \\times \\sqrt{20} = 12\\sqrt{5}\\) cao\nAnswer: (a) \\((5, -2, 2)\\) (c) \\(12\\sqrt{5}\\)` },
      { q: `In this question the unit vectors \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are directed due east and due north respectively, and distances are in kilometres.\n\nTwo boats \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) move with constant velocities. At time \\(t = 0\\) hours, \\(A\\) is at the point with position vector \\((-2\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j})\\) and \\(B\\) is at the point with position vector \\((5\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j})\\). The velocity of \\(A\\) is \\((4\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j})\\) km h⁻¹ and the velocity of \\(B\\) is \\((\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j})\\) km h⁻¹.\n\n(a) Show that the distance \\(d\\) km between the boats at time \\(t\\) hours satisfies\n\\(d^2 = 25t^2 - 98t + 98\\). [4]\n\n(b) Hence find the least distance between the boats and the value of \\(t\\) at which it occurs. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Position vector of either boat at time \\(t\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_A = (-2 + 4t)\\mathbf{i} + (6 + t)\\mathbf{j}\\), \\(\\mathbf{r}_B = (5 + t)\\mathbf{i} + (-1 + 5t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AB} = (7 - 3t)\\mathbf{i} + (4t - 7)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 \\(d^2 = (7 - 3t)^2 + (4t - 7)^2 = 25t^2 - 98t + 98\\) AG, convincingly shown\n(b)\nM1 Completes the square or differentiates: \\(t = \\frac{98}{50}\\)\nA1 \\(t = 1.96\\)\nA1 \\(d^2 = 1.96\\), least distance 1.4 km\nAnswer: (b) 1.4 km when \\(t = 1.96\\)` },
      { q: `\\(OABC\\) is a parallelogram with \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OC} = \\mathbf{c}\\). The point \\(M\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). The line \\(OM\\) meets the diagonal \\(AC\\) at the point \\(X\\).\n\nProve that \\(OX : XM = 2 : 1\\) and find the ratio \\(AX : XC\\).`, marks: 7, command: 'Prove', markScheme: `B1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OM} = \\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}\\)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{AC} = \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a}\\)\nM1 Two expressions: \\(\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\lambda\\left(\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{c}\\right)\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\mathbf{a} + \\mu(\\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a})\\)\nM1 Equates coefficients, stating \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{c}\\) are not parallel: \\(\\lambda = 1 - \\mu\\), \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{2} = \\mu\\)\nA1 \\(\\lambda = \\frac{2}{3}\\)\nA1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\frac{2}{3}\\overrightarrow{OM}\\), so \\(OX : XM = 2 : 1\\) AG\nA1 \\(\\mu = \\frac{1}{3}\\), \\(AX : XC = 1 : 2\\)\nAnswer: \\(AX : XC = 1 : 2\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) of mass 2 kg is initially at rest at the origin \\(O\\). It is acted on by two constant forces \\((3\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j})\\) N and \\((p\\mathbf{i} + q\\mathbf{j})\\) N only. After 4 seconds, the velocity of \\(P\\) is \\((10\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the values of \\(p\\) and \\(q\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the magnitude of the force \\((p\\mathbf{i} + q\\mathbf{j})\\) N and the angle it makes with \\(\\mathbf{i}\\). [2]\n\n(c) Find the distance \\(OP\\) when \\(t = 4\\). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\frac{10\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j}}{4} = 2.5\\mathbf{i} + 1.5\\mathbf{j}\\)\nM1 \\((3 + p)\\mathbf{i} + (q - 2)\\mathbf{j} = 2(2.5\\mathbf{i} + 1.5\\mathbf{j})\\)\nA1 \\(p = 2\\), \\(q = 5\\)\n(b)\nB1 \\(\\sqrt{29} = 5.39\\) N (3 sf) FT\nB1 \\(\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right) = 68.2°\\) FT\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{s} = \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}t^2 = 20\\mathbf{i} + 12\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 \\(\\sqrt{544} = 23.3\\) m (3 sf)\nAnswer: (a) \\(p = 2\\), \\(q = 5\\) (b) 5.39 N, 68.2° (c) 23.3 m` },
      { q: `A triangular glass panel is modelled as the triangle \\(ABC\\), where \\(A\\), \\(B\\) and \\(C\\) have coordinates \\((2, 1, 0)\\), \\((5, -3, 0)\\) and \\((5, 1, k)\\) respectively, with \\(k > 0\\). Units are metres. The triangle is isosceles with \\(AB = AC\\).\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(k\\). [3]\n\n(b) Find the exact area of the panel. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nB1 \\(AB = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5\\)\nM1 \\(9 + k^2 = 25\\)\nA1 \\(k = 4\\) (rejecting \\(-4\\))\n(b)\nB1 \\(\\overrightarrow{BC} = 4\\mathbf{j} + 4\\mathbf{k}\\), \\(BC = 4\\sqrt{2}\\)\nM1 Perpendicular height from \\(A\\) to midpoint of \\(BC\\): \\(\\sqrt{25 - 8}\\) (or cosine rule \\(\\cos A = 0.36\\))\nA1 Height \\(\\sqrt{17}\\) (or \\(\\sin A = \\frac{4\\sqrt{34}}{25}\\))\nA1 Area \\(= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4\\sqrt{2} \\times \\sqrt{17} = 2\\sqrt{34}\\) m² cao\nAnswer: (a) \\(k = 4\\) (b) \\(2\\sqrt{34}\\) m²` },
      { q: `Two particles \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) move in a horizontal plane. At time \\(t = 0\\) seconds:\n• \\(P\\) is at the point with position vector \\((\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m, has velocity \\((4\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹, and moves with constant acceleration \\((-2\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻²;\n• \\(Q\\) is at the point with position vector \\((-2\\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j})\\) m and moves with constant velocity \\((2\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the position vector of \\(P\\) at time \\(t\\). [2]\n\n(b) Show that \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) collide, and find the time and the position vector of the point at which they collide. [4]\n\n(c) Find the speed of \\(P\\) at the instant of collision. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_P = \\mathbf{r}_0 + \\mathbf{u}t + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}t^2\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_P = (1 + 4t - t^2)\\mathbf{i} + (2 - 2t + t^2)\\mathbf{j}\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{r}_Q = (-2 + 2t)\\mathbf{i} + (-4 + 3t)\\mathbf{j}\\) and equates components\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{i}\\): \\(t^2 - 2t - 3 = 0\\), so \\(t = 3\\) (or \\(-1\\)); \\(\\mathbf{j}\\): \\(t^2 - 5t + 6 = 0\\), so \\(t = 2\\) or 3\nE1 Both components equal at the same time \\(t = 3\\), so they collide\nA1 Position vector \\((4\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j})\\) m\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{v}_P = (4 - 2t)\\mathbf{i} + (-2 + 2t)\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 At \\(t = 3\\): \\(-2\\mathbf{i} + 4\\mathbf{j}\\), speed \\(\\sqrt{20} = 4.47\\) m s⁻¹ (3 sf)\nAnswer: (b) \\(t = 3\\), \\((4\\mathbf{i} + 5\\mathbf{j})\\) m (c) \\(2\\sqrt{5} \\approx 4.47\\) m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `In the triangle \\(OAB\\), \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB} = \\mathbf{b}\\). The point \\(P\\) lies on \\(OA\\) with \\(OP = \\frac{2}{3}OA\\), and \\(Q\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\). The line \\(PQ\\) is extended to meet the line \\(OB\\), extended, at the point \\(R\\).\n\n(a) Find \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\) in terms of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{b}\\). [2]\n\n(b) Show that \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = 2\\mathbf{b}\\). [4]\n\n(c) Find the ratio \\(PQ : QR\\). [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{PQ} = \\overrightarrow{OQ} - \\overrightarrow{OP} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}) - \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a}\\)\nA1 \\(-\\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}\\)\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\frac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a} + \\lambda\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}\\right)\\)\nM1 \\(R\\) on line \\(OB\\) so \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = \\mu\\mathbf{b}\\); equates coefficients (\\(\\mathbf{a}\\), \\(\\mathbf{b}\\) not parallel)\nA1 \\(\\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{\\lambda}{6} = 0\\), so \\(\\lambda = 4\\)\nA1 \\(\\overrightarrow{OR} = 2\\mathbf{b}\\) AG\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\overrightarrow{PR} = 4\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\), so \\(\\overrightarrow{QR} = 3\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\)\nA1 \\(PQ : QR = 1 : 3\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(-\\frac{1}{6}\\mathbf{a} + \\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{b}\\) (c) \\(1 : 3\\)` },
      { q: `A particle \\(P\\) of mass 0.5 kg moves in a horizontal plane. At time \\(t\\) seconds, where \\(t \\geq 0\\), the position vector of \\(P\\) relative to a fixed origin \\(O\\) is \\(\\mathbf{r}\\) m, where\n\\(\\mathbf{r} = (2t^3 - 9t^2 + 12t)\\mathbf{i} + (t^2 - 4t)\\mathbf{j}\\).\n\n(a) Find the velocity of \\(P\\) at time \\(t\\). [2]\n\n(b) Show that \\(P\\) is instantaneously at rest at exactly one time, and find this time. [3]\n\n(c) Find the magnitude of the resultant force acting on \\(P\\) at the instant found in (b). [2]\n\n(d) Find the distance of \\(P\\) from \\(O\\) at this instant. [2]`, marks: 9, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 Differentiates at least one component correctly\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{v} = (6t^2 - 18t + 12)\\mathbf{i} + (2t - 4)\\mathbf{j}\\)\n(b)\nM1 Sets both components to zero\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{i}\\): \\(6(t - 1)(t - 2) = 0\\), \\(t = 1\\) or 2; \\(\\mathbf{j}\\): \\(t = 2\\)\nE1 Only \\(t = 2\\) satisfies both, so at rest only when \\(t = 2\\)\n(c)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{a} = (12t - 18)\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j} = 6\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j}\\) at \\(t = 2\\), and \\(\\mathbf{F} = 0.5\\mathbf{a}\\)\nA1 \\(\\mathbf{F} = 3\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}\\), \\(|\\mathbf{F}| = \\sqrt{10} = 3.16\\) N (3 sf)\n(d)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = (16 - 36 + 24)\\mathbf{i} + (4 - 8)\\mathbf{j} = 4\\mathbf{i} - 4\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 \\(4\\sqrt{2} = 5.66\\) m (3 sf)\nAnswer: (b) \\(t = 2\\) (c) \\(\\sqrt{10} \\approx 3.16\\) N (d) \\(4\\sqrt{2} \\approx 5.66\\) m` },
      { q: `A light aircraft is coming in to land. Its position relative to a control tower at \\(O\\) is modelled, at time \\(t\\) minutes, by\n\\(\\mathbf{r} = (-10\\mathbf{i} + 10\\mathbf{j} + 2\\mathbf{k}) + t(2\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k})\\),\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{i}\\) and \\(\\mathbf{j}\\) are horizontal unit vectors directed east and north, \\(\\mathbf{k}\\) is a unit vector vertically upwards, and distances are in kilometres. The ground is modelled as the horizontal plane through \\(O\\).\n\n(a) Find the speed of the aircraft in km h⁻¹. [2]\n\n(b) Find the angle that the path of the aircraft makes with the horizontal. [2]\n\n(c) Find the position vector of the point at which the model predicts the aircraft reaches the ground, and the distance of this point from \\(O\\). [3]\n\n(d) Find the distance of the aircraft from \\(O\\) when \\(t = 1\\). [2]\n\n(e) State one limitation of the model. [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a)\nM1 \\(\\sqrt{4 + 4 + 1} = 3\\) km per minute\nA1 180 km h⁻¹\n(b)\nM1 \\(\\sin\\theta = \\frac{1}{3}\\) or \\(\\tan\\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}\\)\nA1 \\(19.5°\\) (3 sf)\n(c)\nM1 Sets \\(\\mathbf{k}\\)-component \\(2 - t = 0\\)\nA1 \\(t = 2\\), \\(\\mathbf{r} = -6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j}\\)\nA1 Distance \\(6\\sqrt{2} = 8.49\\) km (3 sf)\n(d)\nM1 \\(\\mathbf{r} = -8\\mathbf{i} + 8\\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k}\\)\nA1 \\(\\sqrt{129} = 11.4\\) km (3 sf)\n(e)\nB1 Any one sensible limitation, e.g. aircraft would not descend at a constant velocity all the way to touchdown (it levels off / slows); the aircraft is modelled as a particle; wind is ignored; the ground is not exactly horizontal\nAnswer: (a) 180 km h⁻¹ (b) 19.5° (c) \\(-6\\mathbf{i} + 6\\mathbf{j}\\), 8.49 km (d) 11.4 km` }
    ]
  },
  '2.1': {
    name: 'Statistics',
    questions: [
      { q: `A council analyst wants to estimate the proportion of workers in a local authority who travel to work by train. She asks the first 80 people entering the local railway station on a Monday morning how they normally travel to work.\n\nGive two reasons why this sample is unlikely to be representative of all workers in the local authority.`, marks: 2, command: 'Give', markScheme: `E1 Everyone sampled is at a railway station, so train users are over-represented / people who drive, walk or work from home are excluded\nE1 Second distinct reason, e.g. only one day and one time of day (shift workers, part-time workers missed) / station users may live or work in a different local authority / first 80 is an opportunity sample, not random\nAnswer: e.g. sample biased towards train users; only people travelling at one time on one day` },
      { q: `The lengths, \\(x\\) mm, of a sample of leaves from a hedge are coded using \\(y = \\frac{x - 150}{2}\\). For the coded data the mean is 3.4 and the standard deviation is 1.8.\n\nFind the mean and the standard deviation of the lengths of the leaves.\n\n(data written for this practice question)`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Mean \\(= 150 + 2 \\times 3.4 = 156.8\\) mm\nB1 Standard deviation \\(= 2 \\times 1.8 = 3.6\\) mm (B0 if 150 is added to the standard deviation)\nAnswer: mean 156.8 mm, standard deviation 3.6 mm` },
      { q: `The time, \\(T\\) minutes, that a commuter spends driving to work is modelled by \\(T \\sim \\mathrm{N}(45, 6^2)\\).\n\nFind the probability that, on a randomly chosen day, the journey takes more than 52 minutes.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 \\(P(T > 52)\\) with standardisation \\(\\frac{52 - 45}{6}\\) soi, or correct calculator use\nA1 0.122 (awrt 0.122) BC\nAnswer: 0.122 (3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `At a school fair, a game uses a biased spinner. The score, \\(X\\), on one spin has the probability distribution given by\n\\(P(X = x) = kx\\) for \\(x = 1, 2, 3, 4\\),\nwhere \\(k\\) is a constant.\n\nFind the value of \\(k\\) and hence find \\(P(X \\geq 3)\\).`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(k(1 + 2 + 3 + 4) = 1\\) so \\(k = \\frac{1}{10}\\)\nB1 ft \\(P(X \\geq 3) = \\frac{3 + 4}{10} = \\frac{7}{10}\\)\nAnswer: \\(k = 0.1\\), \\(P(X \\geq 3) = 0.7\\)` },
      { q: `A researcher wants to survey 60 adults in a town about how they travel to work. The numbers of adults in the town in three age groups are shown below.\n\n16–24: 1840\n25–49: 4210\n50–64: 2650\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe researcher decides to take a sample stratified by age group.\n\nFind the number of adults from each age group that should be in the sample, and explain how the adults within each age group should then be chosen.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses \\(\\frac{60}{8700}\\) × group size for at least one group (e.g. 12.69…)\nA1 13, 29, 18 (must total 60)\nE1 Within each group choose adults by simple random sampling, e.g. number the adults in a list (electoral roll) and use random numbers to select\nAnswer: 13 aged 16–24, 29 aged 25–49, 18 aged 50–64, chosen randomly within each group` },
      { q: `A histogram shows the journey times, \\(t\\) minutes, of the employees of a company. On the histogram the bar for \\(20 \\leq t < 30\\) has frequency density 4.2 and the bar for \\(30 \\leq t < 50\\) has frequency density 1.5.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nEstimate the number of employees whose journey time is between 25 and 40 minutes.`, marks: 3, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `M1 Frequency = frequency density × class width for either bar: 42 and 30\nM1 Uses the appropriate fractions of the classes, e.g. \\(\\frac{5}{10} \\times 42 + \\frac{10}{20} \\times 30\\) (assumes data evenly spread in each class)\nA1 36\nAnswer: 36 employees` },
      { q: `In a survey of 120 sixth-form students, 50 walk to college, 38 have a part-time job and 18 both walk to college and have a part-time job.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nOne of these students is chosen at random.\n\n(a) Find the probability that the student neither walks to college nor has a part-time job. [1]\n(b) Given that the student walks to college, find the probability that the student has a part-time job. [2]`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(\\frac{120 - (50 + 38 - 18)}{120} = \\frac{50}{120} = \\frac{5}{12}\\)\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{P(J \\cap W)}{P(W)} = \\frac{18/120}{50/120}\\) or \\(\\frac{18}{50}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{9}{25}\\) or 0.36\nAnswer: (a) \\(\\frac{5}{12}\\) (b) 0.36` },
      { q: `On a randomly chosen weekday, \\(L\\) is the event that a particular bus is late and \\(R\\) is the event that it rains. It is given that \\(P(L) = 0.35\\), \\(P(R) = 0.2\\) and \\(P(L \\cap R) = 0.09\\).\n\nDetermine whether the events \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are independent. Fully justify your answer and interpret it in context.`, marks: 3, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `M1 Compares \\(P(L) \\times P(R)\\) with \\(P(L \\cap R)\\), or \\(P(L \\mid R)\\) with \\(P(L)\\)\nA1 \\(0.35 \\times 0.2 = 0.07 \\neq 0.09\\) (or \\(P(L \\mid R) = 0.45 \\neq 0.35\\)), so not independent\nE1 In context: the bus is more likely to be late when it rains\nAnswer: not independent; the bus is more likely to be late on rainy days` },
      { q: `A garden centre sells packets of seeds. The probability that a seed fails to germinate is 0.08, independently of other seeds. A customer plants 25 seeds.\n\nFind the probability that at least 3 of the seeds fail to germinate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(25, 0.08)\\) soi\nM1 \\(P(X \\geq 3) = 1 - P(X \\leq 2)\\)\nA1 0.323 (awrt) BC\nAnswer: 0.323 (3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `The masses of apples sold by a farm shop are modelled by a normal distribution with mean 152 g and standard deviation 14 g. The shop labels the heaviest 10% of apples as "extra large".\n\nFind the least mass of an apple labelled "extra large".`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(z = 1.282\\) (awrt 1.28) soi\nM1 \\(\\frac{m - 152}{14} = 1.282\\) or inverse normal with \\(P(X > m) = 0.1\\)\nA1 170 g (awrt 169.9)\nAnswer: 170 g (3 s.f.)` },
      { q: `OCR's Large Data Set contains census data on the methods of travel to work used by employed people in each local authority. A student takes a sample of local authorities and, for each, calculates the percentage of workers who cycle to work, \\(c\\), and the percentage who drive a car or van to work, \\(d\\). She finds that the product moment correlation coefficient between \\(c\\) and \\(d\\) is \\(-0.58\\).\n\n(value written for this practice question)\n\nThe student says "This shows that encouraging people to cycle causes fewer people to drive to work."\n\nInterpret the value \\(-0.58\\) in context and comment on the student's statement.`, marks: 3, command: 'Interpret', markScheme: `B1 Moderate negative correlation: local authorities with a higher percentage of cyclists tend to have a lower percentage of drivers\nE1 Correlation does not imply causation\nE1 Suggests a third factor, e.g. urban authorities have shorter distances and better facilities so more cycling and less driving / rural areas have longer journeys\nAnswer: moderate negative correlation; the statement is not justified as correlation does not show causation` },
      { q: `The journey times to work, in minutes, of a group of employees have lower quartile 18 and upper quartile 34. The two longest journey times are 61 minutes and 75 minutes, and the shortest is 2 minutes.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nAn outlier is defined as a value more than 1.5 × the interquartile range below the lower quartile or above the upper quartile.\n\n(a) Determine which, if any, of these journey times are outliers. [3]\n(b) Explain whether the outliers should be removed before analysing the data. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(a) M1 IQR = 16 and \\(1.5 \\times 16 = 24\\)\nA1 Limits \\(18 - 24 = -6\\) and \\(34 + 24 = 58\\)\nA1 61 and 75 are outliers; 2 is not\n(b) E1 They should not be removed unless they are errors: long commutes (e.g. from another town) are plausible genuine values\nAnswer: 61 and 75 are outliers; keep them unless shown to be errors` },
      { q: `The distances, \\(x\\) km, that 25 employees at an office travel to work are summarised by\n\\(\\sum x = 310\\) and \\(\\sum x^2 = 4630\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the mean and the standard deviation of the distances. [3]\n(b) A new employee joins the office and travels exactly 12.4 km to work. State, with a reason, the effect on the standard deviation of including this employee. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Mean \\(= \\frac{310}{25} = 12.4\\) km\nM1 \\(\\frac{4630}{25} - 12.4^2\\) (= 31.44) or \\(\\frac{4630 - 25 \\times 12.4^2}{24}\\) (= 32.75)\nA1 5.61 km (or 5.72 km using divisor 24)\n(b) E1 Standard deviation decreases, because the new value equals the mean so it adds no deviation but n increases\nAnswer: mean 12.4 km, sd 5.61 km (5.72); sd decreases` },
      { q: `Ravi travels to work by train and then a connecting bus. The probability that his train is late is 0.15. If the train is late, the probability that he catches the connecting bus is 0.4. If the train is not late, the probability that he catches the connecting bus is 0.9.\n\n(a) Find the probability that, on a randomly chosen day, Ravi catches the connecting bus. [2]\n(b) Given that Ravi misses the connecting bus, find the probability that his train was late. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(0.15 \\times 0.4 + 0.85 \\times 0.9\\)\nA1 0.825\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{0.15 \\times 0.6}{1 - 0.825}\\) ft their (a)\nA1 \\(\\frac{18}{35}\\) or 0.514 (3 s.f.)\nAnswer: (a) 0.825 (b) 0.514` },
      { q: `The masses of bags of rice filled by a machine are modelled by a normal distribution with mean 500 g and standard deviation \\(\\sigma\\) g. It is known that 10% of bags have a mass greater than 512 g.\n\n(a) Find the value of \\(\\sigma\\). [3]\n(b) Hence find the proportion of bags with mass less than 495 g. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(z = 1.2816\\) (awrt 1.28)\nM1 \\(\\frac{512 - 500}{\\sigma} = 1.2816\\)\nA1 \\(\\sigma = 9.36\\) (awrt)\n(b) B1 ft 0.297 (awrt 0.297)\nAnswer: (a) 9.36 (b) 0.297` },
      { q: `The journey times, \\(t\\) minutes, of 100 workers are summarised below.\n\n\\(0 \\leq t < 10\\): 14\n\\(10 \\leq t < 20\\): 26\n\\(20 \\leq t < 30\\): 32\n\\(30 \\leq t < 45\\): 18\n\\(45 \\leq t < 60\\): 10\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nUse linear interpolation to estimate the median and the interquartile range of the journey times.`, marks: 4, command: 'Estimate', markScheme: `M1 Cumulative frequencies 14, 40, 72, 90, 100 used, with interpolation in the correct class for at least one of median, Q1, Q3\nA1 Median \\(= 20 + \\frac{10}{32} \\times 10 = 23.1\\) min\nA1 \\(Q_1 = 10 + \\frac{11}{26} \\times 10 = 14.2\\) and \\(Q_3 = 30 + \\frac{3}{18} \\times 15 = 32.5\\)\nA1 IQR = 18.3 min (allow 18.2–18.3)\nAllow use of 25.25 / 50.5 / 75.75 positions\nAnswer: median 23.1 min, IQR 18.3 min` },
      { q: `A shop manager believes that the proportion of customers who pay using a phone app is greater than 25%. She will take a random sample of 30 customers and test, at the 5% significance level, \\(H_0: p = 0.25\\) against \\(H_1: p > 0.25\\), where \\(p\\) is the proportion of all customers who pay using a phone app.\n\n(a) Find the critical region for the test. [3]\n(b) State the probability that the test results in rejecting \\(H_0\\) when \\(H_0\\) is true. [1]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(30, 0.25)\\) soi\nM1 Considers upper tail probabilities, e.g. \\(P(X \\geq 12) = 0.0507\\) and \\(P(X \\geq 13) = 0.0216\\)\nA1 \\(X \\geq 13\\)\n(b) B1 0.0216 (awrt)\nAnswer: (a) \\(\\{X \\geq 13\\}\\) (b) 0.0216` },
      { q: `A café owner records, for 20 randomly chosen days, the number of hours of sunshine and the number of ice creams sold. The product moment correlation coefficient is 0.43.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe critical value of the product moment correlation coefficient for a one-tailed test at the 5% level with sample size 20 is 0.3783.\n\nTest, at the 5% significance level, whether there is positive correlation between hours of sunshine and ice cream sales.`, marks: 4, command: 'Test', markScheme: `B1 \\(H_0: \\rho = 0\\), \\(H_1: \\rho > 0\\), where \\(\\rho\\) is the population correlation coefficient between hours of sunshine and ice cream sales\nM1 Compares 0.43 with 0.3783\nA1 \\(0.43 > 0.3783\\) so reject \\(H_0\\)\nE1 There is sufficient evidence at the 5% level of positive correlation between hours of sunshine and ice cream sales (conclusion in context, not over-assertive)\nAnswer: reject \\(H_0\\); evidence of positive correlation` },
      { q: `Using OCR's Large Data Set, a student calculates, for each local authority in two regions, the percentage of employed people who work mainly at or from home. The results are summarised below.\n\nRegion A: minimum 2.1, lower quartile 3.4, median 4.0, upper quartile 4.9, maximum 7.8\nRegion B: minimum 2.8, lower quartile 4.6, median 5.5, upper quartile 6.6, maximum 9.2\n\n(values written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Compare the two distributions. [2]\n(b) Determine whether the maximum value for Region A is an outlier, using the definition of an outlier as more than 1.5 × IQR above the upper quartile. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Compare', markScheme: `(a) B1 Median higher in B (5.5 v 4.0): on average a greater percentage of people work at home in local authorities in Region B\nB1 IQR larger in B (2.0 v 1.5): percentages are more varied between local authorities in Region B\n(b) M1 \\(4.9 + 1.5 \\times 1.5 = 7.15\\)\nA1 \\(7.8 > 7.15\\) so it is an outlier\nAnswer: B higher on average and more spread; 7.8 is an outlier` },
      { q: `The times taken by students to complete a puzzle are modelled by a normal distribution. It is found that 15% of students take less than 20 minutes and 5% of students take more than 35 minutes.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nFind the mean and the standard deviation of the times.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 \\(z = -1.036\\) and \\(z = 1.645\\) (awrt)\nM1 \\(\\frac{20 - \\mu}{\\sigma} = -1.036\\)\nM1 \\(\\frac{35 - \\mu}{\\sigma} = 1.645\\) and attempts to solve simultaneously\nA1 \\(\\sigma = 5.59\\) (awrt)\nA1 \\(\\mu = 25.8\\) (awrt)\nAnswer: mean 25.8 min, standard deviation 5.59 min` },
      { q: `Records show that 40% of the employees at a large company travel to work by car. After the company introduces a cycle-to-work scheme, the manager believes that the proportion who travel to work by car has decreased. A random sample of 25 employees is taken and 5 of them travel to work by car.\n\nTest, at the 5% significance level, whether there is evidence that the proportion of employees who travel to work by car has decreased.`, marks: 5, command: 'Test', markScheme: `B1 \\(H_0: p = 0.4\\), \\(H_1: p < 0.4\\), where \\(p\\) is the proportion of all employees who travel to work by car\nB1 \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(25, 0.4)\\) under \\(H_0\\)\nM1 \\(P(X \\leq 5)\\) attempted\nA1 0.0294 (awrt), compared with 0.05 (or critical region \\(X \\leq 5\\))\nE1 Reject \\(H_0\\); there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level that the proportion of employees who travel by car has decreased\nAnswer: \\(P(X \\leq 5) = 0.0294 < 0.05\\), reject \\(H_0\\)` },
      { q: `Before a new bus lane opened, the time for a bus journey into a city centre was modelled by a normal distribution with mean 32 minutes and standard deviation 4.5 minutes. After the bus lane opened, a random sample of 12 journeys had a mean time of 29.6 minutes.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nAssuming that the standard deviation is unchanged, test at the 5% significance level whether the mean journey time has decreased.`, marks: 5, command: 'Test', markScheme: `B1 \\(H_0: \\mu = 32\\), \\(H_1: \\mu < 32\\), where \\(\\mu\\) is the population mean journey time after the bus lane opened\nB1 \\(\\bar{X} \\sim \\mathrm{N}\\left(32, \\frac{4.5^2}{12}\\right)\\)\nM1 \\(P(\\bar{X} \\leq 29.6)\\) or \\(z = \\frac{29.6 - 32}{4.5/\\sqrt{12}}\\)\nA1 0.0323 (or \\(z = -1.85\\) compared with \\(-1.645\\))\nE1 Reject \\(H_0\\); there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level that the mean journey time has decreased\nAnswer: p-value 0.0323 < 0.05, reject \\(H_0\\)` },
      { q: `A survey of 200 workers recorded their age group and main method of travel to work.\n\nUnder 40: car 48, public transport 30, walk or cycle 22\n40 and over: car 62, public transport 24, walk or cycle 14\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nOne of the 200 workers is chosen at random.\n\n(a) Find the probability that the worker travels by car. [1]\n(b) Given that the worker walks or cycles, find the probability that the worker is under 40. [2]\n(c) Determine whether the events "travels by car" and "is aged 40 and over" are independent. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(\\frac{110}{200} = 0.55\\)\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{22}{22 + 14}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{11}{18}\\) or 0.611\n(c) M1 Compares \\(P(C \\cap O) = \\frac{62}{200} = 0.31\\) with \\(P(C) \\times P(O) = 0.55 \\times 0.5 = 0.275\\)\nA1 \\(0.31 \\neq 0.275\\), so not independent\nAnswer: (a) 0.55 (b) \\(\\frac{11}{18}\\) (c) not independent` },
      { q: `The distances, \\(d\\) km, travelled to work by 80 employees are summarised below.\n\n\\(0 \\leq d < 2\\): 12\n\\(2 \\leq d < 5\\): 18\n\\(5 \\leq d < 10\\): 25\n\\(10 \\leq d < 20\\): 15\n\\(20 \\leq d < 40\\): 10\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Calculate estimates of the mean and the standard deviation of the distances. [4]\n(b) Explain why your answers are only estimates. [1]`, marks: 5, command: 'Calculate', markScheme: `(a) M1 Midpoints 1, 3.5, 7.5, 15, 30 used\nA1 Mean \\(= \\frac{787.5}{80} = 9.84\\) km\nM1 \\(\\frac{14013.75}{80} - 9.84375^2\\) (= 78.27)\nA1 Standard deviation 8.85 km (8.90 with divisor 79)\n(b) E1 The exact distances are not known; midpoints assume the values are evenly spread within each class\nAnswer: mean 9.84 km, sd 8.85 km` },
      { q: `In a town, 30% of households do not own a car. A random sample of 200 households is chosen and \\(X\\) is the number of these households that do not own a car.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Explain why a normal distribution can be used as an approximation to the distribution of \\(X\\). [1]\n(b) Use a suitable normal approximation to find \\(P(X \\leq 50)\\). [4]`, marks: 5, command: 'Use', markScheme: `(a) E1 \\(n\\) is large and \\(np = 60\\) and \\(n(1 - p) = 140\\) are both greater than 5 (p not close to 0 or 1)\n(b) B1 \\(\\mathrm{N}(60, 42)\\)\nB1 Continuity correction: 50.5\nM1 \\(P\\left(Z < \\frac{50.5 - 60}{\\sqrt{42}}\\right)\\)\nA1 0.0713 (awrt); exact binomial 0.0695 scores 0 for the last three marks\nAnswer: 0.0713` },
      { q: `For a sample of second-hand cars of one model, the age, \\(x\\) years, and the value, \\(y\\) thousand pounds, were recorded. The ages ranged from 1 to 8 years. The equation of the regression line of \\(y\\) on \\(x\\) is\n\\(y = 14.2 - 1.35x\\)\nand the product moment correlation coefficient is \\(-0.94\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Interpret the value 1.35 in context. [1]\n(b) Use the regression line to estimate the value of a 5-year-old car. [1]\n(c) Comment on the reliability of using the line to estimate the value of a 5-year-old car and of a 12-year-old car. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Interpret', markScheme: `(a) B1 For each additional year of age, the value decreases by about £1350\n(b) B1 \\(14.2 - 1.35 \\times 5 = 7.45\\), i.e. £7450\n(c) E1 5 years is within the data range (interpolation) so reliable\nE1 r = −0.94 shows strong correlation, so points lie close to the line, supporting reliability\nE1 12 years is outside 1–8 (extrapolation) so unreliable; the line gives a negative value (−2.0), which is impossible\nAnswer: (b) £7450; reliable for 5 years, unreliable for 12 years` },
      { q: `A bag contains 5 red, 4 blue and 3 green counters. Two counters are taken from the bag at random, without replacement.\n\n(a) Find the probability that both counters are the same colour. [3]\n(b) Given that both counters are the same colour, find the probability that they are both red. [2]\n(c) Find the probability that at least one of the counters is green. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 One correct product without replacement, e.g. \\(\\frac{5}{12} \\times \\frac{4}{11}\\)\nM1 Sum of three products \\(\\frac{20 + 12 + 6}{132}\\)\nA1 \\(\\frac{19}{66}\\) (0.288)\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{20/132}{38/132}\\) ft their (a)\nA1 \\(\\frac{10}{19}\\)\n(c) B1 \\(1 - \\frac{9}{12} \\times \\frac{8}{11} = \\frac{5}{11}\\)\nAnswer: (a) \\(\\frac{19}{66}\\) (b) \\(\\frac{10}{19}\\) (c) \\(\\frac{5}{11}\\)` },
      { q: `A seed company claims that 15% of its tomato seeds fail to germinate. A gardener suspects that this figure is wrong. She plants a random sample of 40 seeds and finds that 11 fail to germinate.\n\nTest, at the 5% significance level, whether the proportion of seeds that fail to germinate is different from 15%.`, marks: 6, command: 'Test', markScheme: `B1 \\(H_0: p = 0.15\\), \\(H_1: p \\neq 0.15\\), where \\(p\\) is the proportion of all the company's seeds that fail to germinate\nB1 \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(40, 0.15)\\) under \\(H_0\\)\nM1 \\(P(X \\geq 11) = 1 - P(X \\leq 10)\\) attempted\nA1 0.0299 (awrt)\nM1 Compares with 0.025 (two-tailed)\nA1 \\(0.0299 > 0.025\\): do not reject \\(H_0\\); there is insufficient evidence at the 5% level that the proportion of seeds that fail to germinate is different from 15%\nComparing with 0.05 and rejecting: max B1 B1 M1 A1 M0 A0\nAnswer: do not reject \\(H_0\\)` },
      { q: `The journey times, \\(x\\) minutes, of 15 workers at Office A are coded using \\(y = x - 30\\). It is found that\n\\(\\sum y = -42\\) and \\(\\sum y^2 = 610\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the mean and the standard deviation of the journey times of the 15 workers. [4]\n(b) The mean journey time of 10 workers at Office B is 33.5 minutes. Find the mean journey time of all 25 workers. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(\\bar{y} = \\frac{-42}{15} = -2.8\\)\nA1 \\(\\bar{x} = 27.2\\) min\nM1 \\(\\frac{610}{15} - (-2.8)^2\\) (= 32.83)\nA1 sd = 5.73 min (5.93 with divisor 14); the coding does not change the sd\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{15 \\times 27.2 + 10 \\times 33.5}{25}\\) ft their mean\nA1 29.72 min (29.7)\nAnswer: (a) mean 27.2 min, sd 5.73 min (b) 29.7 min` },
      { q: `The times, \\(t\\) minutes, taken by 170 commuters to travel to work are summarised below.\n\n\\(0 \\leq t < 10\\): 25\n\\(10 \\leq t < 15\\): 30\n\\(15 \\leq t < 20\\): 40\n\\(20 \\leq t < 30\\): 45\n\\(30 \\leq t < 60\\): 30\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nA histogram is drawn to represent the data. The bar for \\(15 \\leq t < 20\\) has height 6.4 cm.\n\n(a) Find the height of the bar for \\(30 \\leq t < 60\\). [2]\n(b) Estimate the number of commuters whose journey time is between 12 and 25 minutes. [2]\n(c) Calculate an estimate of the mean journey time. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Frequency densities 8 and 1 (or scale 0.8 cm per unit of frequency density)\nA1 0.8 cm\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{3}{5} \\times 30 + 40 + \\frac{5}{10} \\times 45\\)\nA1 80.5, so about 80 or 81 commuters\n(c) M1 \\(\\frac{5 \\times 25 + 12.5 \\times 30 + 17.5 \\times 40 + 25 \\times 45 + 45 \\times 30}{170}\\)\nA1 21.6 min (\\(\\frac{3675}{170}\\))\nAnswer: (a) 0.8 cm (b) 80 (c) 21.6 min` },
      { q: `The breaking strength of a type of climbing rope is modelled by a normal distribution with mean 850 N and standard deviation 40 N.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen rope has a breaking strength of less than 800 N. [2]\n(b) A club buys 6 of these ropes. Find the probability that at least one of them has a breaking strength of less than 800 N. State an assumption you have made. [2]\n(c) Find the value of \\(s\\) such that 99% of ropes have a breaking strength greater than \\(s\\) N. [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Standardises \\(\\frac{800 - 850}{40} = -1.25\\) or correct calculator use\nA1 0.106 (awrt)\n(b) M1 \\(1 - (1 - 0.1056)^6\\) and assumption that the strengths of the ropes are independent\nA1 0.488 (awrt)\n(c) M1 \\(\\frac{s - 850}{40} = -2.326\\)\nA1 \\(s = 757\\) (awrt)\nAnswer: (a) 0.106 (b) 0.488 (c) 757 N` },
      { q: `A worker in a city is chosen at random. \\(A\\) is the event that the worker uses a train on the journey to work and \\(B\\) is the event that the worker uses a bus. \\(P(A) = 0.45\\), \\(P(B) = 0.32\\) and \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are independent. \\(C\\) is the event that the worker walks all the way to work; \\(C\\) is mutually exclusive with both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), and \\(P(C) = 0.2\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find \\(P(A \\cap B)\\). [1]\n(b) Find \\(P(A \\cup B)\\). [2]\n(c) Find the probability that none of the events \\(A\\), \\(B\\), \\(C\\) occurs. [1]\n(d) Find \\(P(A \\mid C')\\). [2]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(0.45 \\times 0.32 = 0.144\\)\n(b) M1 \\(0.45 + 0.32 - 0.144\\)\nA1 0.626\n(c) B1 ft \\(1 - 0.626 - 0.2 = 0.174\\)\n(d) M1 \\(\\frac{P(A \\cap C')}{P(C')} = \\frac{0.45}{0.8}\\) (since \\(A \\subseteq C'\\))\nA1 0.5625\nAnswer: (a) 0.144 (b) 0.626 (c) 0.174 (d) 0.5625` },
      { q: `A machine fills bottles with water. When working correctly, the volume of water in a bottle is normally distributed with mean 500 ml and standard deviation 3.2 ml. After the machine is serviced, a random sample of 16 bottles has a mean volume of 501.8 ml.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Test, at the 5% significance level, whether the mean volume has changed. [6]\n(b) State an assumption needed for the test to be valid. [1]`, marks: 7, command: 'Test', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(H_0: \\mu = 500\\), \\(H_1: \\mu \\neq 500\\), where \\(\\mu\\) is the population mean volume after the service\nB1 \\(\\bar{X} \\sim \\mathrm{N}\\left(500, \\frac{3.2^2}{16}\\right)\\), i.e. sd 0.8\nM1 \\(z = \\frac{501.8 - 500}{0.8}\\) or \\(P(\\bar{X} \\geq 501.8)\\)\nA1 \\(z = 2.25\\) or p = 0.0122\nM1 Compares with 1.96 or 0.025 (two-tailed)\nA1 Reject \\(H_0\\); there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level that the mean volume of water in a bottle has changed\n(b) B1 The standard deviation is unchanged at 3.2 ml (or volumes still normally distributed / sample is random)\nAnswer: z = 2.25 > 1.96, reject \\(H_0\\)` },
      { q: `OCR's Large Data Set contains census data for each local authority in England and Wales, including the numbers of employed people using each method of travel to work and the age structure of the population.\n\n(a) A student wants a simple random sample of 30 local authorities from the data set. Describe how she could obtain this sample. [2]\n(b) The student calculates, for each local authority, the percentage of workers who travel to work by underground, metro or light rail. Explain why this distribution is likely to be strongly positively skewed, and state, with a reason, which measure of average would be more suitable. [3]\n(c) Explain why the student uses percentages of workers in each local authority rather than the numbers of workers when comparing local authorities. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Describe', markScheme: `(a) M1 Number the local authorities in the list consecutively\nA1 Use a random number generator to select 30 different numbers, ignoring repeats\n(b) E1 Most local authorities have no such system, so their percentages are 0 or very close to 0\nE1 A few (e.g. London boroughs or large cities) have very high percentages, giving a long tail to the right\nB1 Median, as it is not affected by the extreme high values\n(c) E1 Local authorities have very different numbers of workers / populations\nE1 Percentages allow a fair comparison of travel habits, whereas raw numbers mainly reflect the size of the authority\nAnswer: random number selection; skewed by a few city authorities, so use median; percentages adjust for size` },
      { q: `An archer hits the bullseye with each arrow with probability 0.3. She shoots 20 arrows and \\(X\\) is the number of bullseyes.\n\n(a) State, in context, two conditions needed for \\(X\\) to be modelled by \\(\\mathrm{B}(20, 0.3)\\). [2]\n(b) Find \\(P(X \\geq 8)\\). [2]\n(c) The archer shoots 20 arrows on each of 4 days. Find the probability that she hits at least 8 bullseyes on exactly one of the 4 days. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 The outcome of each arrow is independent of the others\nB1 The probability of hitting the bullseye is the same (0.3) for every arrow\n(b) M1 \\(1 - P(X \\leq 7)\\)\nA1 0.228 (awrt)\n(c) M1 Uses \\(\\mathrm{B}(4, 0.2277)\\) or \\(p(1 - p)^3\\)\nM1 \\(4 \\times 0.2277 \\times 0.7723^3\\)\nA1 0.420 (awrt)\nAnswer: (b) 0.228 (c) 0.420` },
      { q: `A factory has three machines, A, B and C, which make 50%, 30% and 20% of its components respectively. The proportions of components that are faulty are 2% for machine A, 3% for machine B and 5% for machine C.\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the probability that a randomly chosen component is faulty. [2]\n(b) Given that a randomly chosen component is faulty, find the probability that it was made by machine C. [2]\n(c) Two components are chosen at random, independently. Given that at least one of them is faulty, find the probability that both are faulty. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 \\(0.5 \\times 0.02 + 0.3 \\times 0.03 + 0.2 \\times 0.05\\)\nA1 0.029\n(b) M1 \\(\\frac{0.2 \\times 0.05}{0.029}\\) ft\nA1 0.345 (\\(\\frac{10}{29}\\))\n(c) M1 \\(P(\\text{both}) = 0.029^2 = 0.000841\\)\nM1 \\(P(\\text{at least one}) = 1 - 0.971^2 = 0.057159\\)\nA1 0.0147 (awrt)\nAnswer: (a) 0.029 (b) 0.345 (c) 0.0147` },
      { q: `On 12 randomly chosen days, a café owner records the maximum temperature, \\(x\\) °C, and the number of hot chocolates sold, \\(y\\). The temperatures ranged from 4 °C to 18 °C. The product moment correlation coefficient is \\(-0.81\\) and the equation of the regression line of \\(y\\) on \\(x\\) is\n\\(y = 96.4 - 3.2x\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\nThe critical value of the product moment correlation coefficient for a one-tailed test at the 1% level with sample size 12 is 0.6581.\n\n(a) Test, at the 1% significance level, whether there is negative correlation between temperature and the number of hot chocolates sold. [4]\n(b) Use the regression line to estimate the number of hot chocolates sold on a day when the maximum temperature is 10 °C. [1]\n(c) Comment on the reliability of using the line to estimate sales on a day when the maximum temperature is 28 °C. [1]\n(d) Interpret the gradient of the regression line in context, and explain why the value 96.4 should not be interpreted as the number sold on a day with a maximum temperature of 0 °C. [2]`, marks: 8, command: 'Test', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(H_0: \\rho = 0\\), \\(H_1: \\rho < 0\\), \\(\\rho\\) the population correlation coefficient between temperature and hot chocolate sales\nM1 Compares \\(-0.81\\) with \\(-0.6581\\) (or |r| with 0.6581)\nA1 \\(-0.81 < -0.6581\\), reject \\(H_0\\)\nE1 There is sufficient evidence at the 1% level of negative correlation between temperature and hot chocolate sales\n(b) B1 \\(96.4 - 32 = 64.4\\), about 64\n(c) E1 Unreliable: 28 °C is outside the range of the data (extrapolation)\n(d) B1 For each 1 °C rise in maximum temperature, about 3.2 fewer hot chocolates are sold\nE1 0 °C is outside the data range 4–18 °C, so this is extrapolation\nAnswer: reject \\(H_0\\); about 64; unreliable at 28 °C` },
      { q: `The lengths, \\(L\\) cm, of fish in a lake are modelled by \\(L \\sim \\mathrm{N}(34, 5^2)\\).\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find \\(P(30 < L < 40)\\). [2]\n(b) A histogram of the lengths of a large sample of fish from the lake is roughly symmetrical and bell-shaped, with points of inflection at about 29 cm and 39 cm. Explain how this supports the model. [2]\n(c) Fish with length less than 27 cm must be returned to the lake. An angler catches 150 fish. Use a suitable normal approximation to find the probability that more than 15 of these fish must be returned. [4]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Standardises at least one limit, or correct calculator use\nA1 0.673 (awrt)\n(b) E1 Points of inflection of a normal curve are at \\(\\mu \\pm \\sigma\\)\nE1 29 and 39 are \\(34 \\pm 5\\), consistent with mean 34 and standard deviation 5 (and bell shape consistent with normal)\n(c) B1 \\(p = P(L < 27) = 0.0808\\), \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(150, 0.0808)\\)\nM1 Normal approximation \\(\\mathrm{N}(12.11, 11.14)\\) ft their p\nM1 Continuity correction \\(P(Y > 15.5)\\) and standardises\nA1 0.155 (awrt)\nAnswer: (a) 0.673 (c) 0.155` },
      { q: `A council states that 35% of workers in a town travel to work by bus. A campaigner believes that this figure is incorrect. She plans to ask a random sample of 50 workers in the town and test, at the 5% significance level, whether the proportion is different from 35%.\n\n(a) Write down suitable hypotheses for the test, defining any parameter used. [1]\n(b) Find the critical region for the test. [4]\n(c) State the actual significance level of the test. [1]\n(d) In her sample, 10 workers travel to work by bus. Carry out the test, giving your conclusion in context. [2]\n(e) Explain why it is important that the sample is chosen at random. [1]`, marks: 9, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 \\(H_0: p = 0.35\\), \\(H_1: p \\neq 0.35\\), where \\(p\\) is the proportion of all workers in the town who travel by bus\n(b) B1 \\(X \\sim \\mathrm{B}(50, 0.35)\\) and each tail at most 0.025\nM1 Lower tail: \\(P(X \\leq 10) = 0.0160\\), \\(P(X \\leq 11) = 0.0342\\)\nM1 Upper tail: \\(P(X \\geq 25) = 0.0207\\), \\(P(X \\geq 24) = 0.0396\\)\nA1 \\(X \\leq 10\\) or \\(X \\geq 25\\)\n(c) B1 \\(0.0160 + 0.0207 = 0.0367\\) (3.67%)\n(d) M1 10 lies in the critical region, so reject \\(H_0\\)\nA1 There is sufficient evidence at the 5% level that the proportion of workers who travel to work by bus is not 35%\n(e) E1 So that the sample is representative / unbiased, allowing a valid conclusion about all workers in the town\nAnswer: CR \\(X \\leq 10\\) or \\(X \\geq 25\\); actual significance level 0.0367; reject \\(H_0\\)` },
      { q: `The times, in minutes, taken by 15 employees at Firm P to travel to work are listed below.\n\n5 8 12 14 15 18 20 21 23 25 27 30 32 35 64\n\n(data written for this practice question)\n\n(a) Find the median and the quartiles of the times. [3]\n(b) Show that there is exactly one outlier, where an outlier is a value more than 1.5 × IQR below the lower quartile or above the upper quartile. [2]\n(c) Draw a box plot to represent the times, marking the outlier separately. [2]\n(d) For Firm Q the times have minimum 10, lower quartile 18, median 26, upper quartile 31 and maximum 42. Compare the journey times at the two firms. [2]\n(e) Explain why the interquartile range is a more suitable measure of spread than the range for Firm P. [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Draw', diagram: true, markScheme: `(a) B1 Median = 21\nB1 Lower quartile = 14\nB1 Upper quartile = 30\n(b) M1 IQR = 16, limits \\(14 - 24 = -10\\) and \\(30 + 24 = 54\\)\nA1 Only 64 exceeds 54 and no value is below \\(-10\\), so exactly one outlier AG\n(c) B1 Box from 14 to 30 with median at 21, on a labelled linear scale\nB1 Whiskers from 5 to 35, with 64 marked separately as a cross\n(d) B1 Median for Q (26) is higher than for P (21): employees at Q take longer on average\nB1 IQR for Q (13) is smaller than for P (16): times at Q are less varied\n(e) E1 The range is distorted by the outlier 64, while the IQR is not affected by extreme values\nAnswer: median 21, Q1 14, Q3 30; outlier 64; Q longer on average but less varied` }
    ]
  },
  '2.2': {
    name: 'Mechanics',
    questions: [
      { q: `A car travels along a straight road at a constant speed of 72 km h⁻¹.\n\nFind the distance, in metres, that the car travels in 45 seconds.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Converts speed: 72 km h⁻¹ = 20 m s⁻¹ soi\nB1 Distance = 20 × 45 = 900 m cao\nAnswer: 900 m` },
      { q: `A man of mass 70 kg stands on the floor of a lift. The lift is moving upwards and accelerating at 0.6 m s⁻². Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the magnitude of the normal contact force exerted on the man by the floor of the lift.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Newton's second law for the man with three terms: R − 70g = 70 × 0.6\nA1 R = 728 N cao\nM0 if weight and acceleration terms are on the same side with the wrong sign\nAnswer: 728 N` },
      { q: `A remote-controlled boat on a lake moves with constant velocity (3i − 4j) m s⁻¹, where i and j are unit vectors directed east and north respectively.\n\nFind the speed of the boat and the bearing on which it is moving.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `B1 Speed = √(3² + 4²) = 5 m s⁻¹\nB1 Bearing = 90° + tan⁻¹(4/3) = 143° (143.1°)\nAnswer: 5 m s⁻¹ on a bearing of 143°` },
      { q: `A stone is released from rest at a height of 20 m above horizontal ground. The stone is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the time taken for the stone to reach the ground.`, marks: 2, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses s = ut + ½at² with u = 0, s = 20, a = 9.8: 20 = 4.9t²\nA1 t = 2.02 s (2.0203…) awrt 2.02\nAnswer: 2.02 s` },
      { q: `A train is travelling along a straight horizontal track. It decelerates uniformly from 25 m s⁻¹ to 10 m s⁻¹ in 6 seconds.\n\n(a) Find the deceleration of the train. [1]\n(b) Find the distance the train travels in these 6 seconds. [2]`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) B1 Deceleration = (25 − 10)/6 = 2.5 m s⁻²\n(b) M1 Uses s = ½(u + v)t or equivalent complete suvat method\nA1 s = ½(25 + 10) × 6 = 105 m cao\nAnswer: (a) 2.5 m s⁻² (b) 105 m` },
      { q: `Three forces (5i + 2j) N, (−3i + 7j) N and F N act on a particle of mass 2 kg. The particle moves with acceleration (4i − j) m s⁻².\n\nFind F.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses Newton's second law in vector form: (5i + 2j) + (−3i + 7j) + F = 2(4i − j)\nA1 Resultant of given forces (2i + 9j) and ma = (8i − 2j) both correct soi\nA1 F = 6i − 11j cao\nAnswer: F = 6i − 11j` },
      { q: `A uniform plank AB has length 4 m and mass 20 kg. The plank rests horizontally on two supports, one at A and one at the point C, where AC = 3 m. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the magnitudes of the reactions at A and at C.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Takes moments about A (or C) with correct distances: 3R_C = 20g × 2\nA1 R_C = 40g/3 = 131 N (130.66…)\nB1 R_A = 20g − R_C = 65.3 N ft their R_C\nAnswer: R_A = 65.3 N, R_C = 131 N` },
      { q: `A crate of mass 5 kg is pulled along a rough horizontal floor by a horizontal rope. The tension in the rope is 25 N and the coefficient of friction between the crate and the floor is 0.3. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the acceleration of the crate.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Uses F = μR with R = 5g: F = 0.3 × 49 = 14.7 N\nM1 Newton's second law with tension, friction and ma: 25 − 14.7 = 5a\nA1 a = 2.06 m s⁻² cao\nAnswer: 2.06 m s⁻²` },
      { q: `A cyclist moves along a straight road. At time t seconds, where 0 ≤ t ≤ 6, the cyclist's velocity v m s⁻¹ is given by \\(v = 6t - t^2\\).\n\nFind the greatest velocity of the cyclist in this interval.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Differentiates and sets dv/dt = 6 − 2t = 0\nA1 t = 3\nA1 v = 9 m s⁻¹ cao\nAnswer: 9 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `A student models the flight of a cricket ball thrown across a field. The ball is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. The model predicts that the ball lands 42 m from the point of projection. When the throw is carried out, the ball lands 38 m from the point of projection.\n\n(a) State the meaning of modelling the ball as a particle. [1]\n(b) Suggest two refinements to the model that could improve the prediction. [2]`, marks: 3, command: 'Suggest', markScheme: `(a) B1 The ball's size is negligible / its mass is concentrated at a single point, so rotation and air resistance from its shape are ignored\n(b) B1 Include air resistance (acting against the motion)\nB1 Second distinct refinement, e.g. include the height above the ground from which the ball is released; include spin; use a more accurate value of g; include wind\nDo not accept "make the ball heavier" or vague "be more accurate"\nAnswer: see scheme` },
      { q: `A cyclist starts from rest and accelerates uniformly to a speed of V m s⁻¹ in 10 s. She then travels at V m s⁻¹ for 40 s and then decelerates uniformly to rest in a further 15 s. The total distance travelled is 1050 m.\n\nBy considering the area under a velocity–time graph, find V.`, marks: 3, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Area of trapezium (or triangles plus rectangle) equated to 1050\nA1 Correct equation, e.g. ½(10V) + 40V + ½(15V) = 1050 or ½(65 + 40)V = 1050\nA1 V = 20 cao\nAnswer: V = 20` },
      { q: `A small box of mass 3 kg is released from rest on a smooth ramp inclined at 25° to the horizontal. The box is modelled as a particle. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the speed of the box after it has travelled 4 m down the ramp.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Resolves weight parallel to the ramp: 3g sin 25° = 3a\nA1 a = 4.14 m s⁻² (4.1416…)\nM1 Uses v² = u² + 2as with u = 0, s = 4 and their a\nA1 v = 5.76 m s⁻¹ cao\nAnswer: 5.76 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `A block A of mass 3 kg rests on a smooth horizontal table. It is attached to one end of a light inextensible string which passes over a smooth pulley fixed at the edge of the table. A particle B of mass 2 kg hangs freely from the other end of the string. The system is released from rest with the string taut. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the acceleration of the system and the tension in the string.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Newton's second law for B: 2g − T = 2a, or for A: T = 3a\nM1 Second equation, or whole-system equation 2g = 5a\nA1 a = 3.92 m s⁻²\nA1 T = 11.8 N (11.76) cao\nAnswer: a = 3.92 m s⁻², T = 11.8 N` },
      { q: `A football is kicked from a point O on horizontal ground with speed 18 m s⁻¹ at an angle of 35° above the horizontal. The football is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the time for which the football is in the air. [2]\n(b) Find the horizontal distance from O to the point where the football lands. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Uses vertical motion: 0 = 18 sin 35° t − 4.9t², t ≠ 0\nA1 t = 2.11 s (2.107…)\n(b) M1 Horizontal distance = 18 cos 35° × their t\nA1 31.1 m (31.07…) cao\nAnswer: (a) 2.11 s (b) 31.1 m` },
      { q: `A particle P moves in a horizontal plane with constant acceleration (−i + 2j) m s⁻². At time t = 0, P is at the point with position vector (2i + 3j) m and has velocity (4i − j) m s⁻¹.\n\n(a) Find the position vector of P when t = 3. [2]\n(b) Find the value of t when P is moving parallel to the vector j. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Uses r = r₀ + ut + ½at² in vector form with t = 3\nA1 r = 9.5i + 9j\n(b) M1 v = (4 − t)i + (−1 + 2t)j and sets i-component = 0\nA1 t = 4 cao\nAnswer: (a) (9.5i + 9j) m (b) t = 4` },
      { q: `A car of mass 1200 kg tows a trailer of mass 400 kg along a straight horizontal road using a tow bar. The tow bar is modelled as a light rigid rod. The resistances to motion are 300 N on the car and 100 N on the trailer. The driving force of the car's engine is 2000 N.\n\n(a) Find the acceleration of the car and trailer. [2]\n(b) Find the tension in the tow bar. [2]`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Whole system: 2000 − 300 − 100 = 1600a\nA1 a = 1 m s⁻²\n(b) M1 Trailer alone: T − 100 = 400a (or car alone: 2000 − 300 − T = 1200a)\nA1 T = 500 N cao\nAnswer: (a) 1 m s⁻² (b) 500 N` },
      { q: `A uniform ladder of length 5 m and mass 15 kg rests with one end on rough horizontal ground and the other end against a smooth vertical wall. The ladder makes an angle of 60° with the ground and is about to slip.\n\nFind the exact value of the coefficient of friction between the ladder and the ground.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Moments about the foot of the ladder: S × 5 sin 60° = 15g × 2.5 cos 60°\nA1 S = 15g/(2√3) (= 42.4 N)\nM1 Resolves: R = 15g and F = S, with F = μR\nA1 μ = √3/6 cao (accept 1/(2√3))\nDecimal answer only 0.289: A0 for final mark (exact value required)\nAnswer: μ = √3/6` },
      { q: `A hanging basket of mass 2 kg is held in equilibrium by two light strings. One string is inclined at 30° to the horizontal and the other at 45° to the horizontal, on opposite sides of the basket. The basket is modelled as a particle. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the tension in each string.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Resolves horizontally: T₁ cos 30° = T₂ cos 45°\nM1 Resolves vertically: T₁ sin 30° + T₂ sin 45° = 2g\nA1 T₁ = 14.3 N (string at 30°)\nA1 T₂ = 17.6 N (string at 45°)\nAnswer: 14.3 N and 17.6 N` },
      { q: `A particle moves in a straight line. At time t seconds its acceleration is (4 − 2t) m s⁻². When t = 0, the particle is at the origin O and has velocity 3 m s⁻¹.\n\nFind the displacement of the particle from O when t = 3.`, marks: 4, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Integrates acceleration: v = 4t − t² + c\nA1 v = 3 + 4t − t²\nM1 Integrates again with constant: s = 3t + 2t² − t³/3 (+ 0)\nA1 s = 18 m cao\nAnswer: 18 m` },
      { q: `A box of mass 10 kg is pulled up a rough slope inclined at 20° to the horizontal by a rope parallel to a line of greatest slope. The coefficient of friction between the box and the slope is 0.4. The box accelerates up the slope at 0.5 m s⁻². Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\nFind the tension in the rope.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Resolves perpendicular to the slope: R = 10g cos 20°\nA1 R = 92.1 N\nM1 Uses F = 0.4R\nM1 Newton's second law parallel to slope with T, friction and weight component: T − 0.4R − 10g sin 20° = 10 × 0.5\nA1 T = 75.4 N cao\nAnswer: 75.4 N` },
      { q: `A particle of mass 4 kg is placed on a rough plane inclined at an angle α to the horizontal, where sin α = 0.6. The coefficient of friction between the particle and the plane is 0.5. The particle is released from rest. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Show that the particle slides down the plane. [2]\n(b) Find the speed of the particle 2 seconds after it is released. [3]`, marks: 5, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 R = 4g cos α = 4g × 0.8 = 31.36 N and maximum friction 0.5R = 15.68 N\nE1 Component of weight down the plane 4g × 0.6 = 23.52 N > 15.68 N, so the particle slides AG\n(b) M1 Newton's second law down the plane: 23.52 − 15.68 = 4a\nA1 a = 1.96 m s⁻²\nA1 v = 0 + 1.96 × 2 = 3.92 m s⁻¹ cao\nAnswer: (b) 3.92 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `A uniform beam AB has length 6 m and mass 40 kg. It rests horizontally on two supports at C and D, where AC = 1 m and DB = 2 m. A child stands on the beam at B. The child is modelled as a particle. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) The child has mass 15 kg. Find the magnitudes of the reactions at C and D. [3]\n(b) Find the greatest mass of child that can stand at B without the beam tilting. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Moments about D (or C) with correct distances: 3R_C + 15g × 2 = 40g × 1\nA1 R_C = 10g/3 = 32.7 N\nA1 R_D = 55g − R_C = 506 N (506.3)\n(b) M1 Sets R_C = 0 on point of tilting about D: m g × 2 = 40g × 1\nA1 m = 20 kg cao\nAnswer: (a) R_C = 32.7 N, R_D = 506 N (b) 20 kg` },
      { q: `A ball is thrown horizontally with speed 12 m s⁻¹ from the top of a vertical cliff which is 30 m above the sea. The ball is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the horizontal distance from the foot of the cliff to the point where the ball hits the sea. [3]\n(b) Find the speed of the ball as it hits the sea. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Vertical motion with u = 0: 30 = 4.9t²\nA1 t = 2.47 s (2.474…)\nA1 Distance = 12 × 2.474 = 29.7 m\n(b) M1 Vertical velocity = 9.8 × their t (= 24.25) and combines with 12 by Pythagoras\nA1 Speed = 27.1 m s⁻¹ cao\nAnswer: (a) 29.7 m (b) 27.1 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `Car A passes a point P on a straight road at a constant speed of 15 m s⁻¹. At the instant that A passes P, car B starts from rest at P and moves in the same direction as A with constant acceleration 2 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the time taken for B to catch up with A, and the distance from P at which this happens. [3]\n(b) Find the speed of B when it catches A, and comment on whether the model for B's motion is realistic. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Equates displacements: 15t = ½ × 2 × t²\nA1 t = 15 s (t ≠ 0)\nA1 Distance = 225 m\n(b) B1 Speed = 2 × 15 = 30 m s⁻¹\nE1 Comment, e.g. 30 m s⁻¹ (108 km h⁻¹) is high for a road, and a car is unlikely to maintain a constant acceleration for 15 s, so the model is unlikely to be realistic\nAnswer: (a) 15 s, 225 m (b) 30 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `In this question you must show detailed reasoning.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line. Its velocity v m s⁻¹ at time t seconds is given by \\(v = t^2 - 5t + 4\\).\n\nFind the total distance travelled by the particle in the interval 0 ≤ t ≤ 5.`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `M1 Finds when v = 0: (t − 1)(t − 4) = 0, t = 1, t = 4\nM1 Integrates: s = t³/3 − 5t²/2 + 4t\nA1 s(1) = 11/6, s(4) = −8/3, s(5) = −5/6\nM1 Sums magnitudes of displacements over separate intervals\nA1 Total distance = 11/6 + 27/6 + 11/6 = 49/6 m cao\nDR: values from calculator integration without working scores M0\nAnswer: 49/6 m (8.17 m)` },
      { q: `Two particles A and B, of masses 5 kg and 3 kg, are attached to the ends of a light inextensible string which passes over a smooth fixed pulley. The particles hang with the string taut and A is 2 m above the floor. The system is released from rest. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the acceleration of A and the tension in the string. [3]\n(b) Find the speed with which A hits the floor. [2]`, marks: 5, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Equations of motion for both particles: 5g − T = 5a and T − 3g = 3a\nA1 a = 2.45 m s⁻²\nA1 T = 36.75 N (36.8 N)\n(b) M1 v² = 0 + 2 × their a × 2\nA1 v = 3.13 m s⁻¹ cao\nAnswer: (a) 2.45 m s⁻², 36.8 N (b) 3.13 m s⁻¹` },
      { q: `A block A of mass 4 kg rests on a rough plane inclined at 30° to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between A and the plane is 0.25. A is attached to a light inextensible string which runs up the plane parallel to a line of greatest slope, passes over a smooth pulley at the top of the plane and is attached to a particle B of mass 5 kg hanging freely. The system is released from rest and A moves up the plane. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the magnitude of the frictional force acting on A. [2]\n(b) Find the acceleration of A and the tension in the string. [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 R = 4g cos 30° and F = 0.25R\nA1 F = 8.49 N (8.487…)\n(b) M1 For B: 5g − T = 5a\nM1 For A: T − 4g sin 30° − F = 4a\nA1 a = 2.32 m s⁻² (2.3236…)\nA1 T = 37.4 N cao\nAnswer: (a) 8.49 N (b) 2.32 m s⁻², 37.4 N` },
      { q: `A tennis ball is hit from a point 0.5 m above horizontal ground with speed 20 m s⁻¹ at an angle of 30° above the horizontal, towards a vertical wall 25 m away. The wall is 3 m high. The ball is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Determine whether the ball passes over the wall. [5]\n(b) State one assumption, other than modelling the ball as a particle, made in the model. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Determine', markScheme: `(a) M1 Horizontal motion: 25 = 20 cos 30° t\nA1 t = 1.443 s\nM1 Vertical displacement with their t: y = 20 sin 30° t − 4.9t²\nA1 y = 4.23 m above the point of projection, so height 4.73 m above ground\nE1 4.73 > 3, so the ball passes over the wall (by about 1.7 m)\n(b) B1 e.g. no air resistance; ball does not spin; the wall is perpendicular to the ball's path\nAnswer: (a) yes, height 4.73 m at the wall` },
      { q: `At noon, ship A is at the origin O and ship B is at the point with position vector (20i + 5j) km, where i and j are unit vectors east and north. Ship A moves with constant velocity (6i + 8j) km h⁻¹ and ship B moves with constant velocity (−2i + 10j) km h⁻¹.\n\n(a) Show that, t hours after noon, the position vector of B relative to A is ((20 − 8t)i + (5 + 2t)j) km. [2]\n(b) Find the least distance between the ships, giving your answer to 3 significant figures. [4]`, marks: 6, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) M1 r_A = t(6i + 8j) and r_B = (20i + 5j) + t(−2i + 10j)\nA1 r_B − r_A = (20 − 8t)i + (5 + 2t)j AG\n(b) M1 d² = (20 − 8t)² + (5 + 2t)²\nM1 Differentiates and sets equal to 0: −16(20 − 8t) + 4(5 + 2t) = 0\nA1 t = 75/34 (2.206)\nA1 Least distance 9.70 km cao\nAnswer: (b) 9.70 km` },
      { q: `A sledge of mass 12 kg is pulled across rough horizontal snow by a rope inclined at 25° above the horizontal. The tension in the rope is 60 N and the coefficient of friction between the sledge and the snow is 0.2. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the normal contact force between the sledge and the snow. [2]\n(b) Find the acceleration of the sledge. [3]\n(c) Explain why pulling with the rope at an angle above the horizontal, rather than horizontally, can reduce the frictional force. [1]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Resolves vertically: R + 60 sin 25° = 12g\nA1 R = 92.2 N\n(b) M1 F = 0.2R (= 18.4 N)\nM1 Newton's second law horizontally: 60 cos 25° − F = 12a\nA1 a = 2.99 m s⁻² cao\n(c) E1 The vertical component of the tension reduces the normal contact force, and friction μR is therefore smaller\nAnswer: (a) 92.2 N (b) 2.99 m s⁻²` },
      { q: `A particle P moves along a straight line. At time t seconds, the displacement of P from a fixed point O is x m, where \\(x = 2t^3 - 15t^2 + 24t\\).\n\n(a) Find the values of t at which P is instantaneously at rest. [3]\n(b) Find the distance between the two positions at which P is instantaneously at rest. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Differentiates: v = 6t² − 30t + 24\nM1 Sets v = 0 and solves: 6(t − 1)(t − 4) = 0\nA1 t = 1 and t = 4\n(b) M1 Substitutes their t values into x\nA1 x(1) = 11, x(4) = −16\nA1 Distance = 27 m cao\nAnswer: (a) t = 1, t = 4 (b) 27 m` },
      { q: `A uniform shelf bracket is modelled as a uniform rod AB of length 2 m and mass 8 kg. The rod is freely hinged to a vertical wall at A and held horizontal by a light inextensible string attached to B and to the point C on the wall vertically above A, where AC = 1.5 m. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the tension in the string. [3]\n(b) Find the magnitude of the force exerted on the rod by the hinge. [3]`, marks: 6, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Moments about A: T sin θ × 2 = 8g × 1, where sin θ = 1.5/2.5 = 0.6\nB1 sin θ = 0.6 or cos θ = 0.8 used (BC = 2.5 m)\nA1 T = 65.3 N (196/3)\n(b) M1 Horizontal component H = T cos θ = 52.3 N\nM1 Vertical component V = 8g − T sin θ = 39.2 N\nA1 Magnitude √(H² + V²) = 65.3 N cao\nAnswer: (a) 65.3 N (b) 65.3 N` },
      { q: `A lift of mass 600 kg carries a box of mass 50 kg on its floor. The lift is raised by a vertical cable. Starting from rest, the lift accelerates upwards at 1.2 m s⁻² for 3 s, then moves at constant speed for 10 s, then decelerates uniformly at 0.8 m s⁻² until it comes to rest. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the tension in the cable while the lift is accelerating. [2]\n(b) Find the magnitude of the normal contact force on the box while the lift is decelerating. [2]\n(c) Find the total distance travelled by the lift. [3]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Whole system: T − 650g = 650 × 1.2\nA1 T = 7150 N\n(b) M1 Box alone: 50g − R = 50 × 0.8\nA1 R = 450 N\n(c) M1 Maximum speed = 1.2 × 3 = 3.6 m s⁻¹, deceleration time 3.6/0.8 = 4.5 s\nM1 Sums three distances: 5.4 + 36 + 8.1\nA1 Total = 49.5 m cao\nAnswer: (a) 7150 N (b) 450 N (c) 49.5 m` },
      { q: `A particle is projected from a point on the top of a vertical tower 25 m above horizontal ground. The particle is projected with speed 14 m s⁻¹ at 40° above the horizontal and moves freely under gravity. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the greatest height of the particle above the ground. [2]\n(b) Find the time taken for the particle to reach the ground. [3]\n(c) Find the direction of motion of the particle as it reaches the ground. [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 0 = (14 sin 40°)² − 2 × 9.8 × h\nA1 h = 4.13 m, greatest height 29.1 m above ground\n(b) M1 −25 = 14 sin 40° t − 4.9t²\nM1 Solves the quadratic correctly, choosing the positive root\nA1 t = 3.36 s (3.356…)\n(c) M1 Vertical velocity 14 sin 40° − 9.8t = −23.9 m s⁻¹ and horizontal 14 cos 40° = 10.7 m s⁻¹\nA1 65.8° below the horizontal cao\nAnswer: (a) 29.1 m (b) 3.36 s (c) 65.8° below the horizontal` },
      { q: `A non-uniform rod AB of length 3 m and mass 12 kg rests horizontally on two supports, one at A and one at B. The magnitude of the reaction at A is twice the magnitude of the reaction at B. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the distance of the centre of mass of the rod from A. [3]\n\nThe support at A is now moved to the point C on the rod, where AC = 0.5 m. The support at B is unchanged. A particle of mass 15 kg is placed on the rod at the point D, between A and C.\n\n(b) Find the distance AD for which the rod is on the point of tilting about C. [4]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 R_A + R_B = 12g with R_A = 2R_B, so R_B = 4g\nM1 Moments about A: 12g × x = 4g × 3\nA1 x = 1 m\n(b) B1 On the point of tilting about C, the reaction at B is zero\nM1 Moments about C: 15g(0.5 − d) = 12g(1 − 0.5)\nA1 Correct equation with their x\nA1 AD = 0.1 m cao\nAnswer: (a) 1 m (b) 0.1 m` },
      { q: `A drone moves in a horizontal plane. At time t seconds, its velocity is \\(((3t^2 - 4)\\mathbf{i} + 6t\\mathbf{j})\\) m s⁻¹, where i and j are perpendicular unit vectors. When t = 0, the drone is at the origin O.\n\n(a) Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the drone when t = 2. [2]\n(b) Find the position vector of the drone when t = 2. [3]\n(c) Find the exact value of t for which the drone is moving parallel to the vector (i + j). [2]`, marks: 7, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Differentiates: a = 6t i + 6 j\nA1 At t = 2, |12i + 6j| = 13.4 m s⁻² (6√5)\n(b) M1 Integrates: r = (t³ − 4t)i + 3t² j + c\nB1 c = 0 from r = 0 when t = 0\nA1 r = 12j cao\n(c) M1 Sets components equal: 3t² − 4 = 6t\nA1 t = 1 + √21/3 (reject negative root)\nAnswer: (a) 13.4 m s⁻² (b) 12j m (c) t = 1 + √21/3` },
      { q: `A particle A of mass 2 kg rests on a rough horizontal table. It is attached to one end of a light inextensible string which passes over a smooth pulley fixed at the edge of the table. A particle B of mass 3 kg hangs from the other end of the string, 1.2 m above the floor. The coefficient of friction between A and the table is 0.2. The system is released from rest with the string taut. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the acceleration of the particles and the tension in the string. [4]\n\nWhen B hits the floor it does not rebound. A continues to move and comes to rest before reaching the pulley.\n\n(b) Find the total distance moved by A. [4]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 For B: 3g − T = 3a\nM1 For A: T − 0.2 × 2g = 2a\nA1 a = 5.10 m s⁻² (5.096)\nA1 T = 14.1 N (14.112)\n(b) M1 Speed when B hits floor: v² = 2 × 5.096 × 1.2 (= 12.23)\nM1 Deceleration of A after string slackens: 0.2g = 1.96 m s⁻²\nA1 Further distance = 12.23 ÷ (2 × 1.96) = 3.12 m\nA1 Total distance = 1.2 + 3.12 = 4.32 m cao\nAnswer: (a) 5.10 m s⁻², 14.1 N (b) 4.32 m` },
      { q: `A parcel of mass 5 kg is placed on a rough ramp inclined at 30° to the horizontal. The coefficient of friction between the parcel and the ramp is 0.3. The parcel is held in equilibrium by a horizontal force of magnitude P N acting in the vertical plane containing a line of greatest slope. The parcel is modelled as a particle. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the least possible value of P. [4]\n(b) Find the greatest possible value of P. [4]`, marks: 8, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Resolves perpendicular to the ramp: R = 5g cos 30° + P sin 30°\nM1 Friction acts up the ramp when about to slide down: P cos 30° + μR = 5g sin 30°\nM1 Substitutes F = 0.3R and solves for P\nA1 P = 11.6 N\n(b) M1 Friction acts down the ramp when about to slide up\nM1 P cos 30° = 5g sin 30° + 0.3(5g cos 30° + P sin 30°)\nA1 Correct rearrangement: P(cos 30° − 0.3 sin 30°) = 5g(sin 30° + 0.3 cos 30°)\nA1 P = 52.0 N cao\nAnswer: (a) 11.6 N (b) 52.0 N` },
      { q: `A uniform ladder AB has length 6 m and mass 20 kg. The ladder rests with A on rough horizontal ground and B against a smooth vertical wall. The ladder makes an angle α with the ground, where tan α = 2. The coefficient of friction between the ladder and the ground is 0.4. A painter of mass 80 kg climbs up the ladder. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Find the greatest distance from A that the painter can climb before the ladder slips. [7]\n(b) The painter is modelled as a particle. State two other modelling assumptions made in this question and, for one of them, explain how it affects the answer to (a). [2]`, marks: 9, command: 'Find', markScheme: `(a) M1 Resolves vertically: R = 100g\nM1 Resolves horizontally: F = S, the reaction at the wall\nB1 Limiting friction F = 0.4 × 100g = 40g\nM1 Moments about A with all three terms: S × 6 sin α = 20g × 3 cos α + 80g × x cos α\nA1 Correct equation with tan α = 2: 12S = 60g + 80gx\nM1 Substitutes S = 40g and solves\nA1 x = 5.25 m cao\n(b) B1 Two assumptions, e.g. the ladder is a uniform rod / rigid; the wall is smooth; the ladder is in a vertical plane perpendicular to the wall\nE1 e.g. if the wall is in fact rough, friction at the wall helps support the ladder, so the painter could climb further\nAnswer: (a) 5.25 m` },
      { q: `A golf ball is struck from a point O on horizontal ground with speed 25 m s⁻¹ at an angle α above the horizontal, where tan α = 3/4. The ball is modelled as a particle moving freely under gravity. The horizontal and vertical displacements of the ball from O are x m and y m. Take g = 9.8 m s⁻².\n\n(a) Show that the equation of the path of the ball is y = 0.75x − 0.01225x². [4]\n\nThe ball lands on a raised green which is horizontal and 4 m above the level of O. The ball is descending when it lands.\n\n(b) Find the horizontal distance from O to the point where the ball lands. [3]\n(c) Find the speed of the ball as it lands. [2]\n(d) State one refinement that could be made to the model. [1]`, marks: 10, command: 'Show that', markScheme: `(a) B1 Components of initial velocity: 20 horizontally, 15 vertically\nM1 x = 20t and y = 15t − 4.9t²\nM1 Eliminates t: y = 15(x/20) − 4.9(x/20)²\nA1 y = 0.75x − 0.01225x² AG (all steps shown)\n(b) M1 Sets y = 4: 0.01225x² − 0.75x + 4 = 0\nM1 Solves the quadratic\nA1 x = 55.3 m, choosing the larger root since the ball is descending\n(c) M1 Vertical speed from v² = 15² − 2 × 9.8 × 4 (= 146.6) combined with 20\nA1 Speed = 23.4 m s⁻¹ cao\n(d) B1 e.g. include air resistance; include the spin of the ball; model the ball's size\nAnswer: (b) 55.3 m (c) 23.4 m s⁻¹` }
    ]
  }
};

